3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G (two blocks)\n- Multi-item modifier scope: cursed sword + plain amulet → 160 G base + 50 G cursed + (modifier adjustments) → 210 G; this is an integration example\n- Modifier thresholds: 2 years → loyalty; enchantment 5 → high-enchantment; sword enchantment 4 → no surcharge\n- Dragon material + enchantment 8, damage 1000 → payout 400 G (50% rule applies first then deductible)\n- Deductible per event: dragon damages sword (500) and amulet (300) → 600 G total (deductible applies once per item)\n- Standard reimbursement: regular sword, damage 500 → 400 G; rune damage 200 → 100 G\n- Enchantment threshold: dragon sword enchant 9 damage 1000 → 400 G; dragon sword enchant 5 damage 800 → 700 G; steel sword enchant 9 damage 1000 → 400 G\n- Multiple items of same type: two swords → insurance sum 2000, cap 4000; dragon damages both → each treated separately\n- Damages array more entries than policy covers → exit non-zero\n- Cap exhaustion: sword insurance sum 1000 G, cap 2000 G; two claims 1500 G → first 1400 G, cap remaining 600 G; second 600 G, cap remaining 0 G\n- Rounding: 197.5 → 198 (up); 350.5 → 350 (down)\n- Edge cases: empty item list → premium 5 G (only fee); unknown item type → exit non-zero; claim references item not in policy → exit non-zero; negative damage amount → exit non-zero\n\n**Integration examples:**\n- Newcomer cursed sword: customer 0 years, item cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3); premium 165 G\n- Long-standing customer's 2nd contract: customer 3 years, second quote, item cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7); premium 160 G (each item treated as first insurance)\n\nNow I need to think about how to structure the tests. The kata is complex, so I'll need to test:\n1. The quote function\n2. The claim function  \n3. The CLI\n\nThe test list needs to cover every example. Let me create a comprehensive test list.\n\nLooking at the spec, I'll create tests organized by:\n1. Item base premiums (per-item)\n2. Building block for components\n3. Premium modifiers (each in isolation then combined)\n4. Claim processing (deductible, special clauses)\n5. Cap exhaustion\n6. Edge cases / errors\n7. CLI\n\nI think I'll have separate spec files for different modules:\n- `quote.spec.ts` - quote logic\n- `claim.spec.ts` - claim logic\n- `cli.spec.ts` - CLI integration\n\nActually, let me start with a single comprehensive spec that covers all the rules in spec order. Looking again, I think it's cleaner to have separate files per domain:\n- `quote.spec.ts` \n- `claim.spec.ts`\n- `cli.spec.ts`\n\nLet me now think about what to test first. The simplest is computing the base premium for a single item. Let me organize the test list:\n\n**`quote.spec.ts`**:\n1. Empty item list → premium 5 G (only fee)\n2. Single sword (no modifiers) → 105 G\n3. Single amulet → 65 G\n4. Single staff → 85 G\n5. Single potion → 45 G\n6. Single rune → 30 G\n7. Two runes (no block) → 55 G (50 + 5 fee)\n8. Three runes (block applies) → 65 G (60 + 5 fee)\n9. Four runes (no block) → 105 G (100 + 5 fee)\n10. Seven runes (no block) → 180 G (175 + 5 fee)\n11. Two runes + one moonstone (no block) → 80 G (75 + 5 fee)\n12. Three runes + three moonstones (two blocks) → 125 G (120 + 5 fee)\n13. Cursed sword → 160 G (100 base + 50 curse + 5 fee + ... wait, 100 base + 50 curse + 5 fee + 10 first insurance = 165 G)\n   Actually wait, we need to be careful. The first insurance surcharge is 10% on the policy base premium.\n   Let me re-read.\n   \n   The \"first insurance surcharge\" is 10% on the policy base premium. For a single cursed sword, that's 100*0.10 = 10 G. So: 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 = 165 G. But there's no customer info given in many examples, so I assume no loyalty and the first insurance surcharge applies when this is the customer's first item. Let me think about this.\n\n   Looking at \"integration examples\", the first example has customer 0 years, no previous contract, item cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3). Premium: 165 G. Calculation: 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 = 165. So no loyalty (0 years < 2), no follow-up contract (no previous contract), first insurance surcharge applies (this is the customer's first insurance), processing fee.\n   \n   So the surcharge rules: loyalty (≥ 2 years, 20%), first insurance (10% on policy base), follow-up contract (15% discount on each contract after first). These are policy-wide.\n\nLet me look at the integration example more carefully:\n- customer: 3 years; this is customer's second quote\n- item: cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7)\n- premium: 160 G\n- (100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155 + 5 fee = 160 G)\n\nSo both first insurance surcharge AND follow-up contract discount apply. The first insurance surcharge applies because each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance.\n\nWait, that's interesting. So both 10% surcharge AND 15% discount apply. 100 + 50 + 30 = 180 (with item-specific modifiers). 180 * 0.1 = 18 first insurance. 180 * 0.15 = 27 follow-up. 180 - 27 - 20 (loyalty) = 133. Then 133 + 18 (first insurance 10%) = 151. Hmm, that doesn't match.\n\nLet me re-read: \"(100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155 + 5 fee = 160 G)\"\n\nSo 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155. OK so the first insurance is 10 G (10% of 100 base, not 180?), and follow-up is -15 G (15% of 100 base).\n\nHmm, let me look at the newcomer example: \"(100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G)\"\n\n100 + 50 + 10 = 160. So 10% of 100 = 10 (first insurance).\n\nOK so the percentage modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up) apply to the **base premium of the items covered** (per-item base premium). For a single sword, the item base is 100 G. So loyalty 20% of 100 = 20 G, first insurance 10% of 100 = 10 G, follow-up 15% of 100 = 15 G.\n\nWait, but the spec says: \"policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium (the sum of all item base premiums)\"\n\nFor a single sword, base premium = 100 G. So loyalty = 20% of 100 = 20, first insurance = 10% of 100 = 10, follow-up = 15% of 100 = 15. That matches: 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155 G before fee.\n\nFor the multi-item example: \"a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nSo 100 + 60 + 50 = 210 G. The policy base premium is 160 G. After all item-specific modifiers, before policy-wide modifiers and fee. So 210 G is base + item-specific modifiers.\n\nOK so my understanding is correct: policy base premium = sum of item base premiums, item-specific modifiers apply to affected item, then policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base premium.\n\nNow for tests, I need to construct scenarios that don't involve customer data so the tests are simpler. But many examples don't mention customer info. So I think we should set customer years to 0 and let the tests construct customer info as needed.\n\nActually, re-reading more carefully:\n- Loyalty discount: \"≥ 2 years\" - so if not stated, I assume 0 years (no loyalty)\n- Follow-up contract: \"after their first\" - so if first quote, no discount\n- First insurance surcharge: \"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge\" - \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance\" per the integration example, so this always applies.\n\nWait, but in the integration example for the newcomer, the calculation includes \"+ 10 G first insurance\". And for the long-standing customer second contract, it also includes \"+ 10 G first insurance\". So first insurance always applies per item, regardless of customer history.\n\nBut what if there are multiple items? Is the surcharge 10% of the policy base premium (sum of all base premiums) or 10% per item?\n\nLooking at: \"each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nHmm, that suggests per-item. But then the formula gives \"+ 10 G first insurance\" for a single sword (10% of 100 = 10). For a multi-item policy, it would be 10% per item.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\" \n\nSo a first insurance = a new insurance of an item. 10% surcharge. Each item is a first insurance. So the surcharge is 10% of each item's base premium.\n\nBut wait, \"policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium\". So first insurance is policy-wide and applies to policy base premium, not per item.\n\nHmm, this is contradictory. Let me look at the math more carefully.\n\nFor the long-standing customer second contract with a single cursed sword:\n- Base: 100\n- Curse (item): +50\n- High enchantment (item): +30\n- Subtotal with item modifiers: 180\n- Policy base premium: 100 (sum of all item base premiums, only one item here)\n- Loyalty (20% of policy base 100): -20\n- First insurance (10% of policy base 100): +10\n- Follow-up contract (15% of policy base 100): -15\n- Subtotal: 180 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155\n- Processing fee: +5\n- Total: 160 G ✓\n\nSo first insurance = 10% of policy base premium (which is 100 here).\n\nNow if a customer has a multi-item policy, first insurance would be 10% of policy base premium. So it's not per-item, it's on the policy base premium.\n\nBut the statement \"each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance\" means that even if the customer has been around a long time, each new item is treated as a first insurance (so the first insurance surcharge always applies). Got it.\n\nOK so for tests, I'll need to specify customer years in some cases. For tests where loyalty/follow-up don't apply, I'll set years to 0.\n\nActually wait, looking at the example:\n- \"(100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G)\" for a cursed sword (single item)\n\n100 + 50 + 10 = 160. 10 is the first insurance surcharge, which is 10% of 100. So for a single item, the first insurance surcharge is 10% of the item's base premium (or equivalently, 10% of policy base premium, which equals 100 here).\n\nOK so I'm convinced. Let me now design tests.\n\nActually, let me look at this test list carefully. For \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance\", maybe it means we should still apply first insurance surcharge per item? Let me check by computing for a multi-item scenario:\n\nIf a customer has a regular sword + regular amulet (no customer info), would it be:\n- Base: 100 + 60 = 160 (policy base premium)\n- First insurance surcharge: 10% of 160 = 16 G\n- Follow-up contract: no (first contract)\n- Loyalty: no (0 years)\n- Processing fee: +5\n- Total: 160 + 16 + 5 = 181 G\n\nOr, \"each item is a first insurance\" meaning each item gets a 10% surcharge on its base premium:\n- Sword: 100 + 10 = 110\n- Amulet: 60 + 6 = 66\n- Total: 110 + 66 + 5 = 181 G\n\nSame! So they're equivalent for single-item policies. For multi-item policies, the rule \"policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base premium\" is what determines the calculation. So first insurance = 10% of policy base premium (sum of item base premiums).\n\nOK now I'll proceed with this understanding. Let me also clarify: \"first insurance\" surcharge applies even if the customer is on a follow-up contract. So for the long-standing customer, both the first insurance surcharge (10% of policy base = 10) AND follow-up discount (15% of policy base = 15) apply. That matches the math.\n\nBut wait, in the long-standing customer case, the follow-up discount is on the second contract. So:\n- First insurance surcharge: per-item (10% of base) - applies to NEW items being insured for the first time\n- Follow-up discount: applies to each contract after the first one (so the whole contract gets 15% off)\n\nThe note says: \"each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history\"\n\nThis means even if it's the customer's 5th contract, each new item being insured is a first insurance for that item. So 10% surcharge per item applies regardless.\n\nBut the policy base premium is the sum of all item base premiums. So 10% of policy base premium = 10% of sum of all items' base premiums.\n\nBut \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance\" might mean: even if the same item type was insured before, insuring another one now is still a first insurance. This is consistent with the long-standing customer example where they had a previous quote, but each item in this new quote is a \"first insurance\" for that specific item.\n\nSo my interpretation:\n- First insurance surcharge: 10% of policy base premium (always applies per quote)\n- Follow-up contract: 15% of policy base premium (applies only if this is the customer's 2nd+ quote)\n- Loyalty: 20% of policy base premium (applies if years ≥ 2)\n\nFor the long-standing customer, both first insurance surcharge AND follow-up discount apply. Net: 10% - 15% = -5% of policy base. So for a single sword, that's -5 G. Then 100 base + 50 curse + 30 enchant + (180 - 20 + 10 - 15) wait I'm confusing myself.\n\nLet me redo:\n- Item base premium: 100 G\n- Item-specific modifiers:\n  - Cursed: +50 G (50% of 100)\n  - High enchantment (≥5): +30 G (30% of 100)\n- Subtotal with item modifiers: 180 G\n\nWait, but the high enchantment rule says \"≥ 5 enchantment → 30% surcharge\". The cursed rule says \"50% surcharge\". Are these on the item base premium or the running total?\n\nLooking at the multi-item example: \"cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nOK so 50% of the cursed item's base premium (100 * 0.5 = 50). So item-specific modifiers are based on the item's base premium.\n\nFor high enchantment: 30% surcharge. So for an item with base premium 100 and enchantment ≥ 5, +30 G surcharge.\n\nSo for the cursed sword with enchantment 7:\n- Base: 100\n- Curse: +50\n- High enchantment: +30\n- Subtotal: 180\n\nIf both modifiers apply, do they compound? Let me check: \"sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge applies; if cursed, both surcharges apply\"\n\nSo they both apply additively: 100 + 50 + 30 = 180.\n\nFor the new comer with cursed sword (no enchantment issue):\n- Base: 100\n- Curse: +50\n- Subtotal: 150\n\nThen policy-wide:\n- Policy base premium: 100\n- Loyalty (no, 0 years): 0\n- First insurance (10% of policy base 100): +10\n- Follow-up contract (no, first contract): 0\n- Subtotal: 150 + 10 = 160\n- Processing fee: +5\n- Total: 165 G ✓\n\nFor the long-standing customer with cursed sword (enchant 7):\n- Base: 100\n- Curse: +50\n- High enchantment: +30\n- Subtotal with item modifiers: 180\n\nThen policy-wide:\n- Policy base premium: 100\n- Loyalty (yes, 3 years): -20\n- First insurance (yes): +10\n- Follow-up contract (yes, 2nd quote): -15\n- Subtotal: 180 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155\n- Processing fee: +5\n- Total: 160 G ✓\n\nGreat, the math works out. Now let me design the tests.\n\nFor tests, I'll specify:\n- `customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 }` to avoid loyalty unless explicitly testing\n- 1st quote so no follow-up discount\n- First insurance surcharge always applies\n\nLet me now design the test list:\n\n**Test List for `quote.spec.ts`:**\n\nBase premiums (single items, no modifiers):\n1. Empty item list → premium 5 G\n2. Single sword → premium 105 G (100 base + 5 fee)\n3. Single amulet → premium 65 G\n4. Single staff → premium 85 G\n5. Single potion → premium 45 G\n\nComponents (single):\n6. Single rune → premium 30 G\n7. Single moonstone → premium 30 G\n\nBuilding blocks of components:\n8. Two runes → premium 55 G (50 + 5 fee)\n9. Three runes → premium 65 G (block, 60 + 5 fee)\n10. Four runes → premium 105 G (no block)\n11. Seven runes → premium 180 G (no block)\n12. Two runes + 1 moonstone → premium 80 G (no block)\n13. Three runes + 3 moonstones → premium 125 G (two blocks)\n\nCursed modifier:\n14. Cursed sword → premium 165 G (100 + 50 + 10 first insurance + 5 fee)\n\nHigh enchantment:\n15. Sword with enchantment 5 → premium 135 G (100 + 30 + 10 first insurance + 5 fee - wait, 10% of 100 is 10, so 100+30+10+5 = 145)\n\nWait, let me redo. For a sword with enchantment 5, the high-enchantment surcharge is 30% of the item's base premium = 30 G.\n- Item base: 100\n- High enchantment: +30\n- Subtotal: 130\n- Policy base premium: 100\n- First insurance (10%): +10\n- Subtotal: 140\n- Fee: +5\n- Total: 145 G\n\nBut the example doesn't give a value for \"sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge applies; if cursed, both surcharges apply\". It just says the rule applies.\n\nHmm, but the spec gives explicit values for some cases. For the integration examples, it gives the exact calculation. For the rules with \"sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge applies\", it just says the rule applies. So I need to compute the expected value myself.\n\nLet me reconsider the test list. I'll be selective and create tests based on what the spec explicitly shows vs. what I'd need to derive. Since the spec gives the exact values for the integration examples, those are the most important to test.\n\nLooking at all the explicit examples:\n\nQuote (premium):\n1. Empty items → 5 G (only fee)\n2. Single sword → 100 base + 10 first + 5 fee = 115 G (assuming 0 years customer, no follow-up). Wait actually, first insurance 10% applies, so 10 G. 100+10+5 = 115.\n3. Single amulet → 60+6+5 = 71 G\n4. Single staff → 80+8+5 = 93 G\n5. Single potion → 40+4+5 = 49 G\n6. Single rune → 25+2.5 → 27.5, rounded up to 28? Hmm but the spec doesn't say \"intermediate amounts\" must be rounded. Let me re-read.\n\nThe spec says: \"intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions; only the final premium or payout is rounded\"\n\nSo for a rune, base is 25 G. First insurance = 10% of 25 = 2.5. Subtotal: 25 + 2.5 = 27.5. Fee: +5. Total: 32.5. Rounded up (in MHPCO favor) to 33 G.\n\nBut the spec doesn't show this exact case. The closest is \"a premium calculation that yields 197.5 G → final premium 198 G (rounded up)\" - this is a generic rounding example.\n\nLet me look at the component block examples:\n- 2 runes → 50 G base premium\n- 3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\n- 4 runes → 100 G base premium\n- 7 runes → 175 G base premium\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium\n\nThese are base premiums, not final premiums. The final premium would include modifiers and fee.\n\nFor 3 runes (block):\n- Base premium: 60\n- Policy base premium: 60\n- First insurance: 10% of 60 = 6\n- Follow-up: no\n- Loyalty: depends on customer\n- Fee: +5\n- With 0 years customer, first quote: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71 G\n- With 0 years customer, first quote, but if rounded... 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. So 71 G.\n\nHmm wait, the spec doesn't explicitly state the final premium for these cases. Let me look at the integration examples for guidance.\n\nIntegration example 1 (newcomer cursed sword):\n- premium: 165 G\n- calculation: 100 base + 50 curse + 10 first insurance = 160 + 5 fee = 165 G\n\nIntegration example 2 (long-standing customer's 2nd contract):\n- premium: 160 G\n- calculation: 100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment - 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance - 15 follow-up = 155 + 5 fee = 160 G\n\nOK so the spec is detailed about these. Let me focus on those as primary test cases.\n\nBut the question is: what's the expected value for the component block examples? The spec says \"60 G base premium\". The total premium would be 60 + first insurance + loyalty + follow-up + fee. With 0 years customer, first quote: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71 G.\n\nOK so for tests, I need to choose customer info that makes the test simple. Let me always use:\n- yearsWithMHPCO: 0 (no loyalty)\n- first quote (no follow-up)\n\nSo first insurance surcharge always applies, loyalty and follow-up never apply unless explicitly tested.\n\nLet me revise my test list:\n\n**`quote.spec.ts`** (in order from simple to complex):\n\n1. Empty item list → premium 5 G (only fee)\n2. Single sword → premium 115 G (100 base + 10 first + 5 fee)\n3. Single amulet → premium 71 G\n4. Single staff → premium 93 G\n5. Single potion → premium 49 G\n\nComponents:\n6. Single rune → premium 33 G (25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 → 33)\n7. 2 runes (no block) → premium 55 G (50 + 5 = 55)\n8. 3 runes (block) → premium 71 G (60 + 6 + 5)\n9. 4 runes (no block) → premium 115 G (100 + 10 + 5)\n10. 7 runes (no block) → premium 198 G (175 + 17.5 + 5 = 197.5 → 198) ← this is the rounding example!\n11. 2 runes + 1 moonstone (no block, different types) → premium 80 G (75 + 7.5 + 5 = 87.5 → 88)\n12. 3 runes + 3 moonstones (two blocks) → premium 137 G (120 + 12 + 5 = 137) - wait, 120 + 12 + 5 = 137. Hmm let me recompute. 120 base + 12 first insurance + 5 fee = 137. Yes.\n\nActually wait. Let me check 11 more carefully: 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block, different types). So the base premiums: 2*25 = 50 for runes, 25 for moonstone. Total: 75. Policy base: 75. First insurance: 7.5. Fee: 5. Total: 87.5 → 88 G (rounded up).\n\nHmm wait, 2 runes + 1 moonstone = 2 runes at 25 each + 1 moonstone at 25 = 75 G. Not a block (different types). So yes, base 75, +first insurance 7.5, +fee 5 = 87.5 → 88.\n\nCursed modifier:\n13. Cursed sword → premium 165 G (integration example 1)\n\nHigh enchantment:\n14. Sword with enchantment 5 → premium 145 G (100 + 30 + 10 + 5)\n\nCursed + high enchantment:\n15. Sword with enchantment 7, cursed → 100 + 50 + 30 + 10 + 5 = 195 G\n\nWait, but the integration example 2 has the same scenario but with a 3-year customer and second quote, getting 160 G. Let me check my math: 100 + 50 + 30 = 180. With loyalty 3 years (20%): -20 → 160. With first insurance: +10 → 170. With follow-up: -15 → 155. With fee: +5 → 160. Yes.\n\nBut for a 0-year first-quote scenario with the same item: 100 + 50 + 30 = 180. No loyalty. First insurance: +10 → 190. No follow-up. Fee: +5 → 195 G.\n\nMulti-item policies (cursed surcharge scope):\n16. Cursed sword + plain amulet → premium 226 G\n   Base: 100 + 60 = 160\n   Curse (on cursed sword only): +50\n   Subtotal: 210\n   Policy base: 160\n   First insurance (10%): +16\n   Subtotal: 226\n   Fee: +5\n   Total: 231 G\n   But the spec says \"→ 210 G before further modifiers and fee\", so the modifiers are still to come. So 210 + 16 + 5 = 231 G.\n\nLoyalty:\n17. Customer with 2 years, single sword → premium 95 G (100 - 20 + 10 + 5 = 95)\n\nFollow-up:\n18. Multiple quotes from same customer, sword on 2nd quote → premium 100 G (100 - 15 + 10 + 5 = 100)\n\nLong-standing customer integration:\n19. Integration example 2: 3 years, 2nd quote, cursed sword (steel, enchant 7) → premium 160 G\n\nNow for claims. Let me also list the claim test cases:\n\n**`claim.spec.ts`**:\n\nStandard reimbursement:\n1. Sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 → payout 400 G (500 - 100 deductible)\n2. Rune (no enchantment/material), damage 200 → payout 100 G (200 - 100)\n\nPer-item deductible:\n3. Sword (damage 500) + amulet (damage 300) → payout 600 G (400 + 200)\n\nHigh enchantment reimbursement:\n4. Sword (steel, enchantment 9), damage 1000 → payout 400 G (50% then deductible)\n\nDragon material:\n5. Sword (dragon, enchantment 5), damage 800 → payout 700 G (full then deductible)\n6. Sword (dragon, enchantment 8), damage 1000 → payout 400 G (50% rule wins then deductible)\n7. Sword (dragon, enchantment 9), damage 1000 → payout 400 G (50% wins then deductible)\n\nWait, example 6 and 7 give the same result. Hmm. Looking at example 6: \"dragon-material sword, enchantment 8, damage 1000 → payout 400 G (high-enchantment clause applies, then deductible: 500 - 100)\". So enchantment 8 ≥ 8 triggers the 50% rule. The dragon material clause would say \"full reimbursement\" but since high-enchantment rule applies and is more restrictive, it wins. Then 50% of 1000 = 500, minus 100 deductible = 400.\n\nFor example 7: dragon sword enchantment 9 damage 1000. Both clauses apply. 50% wins. 500 - 100 = 400.\n\nSo examples 6 and 7 are equivalent. That's the spec's way of showing that the 50% rule applies whenever enchantment ≥ 8.\n\nMultiple items of same type:\n8. Two swords in policy, dragon damages both with two damage entries → 2 * (1000 - 100) = 1800 G payout. Each damage has its own deductible.\n\nCap exhaustion:\n9. Sword insured, two claims 1500 G each → first payout 1400 G (cap remaining 600), second payout 600 G (cap remaining 0)\n\nInsurance sum and cap:\n10. Two swords in policy → insurance sum 2000, cap 4000\n11. Sword + amulet policy → insurance sum 1600, cap 3200\n12. Sword + 3 runes policy → insurance sum 1750 (block affects premium not sum)\n\nEdge cases / errors:\n13. Quote with unknown item type → exit non-zero\n14. Claim references item not in policy → exit non-zero\n15. Negative damage amount → exit non-zero\n16. Claim damages array exceeds items in policy → exit non-zero\n\nOK now I need to think about the API. The kata says the implementation is a CLI. But the tests can test the underlying functions directly. Let me design the API:\n\n```typescript\n// quote.ts\nexport type Item = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Customer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n};\n\nexport type QuoteStep = {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n};\n\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[];\n  };\n};\n\nexport type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\nexport type Scenario = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type Result = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n\nexport function quote(items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number, quoteCount: number): number;\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: ...): { payout, remainingCap };\nexport function process(scenario: Scenario): { results: Result[] };\n```\n\nHmm, but TDD says I should drive the design from the tests. Let me think about what's the simplest API to test.\n\nI think I'll have:\n- `quote(items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number, totalQuotes: number): number` returns premium\n- `claim(items: Item[], damages: Damage[], remainingCap: number): { payout: number, remainingCap: number }` \n- A top-level `processScenario(scenario): { results: Result[] }`\n\nActually, let me simplify. Looking at the spec, the operation is:\n1. A scenario has customer info + steps\n2. Each step is a quote or claim\n3. Quotes create policies, claims operate on policies\n4. The policy tracks insurance sum, cap, items\n\nFor testing the quote logic in isolation, I need:\n- input: items + customer info\n- output: premium (just the number)\n\nFor testing the claim logic in isolation, I need:\n- input: items (the policy's items) + damage entries + remaining cap\n- output: payout + new remaining cap\n\nFor testing the scenario orchestration:\n- input: a full scenario\n- output: results array\n\nLet me also think about how the API should work for multi-quote scenarios. The follow-up contract discount applies based on how many quotes this customer has had. So I need to know which quote this is (1st, 2nd, etc.). I'll pass that as a parameter.\n\nActually for the follow-up contract: it says \"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\" So the 2nd contract is discounted. So for a 2nd quote, apply the discount. For a 1st quote, don't.\n\nFor the first insurance surcharge: it always applies (per item). So for every quote, the first insurance surcharge is added.\n\nOK now I have a clear plan. Let me proceed.\n\nActually, one more thing. The \"first insurance\" applies to each item in a quote. But the surcharge is 10% of policy base premium. So for a multi-item policy, the surcharge is 10% of the sum of all item base premiums.\n\nBut wait, \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance\". This might mean the surcharge applies per-item? Let me check.\n\nIf \"first insurance\" is 10% of policy base premium (sum of all item base premiums), then for a 2-sword policy: 200 base, 10% = 20 first insurance surcharge.\n\nIf \"first insurance\" is 10% per item, then each sword gets a 10% surcharge on its base premium. For a 2-sword policy: 100 * 0.1 + 100 * 0.1 = 20. Same result.\n\nSo they're equivalent for these cases. Good. I'll use the policy-base interpretation.\n\nBut wait, \"each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance\" might also mean that for a customer who had a sword before, insuring another sword now is also a \"first insurance\" for that new sword. But the calculation is the same: 10% of the policy base premium = sum of all base premiums.\n\nOK let me just proceed.\n\nHmm, but I'm noticing that the first insurance surcharge is for the assessment of a new item. So for a multi-item policy, each new item is a \"first insurance\". So the surcharge applies per item? Or just on the sum?\n\nThe note says: \"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo for a long-standing customer on a 2nd contract with a single sword:\n- 10% first insurance applies (per the note)\n- 15% follow-up contract discount applies\n\nFor 100 base: 10 - 15 = -5 G net. The math works.\n\nFor a multi-item policy, the surcharge applies to the policy base premium (sum of all base premiums). So for 2 swords (100 each, 200 total base): 10% first insurance = 20 G, 15% follow-up = -30 G. Net = -10 G.\n\nOK I think I have a consistent understanding. Let me proceed.\n\nBut wait, the spec says \"policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium (the sum of all item base premiums)\". So:\n- Loyalty: 20% of policy base\n- First insurance: 10% of policy base\n- Follow-up: -15% of policy base\n\nAnd item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the affected item's base premium.\n\nOK confirmed. Let me write the test list now.\n\nHmm, actually let me think about the claim tests more. For claim processing:\n\nThe claim takes a list of damages (each with itemType and amount) and operates on a policy (which has a list of items). For each damage:\n1. Verify the item is in the policy (else error)\n2. Verify amount ≥ 0 (else error)\n3. Look up the item's properties (material, enchantment)\n4. Compute the damage reimbursement:\n   - If enchantment ≥ 8: 50% of amount, then deductible\n   - If material == \"dragon\": full reimbursement, then deductible\n   - If both apply (enchantment ≥ 8 AND material == dragon): 50% wins\n   - Else: full, then deductible\n5. Subtract 100 G deductible\n6. Cap at remaining cap\n7. Sum across all damages\n\nFor multiple items of the same type in damages:\n- If 2 swords in policy, and damages has 2 sword entries → each is treated separately with its own deductible\n- If 1 sword in policy, damages has 2 sword entries → error (more entries than policy)\n\nWait, this is ambiguous. Let me re-read:\n\n\"if the `damages` array contains more entries of a given type than the policy actually covers (e.g. two sword damages but only one sword insured) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code; the whole claim is rejected\"\n\nOK so the count of damage entries of each type must be ≤ the count of items of that type in the policy. If exceeded, error.\n\nNow I think I understand. Let me now structure the test list.\n\nLooking at all this, I think the simplest API for testing would be:\n- `quote(items, customer, quoteIndex)`: returns premium\n- `claim(policyItems, damages, remainingCap)`: returns {payout, newRemainingCap}\n- `runScenario(scenario)`: returns {results} - orchestrates multiple steps\n\nBut for tests of individual rules, I can test quote and claim directly.\n\nActually, let me also think about whether I need to test the CLI itself. The spec says CLI reads JSON from stdin and writes JSON to stdout. For tests, I can either:\n1. Test the CLI by spawning a subprocess (complex)\n2. Test the underlying functions and have the CLI as a thin wrapper\n\nLet me go with option 2. The CLI tests can just verify that a simple invocation works.\n\nOK let me create the test list now. I'll structure it as multiple spec files.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The kata emphasizes testing the CLI behavior. But unit-testing the functions is simpler. Let me have:\n- `quote.spec.ts`: tests for the quote function\n- `claim.spec.ts`: tests for the claim function\n- `process-scenario.spec.ts`: tests for the full scenario processing (multi-step)\n- `cli.spec.ts`: tests for the CLI (reads stdin, writes stdout, handles errors)\n\nActually let me keep it simple with just two main spec files: `quote.spec.ts` and `claim.spec.ts`. The scenario processing and CLI can be tested implicitly through these.\n\nLet me think about this differently. I'll have:\n- `quote.ts` and `quote.spec.ts` - the quote logic\n- `claim.ts` and `claim.spec.ts` - the claim logic\n- `scenario.ts` and `scenario.spec.ts` - the orchestrator\n- `cli.ts` and `cli.spec.ts` - the CLI\n\nOr just:\n- `quote.ts` and `quote.spec.ts`\n- `claim.ts` and `claim.spec.ts`\n- `scenario.ts` (no spec) - orchestrator\n- `cli.ts` (no spec) - thin wrapper\n\nI'll go with the simpler approach. Let me now draft the tests.\n\nLet me now organize the test list:\n\n`quote.spec.ts` tests:\n1. Empty item list → 5 G (only fee)\n2. Single sword → 115 G\n3. Single amulet → 71 G\n4. Single staff → 93 G\n5. Single potion → 49 G\n6. Single rune → 33 G\n7. Two runes (no block) → 55 G\n8. Three runes (block) → 71 G\n9. Four runes (no block) → 115 G\n10. Seven runes (no block) → 198 G (rounding example)\n11. Two runes + 1 moonstone (different types) → 88 G\n12. Three runes + 3 moonstones (two blocks) → 137 G\n13. Cursed sword (integration example 1) → 165 G\n14. Sword with enchantment 5 (high enchantment) → 145 G\n15. Sword with enchantment 7, cursed → 195 G\n16. Cursed sword + plain amulet (multi-item modifier scope) → 231 G\n17. Customer 2 years + sword → 95 G (loyalty)\n18. Customer 2 years + sword (just at threshold) → 95 G\n19. Sword with enchantment 4 (no high enchantment) → 115 G (just base + first insurance + fee)\n20. Long-standing customer 2nd quote + cursed sword enchant 7 (integration example 2) → 160 G\n\n`claim.spec.ts` tests:\n1. Sword (steel, enchant 3), damage 500 → payout 400 G\n2. Rune damage 200 → payout 100 G\n3. Sword damage 500 + amulet damage 300 (per-item deductible) → payout 600 G\n4. Sword (steel, enchant 9) damage 1000 → payout 400 G (high enchantment clause)\n5. Sword (dragon, enchant 5) damage 800 → payout 700 G (dragon clause)\n6. Sword (dragon, enchant 8) damage 1000 → payout 400 G (high enchantment wins)\n7. Sword (dragon, enchant 9) damage 1000 → payout 400 G (both apply, 50% wins)\n8. Two swords + two damages (one per sword) → payout 1800 G (each has own deductible)\n9. Sword insured, two claims 1500 G each → first payout 1400 G (cap remaining 600), second payout 600 G (cap remaining 0)\n10. Quote with unknown item type → throws\n11. Claim with item not in policy → throws\n12. Claim with negative amount → throws\n13. Claim with more damages than items → throws\n\nThat's a lot of tests. Let me see if I can simplify or organize.\n\nActually, I realize I should also test the cap and remaining cap tracking. And the integration with multi-step scenarios (where a quote is followed by a claim).\n\nLet me think about the API again. For quote tests, I can have a function:\n```\nquote(items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): { premium: number, insuranceSum: number }\n```\n\nWhere quoteIndex is 0-based (0 = first quote). This way I can test follow-up contract logic.\n\nOr simpler: just pass whether it's a follow-up contract:\n```\nquote(items: Item[], customer: Customer, isFollowUp: boolean): { premium: number, insuranceSum: number }\n```\n\nI think `quoteIndex` is more flexible. Let me go with that.\n\nFor claim tests:\n```\nclaim(policyItems: Item[], damages: Damage[], remainingCap: number): { payout: number, remainingCap: number }\n```\n\nOr maybe the function should be:\n```\nclaim(insuranceSum: number, remainingCap: number, policyItems: Item[], damages: Damage[]): { payout: number, remainingCap: number }\n```\n\nHmm, the insurance sum is needed because the cap is 2x insurance sum.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the spec:\n- \"The total payout per policy is capped at twice the insurance sum.\"\n\nSo cap = 2 * insurance sum. The cap is fixed per policy and doesn't change. The \"remaining cap\" is the cap minus payouts to date.\n\nSo when a claim is made:\n1. Compute the desired payout\n2. Cap it at remainingCap\n3. Return payout + new remainingCap (remainingCap - payout)\n\nSo claim() needs to know the cap (or remainingCap) and the policy's items.\n\nLet me also think about the items tracking. The policy knows which items are covered. The claim's damages specify itemType + amount. For each damage:\n1. Find a matching item in the policy (with same itemType). If not enough items, error.\n2. Look up the item's properties (material, enchantment).\n3. Compute the reimbursement per the rules.\n\nSo the policy items need to be stored as a list (with duplicates for multiple of same type).\n\nOK let me also think about how to handle multiple items of same type in damages. The spec says \"each entry is treated as a separate damage with its own deductible\". So we process each damage entry independently.\n\nFor the \"more damages than policy items\" case: count damages by type, compare to count of policy items by type. If exceeded, error.\n\nOK let me now actually write the code. First, the test list.\n\nWait, I also realized I should think about the \"process\" or orchestration. The CLI reads a scenario, processes steps in order, and outputs results. For multi-step scenarios:\n- Quote creates a policy (stored in policies array, indexed by quote step index)\n- Claim references a policy by its quote step index\n\nSo we need a Policy type that stores: items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap.\n\nLet me code this up. I'll start with the test list now.\n\nActually let me think about whether the spec means the *policy* tracks insurance sum and cap, or just the items. Looking at the integration tests:\n- Two swords → insurance sum 2000, cap 4000\n- Sword + amulet → insurance sum 1600, cap 3200\n- Sword + 3 runes → insurance sum 1750 (block affects premium only, not sum)\n\nSo the policy needs to track insurance sum. And the cap is 2x the insurance sum.\n\nBut what about the \"policy index\"? In the spec, claims reference a quote step index. So when a quote is created, it's stored in an array. Claims reference policies by step index.\n\nOK now let me design the API:\n\n```typescript\n// types.ts\nexport type Item = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Customer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n};\n\nexport type QuoteStep = {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n};\n\nexport type Damage = {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n};\n\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Damage[];\n  };\n};\n\nexport type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\nexport type Scenario = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: Item[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type Result = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n```\n\n```typescript\n// quote.ts\nexport function quotePremium(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  isFollowUpContract: boolean\n): { premium: number, policyItems: Item[], insuranceSum: number };\n\nexport function computeInsuranceSum(items: Item[]): number;\n```\n\n```typescript\n// claim.ts\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  damages: Damage[]\n): { payout: number, remainingCap: number };\n```\n\n```typescript\n// scenario.ts\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: Result[] };\n```\n\nHmm, let me also think about how to compute the premium. Let me write out the algorithm:\n\n1. For each item, compute its \"adjusted base premium\":\n   - Main items (sword, amulet, staff, potion): use the standard base premium (100, 60, 80, 40)\n   - Components (rune, moonstone, etc.): 25 G each, with block of 3 alike at 60 G\n   - Apply item-specific modifiers (cursed: +50%, high enchantment: +30%)\n2. Sum all adjusted base premiums to get \"adjusted policy total\"\n3. Compute policy base premium (sum of unmodified item base premiums)\n4. Apply policy-wide modifiers to policy base premium:\n   - Loyalty (years ≥ 2): -20%\n   - First insurance (always): +10%\n   - Follow-up contract (not first quote): -15%\n5. Add the policy-wide modifier adjustment to the adjusted policy total\n6. Add 5 G processing fee\n7. Round up to whole G\n\nWait, this isn't quite right. Let me re-think.\n\nItem base premium: 100 G for sword.\nItem-specific modifiers: cursed (+50%), high enchantment (+30%). These are applied to the item's base premium.\nItem adjusted premium: 100 + 50 + 30 = 180 G (for cursed sword with enchantment 7).\n\nPolicy base premium: sum of all item base premiums (without item-specific modifiers).\nPolicy-wide modifiers: applied to policy base premium.\n  - Loyalty: -20% * policy base = -20\n  - First insurance: +10% * policy base = +10\n  - Follow-up: -15% * policy base = -15\nTotal policy adjustment: -20 + 10 - 15 = -25 G\n\nFinal premium = sum of item adjusted premiums + total policy adjustment + fee\n= 180 + (-25) + 5 = 160 G ✓\n\nOK so the formula is:\n- For each item, compute adjusted premium: base + (cursed ? base * 0.5 : 0) + (enchantment ≥ 5 ? base * 0.3 : 0)\n- Sum adjusted premiums to get \"items total\"\n- Compute policy base = sum of base premiums\n- Compute policy adjustment: loyalty * 0.2 - 0.1 + 0.15 (wait, first insurance is +10%, not -)\n  Actually: loyalty (if ≥2 years) = -20%, first insurance = +10%, follow-up (if not first) = -15%\n- Final premium = items total + policy adjustment * policy base + fee, rounded up\n\nWait, let me re-check. For the long-standing customer:\n- Items total (adjusted): 180\n- Policy base: 100\n- Policy adjustment: -0.20 (loyalty) + 0.10 (first insurance) - 0.15 (follow-up) = -0.25\n- Policy adjustment in G: 100 * (-0.25) = -25\n- Final: 180 + (-25) + 5 = 160 ✓\n\nFor the newcomer:\n- Items total (adjusted): 150 (100 + 50 curse, no enchantment)\n- Policy base: 100\n- Policy adjustment: 0 (no loyalty) + 0.10 (first insurance) - 0 (no follow-up) = 0.10\n- Policy adjustment in G: 100 * 0.10 = 10\n- Final: 150 + 10 + 5 = 165 ✓\n\nFor 7 runes:\n- 7 runes, no block (block requires exactly 3 alike)\n- Item base: 7 * 25 = 175 (all are runes, but only blocks of 3 count)\n- Wait, but the spec says \"4 runes → 100 G base premium\". So 4 runes = 100 G base = 4 * 25. No block applies. So blocks are only applied when there are EXACTLY 3 alike. Otherwise, all components are individually priced.\n- 7 runes: 7 * 25 = 175 G base ✓\n- Adjusted: same as base (no item-specific modifiers on runes)\n- Policy base: 175\n- First insurance: +10% = 17.5\n- Subtotal: 192.5\n- Fee: 5\n- Total: 197.5 → 198 G ✓ (rounding example)\n\nFor 3 runes + 3 moonstones:\n- Two blocks, each at 60 G base premium\n- Total base: 60 + 60 = 120 G ✓\n- Adjusted: 120 (no item-specific modifiers)\n- Policy base: 120\n- First insurance: +10% = 12\n- Subtotal: 132\n- Fee: 5\n- Total: 137 G\n\nOK my understanding is correct. Let me code this up.\n\nNow for the components and the block rule. Let me think about how to determine when a block applies:\n- A block is 3 alike components\n- \"alike\" means same type (e.g., 3 runes form a block, but 2 runes + 1 moonstone don't)\n- Block requires EXACTLY 3 alike; 4 runes doesn't form a block\n\nSo for each component type, compute floor(count / 3) blocks and count % 3 individual components.\n\nFor 7 runes: floor(7/3) = 2 blocks, 7 % 3 = 1 individual. So 2 blocks * 60 + 1 * 25 = 145 G.\nBut the spec says 7 runes → 175 G. So blocks are not applied here? Let me re-read.\n\nThe spec example: \"7 runes → 175 G base premium\". 175 = 7 * 25. So no block applies. Interesting.\n\nSo the block rule is: a block of 3 alike components is offered at 60 G. But you can't have multiple blocks of the same type? Or is it \"at least 3\"?\n\nLet me re-read: \"Components — for example runes and moonstones — are insured at 250 G each, with a base premium of 25 G per component. A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\"\n\n\"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\"\n\nSo if you have exactly 3 of the same type, you get a block at 60 G. Otherwise, individual pricing at 25 G each.\n\n\"2 runes → 50 G base premium\" - 2 * 25 = 50 ✓ (no block)\n\"3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\" - 60 ✓ (block)\n\"4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\" - 4 * 25 = 100 ✓ (no block)\n\"7 runes → 175 G base premium\" - 7 * 25 = 175 ✓ (no block)\n\nSo block requires EXACTLY 3, and only one block per type. Hmm but what if you have 6 runes? \"2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block: different types)\".\n\nWhat about 6 runes? The spec doesn't say. Following the pattern \"block requires exactly 3\", 6 runes = 6 * 25 = 150 G. So 2 blocks don't apply.\n\nBut that seems weird. Let me check 3 runes + 3 moonstones = 120 G. 60 + 60 = 120 G. So 3 runes form a block (60 G), 3 moonstones form a block (60 G). So per-type, exactly 3 = block.\n\nIf you have 6 runes, by the strict rule \"exactly 3 = block\", we'd have 6 runes but each individual is at 25 G. So 6 * 25 = 150 G. But intuitively, you'd think 6 runes = 2 blocks = 120 G.\n\nHmm. The spec is ambiguous here. Let me look for more clues.\n\n\"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\"\n\nThis sounds like ONE block of 3 components = 60 G. The spec doesn't explicitly say you can have multiple blocks. The 7 runes example suggests blocks are not stacked (7 runes → 175, not 60 + 60 + 25 = 145).\n\nBut 3 runes + 3 moonstones = 120 G suggests one block per type can be applied.\n\nOK let me assume: for each component type, if there are exactly 3 alike, apply the block (60 G instead of 75 G). Otherwise, all are at 25 G each.\n\nFor 6 runes: not exactly 3, so all at 25 G each = 150 G.\nFor 9 runes: not exactly 3, so all at 25 G each = 225 G.\n\nThis is the simplest interpretation. Let me go with it.\n\nActually wait, this means for 9 runes (exactly 3 + 3 + 3), you'd want to apply 3 blocks. But the spec doesn't say that. So I'll stick with my interpretation: exactly 3 alike = block, otherwise individual.\n\nActually, thinking about this more, the spec gives \"3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\" which strongly implies \"3 runes\" specifically (exactly 3). And \"4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\" explicitly says \"exactly 3\".\n\nSo the rule is: a block applies ONLY when there are exactly 3 of the same component type in the policy. Otherwise, each component is at 25 G.\n\nOK going with that.\n\nHmm but for 3 runes + 3 moonstones, both apply because each has exactly 3 of its type. So 60 + 60 = 120 G. ✓\n\nWhat about 4 runes + 2 moonstones? Each component type doesn't have exactly 3, so no blocks. 4 * 25 + 2 * 25 = 150 G.\n\nOK now let me also clarify \"alike\":\n\"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\"\n\"2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block: different types)\"\n\nSo \"alike\" means same type (rune with rune, moonstone with moonstone). Runes and moonstones are different types.\n\nOK let me also clarify insurance value:\n- Components are insured at 250 G each\n- So insurance value per component = 250 G\n\nFor 7 runes, insurance value = 7 * 250 = 1750 G.\nFor 3 runes (block), insurance value = 3 * 250 = 750 G. (Block doesn't affect insurance sum.)\n\nPer the cap example: \"a policy covers a sword and 3 runes (a block) → insurance sum 1750 G (= 1000 + 3×250); the block discount affects the premium only, not the insurance sum\"\n\nSo insurance sum is the sum of individual insurance values, regardless of blocks.\n\nOK now I'm confident. Let me write the test list and code.\n\nLet me first check the integration example for cap exhaustion:\n\"a sword is insured (insurance sum 1000 G, cap 2000 G); two successive claims of 1500 G each\n- first claim → payout 1400 G, cap remaining 600 G\n- second claim → payout 600 G, cap remaining 0 G (the desired 1400 G is reduced to the remaining cap)\"\n\nSo for a single sword (no enchantment, no dragon material, regular steel sword), damage 1500:\n- Full reimbursement: 1500 - 100 (deductible) = 1400 G\n- Cap remaining: 2000 - 1400 = 600 G\n\nFor the second claim of 1500:\n- Full reimbursement: 1500 - 100 = 1400 G\n- Capped at 600 G (remaining cap)\n- Payout: 600 G\n- Cap remaining: 0 G\n\nNow for the multi-item claim:\n\"a dragon attack damages an insured sword (500 G) and an insured amulet (300 G); payout = 600 G (the 100 G deductible applies once per damaged item)\"\n\nSo sword damage 500: 500 - 100 = 400\nAmulet damage 300: 300 - 100 = 200\nTotal: 600 G ✓\n\nAnd for multiple of same type:\n\"a policy covers two swords → insurance sum 2000 G (= 2×1000), cap 4000 G\na dragon attack damages both swords; `damages` contains two `{itemType: \"sword\", ...}` entries → each entry is treated as a separate damage with its own deductible\"\n\nSo if both damages are 1000 G each (sword damage = insurance value), then:\n- First damage: 1000 - 100 = 900 G (no special clauses on regular steel sword)\n- Second damage: 1000 - 100 = 900 G\n- Total: 1800 G\n\nBut the spec doesn't give an explicit value here. Let me just check the math.\n\nActually, the spec mentions this is for \"dragon attack damages both swords\", but it doesn't say they're dragon-material. The high-enchantment and dragon-material clauses apply to the item's properties, not the cause of damage. So for regular steel swords:\n- Each damage is processed separately\n- Each has 100 G deductible\n- No special clauses apply (regular steel, enchantment not specified)\n\nIf damage is 1000 G each, total payout = (1000-100) + (1000-100) = 1800 G.\n\nOK let me write the test list. I'll keep it focused and prioritized.\n\nOne more thing: for the test where a customer is on a \"follow-up contract\", the spec says \"this is the customer's second `quote` in the scenario\". So the second quote in the scenario is a follow-up. The first quote is not.\n\nSo in the scenario processor:\n- Track the count of quotes seen so far for this customer\n- The Nth quote is a follow-up if N > 1\n\nOK let me now finalize my test list and code.\n\nI'll have the following files:\n- `src/types.ts` - shared types\n- `src/quote.ts` - quote logic\n- `src/claim.ts` - claim logic\n- `src/scenario.ts` - scenario orchestrator\n- `src/cli.ts` - CLI entry point\n- `src/quote.spec.ts` - quote tests\n- `src/claim.spec.ts` - claim tests\n- `src/scenario.spec.ts` - scenario tests\n- `src/cli.spec.ts` - CLI tests\n\nLet me start with the test list.\n\nActually let me re-examine the integration example 2 one more time:\n\"customer: 3 years with MHPCO; this is the customer's second `quote` in the scenario\nitem: a cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7)\npremium: 160 G\n(100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155 + 5 fee = 160 G)\"\n\nSo:\n- Customer: 3 years, 2nd quote\n- Item: cursed sword, steel, enchantment 7\n- Premium: 160 G\n\nCalculation breakdown:\n- Base: 100\n- Curse: +50 (50% of 100)\n- High enchantment: +30 (30% of 100)\n- Loyalty (20% of policy base 100): -20\n- First insurance (10% of policy base 100): +10\n- Follow-up contract (15% of policy base 100): -15\n- Subtotal: 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155\n- Fee: +5\n- Total: 160 G\n\nOK now let me create the test list. Let me also think about how I'll structure each test.\n\nEach test will:\n1. Set up the input (items, customer, etc.)\n2. Call the function\n3. Assert the output\n\nFor tests that need multi-step scenarios, I'll use `processScenario`.\n\nLet me start writing.\n\nActually wait, let me also think about whether there are tests I should write that aren't directly in the spec. The spec mentions:\n- \"quote includes an item with an unknown type (e.g. `{type: \"broomstick\"}`) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\"\n\nSo unknown item type → error. Should this throw, or set process.exit? Let me have the function throw a typed error, and the CLI catches it and exits with non-zero.\n\nFor the spec, I think the cleanest API is:\n- `quote(items, customer, quoteIndex)` returns `{ premium, insuranceSum, items }` where `items` is the processed list (same as input but normalized)\n- `claim(policy, damages)` returns `{ payout, remainingCap }` where `policy` is `{ items, cap, remainingCap }`\n- `processScenario(scenario)` orchestrates these\n\nFor errors, the functions throw and the CLI catches.\n\nOK let me start. First, let me think about the test ordering.\n\nFrom simple to complex:\n1. Base premiums for single items\n2. Base premiums for multiple items of same type\n3. Component blocks\n4. Item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment)\n5. Policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up)\n6. Integration examples\n\nFor claims:\n1. Simple reimbursement (no special clauses)\n2. Per-item deductible\n3. Special clauses (high enchantment, dragon material)\n4. Multiple damages\n5. Cap exhaustion\n6. Errors\n\nLet me create the test files now.\n\nFirst, let me look at how many tests we'll have. Probably ~30-40 tests total.\n\nActually let me think about what I want the API to look like more carefully.\n\nFor quote:\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number  // 0 = first quote, 1 = second, etc.\n): { premium: number, insuranceSum: number };\n```\n\nThe caller passes quoteIndex to indicate whether this is a follow-up contract.\n\nBut wait, the quoteIndex is about the customer's number of quotes in this scenario. So the caller (scenario processor) tracks this.\n\nFor the standalone quote tests, I'll just pass 0 (first quote, not follow-up).\n\nFor claim:\n```typescript\nexport function claim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  damages: Damage[]\n): { payout: number, remainingCap: number };\n```\n\nThe caller passes the policy (which has items, cap, remainingCap). The function computes the payout and updates remainingCap.\n\nFor tests of the scenario orchestrator:\n```typescript\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: Result[] };\n```\n\nThis iterates through steps, maintaining policies, and returns results.\n\nFor the CLI:\n- Reads stdin (JSON scenario)\n- Calls processScenario\n- Writes stdout (JSON results)\n- On error, writes stderr and exits with non-zero\n\nOK now let me write the code.\n\nActually, I realize for the quote function, I should think about whether to track the customer's quoteIndex internally or have it passed in. Since a customer's quoteIndex is determined by their scenario history, I'll pass it in.\n\nBut this means tests have to pass quoteIndex. Let me make the API:\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number  // 0 = first quote\n): { premium: number, insuranceSum: number, policyItems: Item[] };\n```\n\nHmm, but actually, the spec says: \"policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium\". And \"first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance\".\n\nSo first insurance is always +10%, loyalty is -20% if years ≥ 2, follow-up is -15% if quoteIndex > 0.\n\nFor the spec examples that don't mention customer info, I'll assume yearsWithMHPCO=0 and quoteIndex=0.\n\nBut what about the \"long-standing customer\" integration example? It has quoteIndex=1 (2nd quote). And yearsWithMHPCO=3.\n\nOK my API should accept these as parameters. Let me proceed.\n\nLet me also think about how to compute the premium in code:\n\n```typescript\nfunction basePremiumForItem(item: Item): number {\n  switch (item.type) {\n    case \"sword\": return 100;\n    case \"amulet\": return 60;\n    case \"staff\": return 80;\n    case \"potion\": return 40;\n    default: return 0;  // component, handle separately\n  }\n}\n\nfunction insuranceValueForItem(item: Item): number {\n  switch (item.type) {\n    case \"sword\": return 1000;\n    case \"amulet\": return 600;\n    case \"staff\": return 800;\n    case \"potion\": return 400;\n    default: return 250;  // component\n  }\n}\n\nfunction computePolicyBase(items: Item[]): number {\n  // Group components by type\n  const componentsByType: Record<string, number> = {};\n  let base = 0;\n  \n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isMainItem(item.type)) {\n      base += basePremiumForItem(item);\n    } else {\n      componentsByType[item.type] = (componentsByType[item.type] || 0) + 1;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // For each component type, if exactly 3, apply block\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(componentsByType)) {\n    if (count === 3) {\n      base += 60;  // block\n    } else {\n      base += count * 25;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return base;\n}\n\nfunction computeInsuranceSum(items: Item[]): number {\n  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItem(item), 0);\n}\n\nfunction computeAdjustedPremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let adjusted = 0;\n  \n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isMainItem(item.type)) {\n      const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n      let premium = base;\n      if (item.cursed) premium += base * 0.5;\n      if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 5) premium += base * 0.3;\n      adjusted += premium;\n    }\n    // Components don't have item-specific modifiers\n  }\n  \n  // For component blocks, we already accounted for them in the base\n  // But item-specific modifiers don't apply to components\n  \n  // Hmm, this is getting confusing. Let me think differently.\n  \n  return adjusted;\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, the blocks affect the base premium, not the adjusted premium. So let me restructure:\n\n```typescript\nfunction quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number\n): { premium: number, insuranceSum: number } {\n  // Compute insurance sum\n  const insuranceSum = computeInsuranceSum(items);\n  \n  // Compute policy base premium (sum of item base premiums with blocks)\n  const policyBase = computePolicyBase(items);\n  \n  // Compute adjusted item premiums (item base + item-specific modifiers)\n  let adjustedTotal = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isMainItem(item.type)) {\n      const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n      let premium = base;\n      if (item.cursed) premium += base * 0.5;\n      if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 5) premium += base * 0.3;\n      adjustedTotal += premium;\n    }\n    // Components don't get item-specific modifiers\n  }\n  \n  // Wait, but the block affects the policy base premium, not the adjusted total.\n  // So for components, the adjusted premium = block price (60) or individual (25 per)\n  // But components have no item-specific modifiers, so adjusted = base for them.\n  // \n  // For the main items, adjusted = base + item modifiers.\n  // \n  // Sum:\n  // adjustedTotal = sum of main item adjusted + sum of component base\n  \n  // Policy-wide modifiers\n  let policyAdjustment = 0;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2) {\n    policyAdjustment -= 0.20 * policyBase;\n  }\n  policyAdjustment += 0.10 * policyBase;  // first insurance always\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) {\n    policyAdjustment -= 0.15 * policyBase;  // follow-up contract\n  }\n  \n  // Final premium\n  const total = adjustedTotal + policyAdjustment + 5;\n  const premium = Math.ceil(total);\n  \n  return { premium, insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I have a bug. For components with blocks, the adjustedTotal needs to account for the block price. Let me think again.\n\nFor 3 runes:\n- Item base = 60 G (block applies)\n- Item adjusted = 60 G (no item-specific modifiers on runes)\n- Policy base = 60 G\n- First insurance = 6 G\n- Subtotal = 66 G\n- Fee = 5 G\n- Total = 71 G ✓\n\nFor 4 runes:\n- Item base = 100 G (no block, individual pricing)\n- Item adjusted = 100 G\n- Policy base = 100 G\n- First insurance = 10 G\n- Subtotal = 110 G\n- Fee = 5 G\n- Total = 115 G ✓\n\nFor 2 runes:\n- Item base = 50 G\n- Item adjusted = 50 G\n- Policy base = 50 G\n- First insurance = 5 G\n- Subtotal = 55 G\n- Fee = 5 G\n- Total = 60 G\n\nBut wait, the spec says \"2 runes → 50 G base premium\". This is the base premium. So the final premium would be 50 + first insurance + fee. With 0 years customer, first quote: 50 + 5 + 5 = 60 G. OK.\n\nHmm, the spec doesn't give the final premium for 2 runes, only the base. Let me verify my approach.\n\nFor 3 runes + 3 moonstones (two blocks):\n- Items: 3 runes + 3 moonstones\n- Per type: 3 runes (block), 3 moonstones (block)\n- Item base = 60 + 60 = 120 G\n- Item adjusted = 120 G (no item-specific modifiers)\n- Policy base = 120 G\n- First insurance = 12 G\n- Subtotal = 132 G\n- Fee = 5 G\n- Total = 137 G\n\nFor 7 runes (no block):\n- Item base = 7 * 25 = 175 G\n- Item adjusted = 175 G\n- Policy base = 175 G\n- First insurance = 17.5 G\n- Subtotal = 192.5 G\n- Fee = 5 G\n- Total = 197.5 G → 198 G (rounded up) ✓\n\nOK my approach works. Let me code it up.\n\nNow for the claim:\n\n```typescript\nfunction claim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  damages: Damage[]\n): { payout: number, remainingCap: number } {\n  // Validate damages\n  // Count damages by type\n  const damageCount: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(\"Negative damage amount\");\n    }\n    damageCount[damage.itemType] = (damageCount[damage.itemType] || 0) + 1;\n  }\n  \n  // Count items in policy by type\n  const itemCount: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of policy.items) {\n    itemCount[item.type] = (itemCount[item.type] || 0) + 1;\n  }\n  \n  // Check if damages exceed items\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(damageCount)) {\n    if ((itemCount[type] || 0) < count) {\n      throw new Error(`More damages of type ${type} than items in policy`);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Process each damage\n  let totalPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    // Find the item\n    const item = findItem(policy.items, damage.itemType);\n    // Compute reimbursement\n    let reimbursement = damage.amount;\n    if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n      reimbursement *= 0.5;  // high enchantment clause\n    } else if (item.material === \"dragon\") {\n      // full reimbursement\n    }\n    reimbursement -= 100;  // deductible\n    if (reimbursement < 0) reimbursement = 0;  // ?  spec doesn't say\n    totalPayout += reimbursement;\n  }\n  \n  // Cap at remaining cap\n  const payout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  const remainingCap = policy.remainingCap - payout;\n  \n  return { payout, remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nWait, the spec says \"Dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 → payout 400 G (both clauses apply; the 50 % rule wins, then deductible: 500 − 100)\". So when both apply, 50% wins. My logic does that with if-else.\n\nBut what if enchantment is < 8 AND material is not dragon? Then full reimbursement. That's the default case.\n\nFor reimbursement < 0, I think the spec implies it should be 0 (can't have negative payout). Let me check: if damage is 50 and deductible is 100, then... well the spec doesn't have this case. Let me assume Math.max(0, reimbursement).\n\nActually, let me also handle the case where the policy items list doesn't contain the item. Wait, that's handled by the count check above.\n\nLet me also handle the case where itemType is unknown. The spec says \"claim references a damage entry whose item is not part of the policy (e.g. an amulet damaged when only a sword is insured, or an item with an unknown type) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\".\n\nSo unknown itemType in damages → error. My count check handles this: if itemType isn't in the policy, itemCount[type] is 0, which is less than count ≥ 1, so error.\n\nOK let me also think about edge cases:\n- What if remainingCap is already 0? Then payout = 0, remainingCap = 0.\n- What if totalPayout is 0? payout = 0, remainingCap unchanged.\n\nFor test 9 (cap exhaustion):\n- Sword insured, cap 2000\n- First claim 1500: reimbursement = 1500 - 100 = 1400, payout = min(1400, 2000) = 1400, remaining = 600\n- Second claim 1500: reimbursement = 1500 - 100 = 1400, payout = min(1400, 600) = 600, remaining = 0 ✓\n\nOK my logic is correct.\n\nNow let me think about how to organize the tests and what test cases I want to write.\n\nFor quote tests:\n1. empty → 5\n2. sword → 115\n3. amulet → 71\n4. staff → 93\n5. potion → 49\n6. rune → 33\n7. 2 runes → 60\n8. 3 runes → 71\n9. 4 runes → 115\n10. 7 runes → 198\n11. 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 88\n12. 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 137\n13. cursed sword (integration 1) → 165\n14. sword enchant 5 → 145\n15. sword enchant 4 → 115 (no high enchantment)\n16. cursed sword enchant 7 → 195 (with 0 years, 1st quote)\n17. cursed sword + plain amulet → 231\n18. 2 years + sword → 95\n19. exactly 2 years (boundary) → 95\n20. integration 2 (3 years, 2nd quote, cursed sword enchant 7) → 160\n21. unknown item type → throws\n\nFor claim tests:\n1. sword steel enchant 3 damage 500 → 400\n2. rune damage 200 → 100\n3. sword damage 500 + amulet damage 300 → 600\n4. sword steel enchant 9 damage 1000 → 400 (50% clause)\n5. sword dragon enchant 5 damage 800 → 700 (dragon clause)\n6. sword dragon enchant 8 damage 1000 → 400 (both, 50% wins)\n7. sword dragon enchant 9 damage 1000 → 400 (same as 6)\n8. 2 swords + 2 damages → 1800\n9. sword insured, 2 claims 1500 → first 1400/remaining 600, second 600/remaining 0\n10. claim with item not in policy → throws\n11. claim with negative amount → throws\n12. claim with more damages than items → throws\n\nFor scenario tests:\n1. Quote followed by claim\n2. Multiple quotes with follow-up contract\n\nFor CLI tests:\n1. Simple quote from stdin → stdout\n2. Multi-step scenario\n3. Error case\n\nOK let me now actually write this. First the test list.\n\nLet me actually trim down my test list a bit to focus on the most important cases. I'll keep all the spec examples but consolidate where possible.\n\nHmm, actually the more tests the better. Let me keep them all.\n\nLet me write the test list now.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-read the spec on insurance sum:\n\n\"a policy covers a sword and an amulet → insurance sum 1600 G (= 1000 + 600, the sum of the items' insurance values), cap 3200 G\"\n\"a policy covers two swords → insurance sum 2000 G (= 2×1000), cap 4000 G\"\n\"a policy covers a sword and 3 runes (a block) → insurance sum 1750 G (= 1000 + 3×250); the block discount affects the premium only, not the insurance sum\"\n\nOK so insurance sum = sum of individual item insurance values. Cap = 2 * insurance sum.\n\nLet me write the test list now.\n\nFor the test list format, I'll follow the SKILL.md guidance: create a single comprehensive test file with it.todo() entries, organized by feature area.\n\nI'll create:\n- `src/quote.spec.ts` with all quote tests\n- `src/claim.spec.ts` with all claim tests\n\nActually wait, the test list SKILL says one test file per feature. Let me follow that.\n\nOK let me now write the code. Start with test list.\n\nHmm, before I start, let me think about how I'll handle the \"first insurance\" surcharge. The spec says:\n\n\"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\"\n\nAnd: \"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a `quote` is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo first insurance applies always, per item. But the surcharge is 10% of the policy base premium (sum of all item base premiums), since it's a policy-wide modifier.\n\nFor a single sword: 10% of 100 = 10. ✓\nFor a multi-item policy: 10% of (sum of all base premiums) = 10% of policy base.\n\nOK confirmed. My understanding is correct.\n\nNow, one more clarification: in the integration example 2, the formula is:\n\"100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155 + 5 fee = 160 G\"\n\nNotice: 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155. So the policy-wide modifiers are summed and then added/subtracted from the item-adjusted total.\n\nItem-adjusted total: 100 + 50 + 30 = 180\nPolicy-wide: -20 (loyalty) + 10 (first insurance) - 15 (follow-up) = -25\nTotal before fee: 180 - 25 = 155\nFee: +5\nPremium: 160 ✓\n\nOK my understanding is correct.\n\nLet me now finalize the test list.\n\nWait, one more thing. The \"policy base premium\" - is this the sum of all item base premiums, or just the sum of main item base premiums?\n\nLooking at the integration example 2: 100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155.\n\nThe 100 base is the sword's base premium. The modifiers are 50 (curse), 30 (high enchantment), -20 (loyalty), +10 (first insurance), -15 (follow-up).\n\nIf policy base = 100 (just the sword), then:\n- 20% of 100 = 20 (loyalty)\n- 10% of 100 = 10 (first insurance)\n- 15% of 100 = 15 (follow-up)\nTotal policy modifiers: -25 G. Matches!\n\nWhat if there were components too? For a sword + 3 runes:\n- Sword base: 100\n- 3 runes block base: 60\n- Policy base: 160\n- Loyalty 20% of 160 = 32\n- First insurance 10% of 160 = 16\n- Follow-up 15% of 160 = 24\n- Total policy modifiers: -32 + 16 - 24 = -40\n\nSo yes, policy base = sum of all item base premiums (with blocks applied to components).\n\nOK confirmed.\n\nNow let me write the code. Actually, let me just write the test list and the implementation. I'll create the files now.\n\nWait, I should also think about what happens if there's no customer info or quote index. The spec mandates these in the input. But for unit tests, I'll just pass them as arguments to the function.\n\nOK let me now create the files.\n\nActually, I realize I should also have a way to handle \"unknown item type\". My current `basePremiumForItem` returns 0 for unknown types. But the spec says this should be an error. Let me throw an error instead.\n\nLet me adjust:\n```typescript\nfunction basePremiumForItem(item: Item): number {\n  switch (item.type) {\n    case \"sword\": return 100;\n    case \"amulet\": return 60;\n    case \"staff\": return 80;\n    case \"potion\": return 40;\n    default: throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOK let me now write everything.\n\nOne more clarification. The spec says:\n\"claim references a damage entry whose item is not part of the policy (e.g. an amulet damaged when only a sword is insured, or an item with an unknown type) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\"\n\nSo unknown item type in damages → error. This is different from unknown item type in quote items.\n\nFor quote: unknown item type in items → error\nFor claim: unknown item type in damages → error (handled by the count check, since the policy doesn't have it)\n\nWait, my count check handles this: if itemType isn't in policy, itemCount[type] = 0, count ≥ 1, so 0 < 1 → error. ✓\n\nOK let me now write everything.\n\nLet me start with the test list. I'll create the files in this order:\n1. Create test files (quote.spec.ts, claim.spec.ts)\n2. Create implementation files (types.ts, quote.ts, claim.ts, scenario.ts, cli.ts)\n3. Run tests, fix failures\n\nBut TDD says I should create the test list first with all tests as it.todo(), then iterate Red-Green-Refactor for each test.\n\nOK let me start.\n\nHmm actually one more thought. The kata says \"Source files implementing the logic and the `claim-office` CLI\" and \"Test files\" and \"CLI entry point at `src/cli.ts`\". So I need:\n- src/cli.ts (CLI entry point)\n- Other source files (free organization)\n- Test files (free organization, but must follow vitest config which expects src/**/*.spec.ts)\n\nI'll have:\n- src/types.ts (shared types)\n- src/items.ts (item base premiums, insurance values, etc.)\n- src/quote.ts (quote logic)\n- src/claim.ts (claim logic)\n- src/scenario.ts (orchestrator)\n- src/cli.ts (CLI entry point)\n- src/quote.spec.ts (quote tests)\n- src/claim.spec.ts (claim tests)\n\nActually let me keep it simpler. Let me have just one source file `src/claim-office.ts` and one or two test files. Or actually let me split by responsibility: types, quote, claim, scenario, cli.\n\nOK let me just start writing.\n\nFor the test list, I'll write it all out in one go, then iterate.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Looking at this exercise, I need to implement a CLI tool for an insurance office using TDD. Let me start by reading the skills and understanding the workflow.\n\nI've already read the skills. Let me now plan the test list and create the initial test file."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_75cd622e3c7d4f299a6269a9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking && pnpm test 2>&1 | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":15037,"output":19990,"cacheRead":15616,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":19855,"totalTokens":50643,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784942688490,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-4e76d89e-1835-4b62-847a-343cf57f2a36","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_75cd622e3c7d4f299a6269a9","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n> vitest run\n\nsh: 1: vitest: not found\n ELIFECYCLE  Test failed. See above for more details.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784943155005},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_40c528adff94405e9cb2156f","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking && ls node_modules/.bin/ | grep -i vit"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":183,"output":84,"cacheRead":50560,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":50827,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784943155007,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-aed8db56-a025-43e5-878a-2cbce3204b4c","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_40c528adff94405e9cb2156f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ls: cannot access 'node_modules/.bin/': No such