pes.ts` and `quote.ts`. That's potential duplication. Let me check:\n\nIn `types.ts`:\n```typescript\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\n```\n\nIn `quote.ts`:\n```typescript\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n```\n\nThese are DIFFERENT - one has only `premium`, the other has both `premium` and `insuranceSum`. So this is a duplication concern - we have two different types with the same name. But since they're in different modules and used differently (claim.ts likely uses the simpler one), this might be intentional.\n\nLet me check the claim.ts:","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ec1adaf8ad1748b5831539f1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.spec.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6bfc5ba07b954555b6e2e928","name":"find","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src","pattern":"claim.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":320,"output":543,"cacheRead":7680,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":395,"totalTokens":8543,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784943325628,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-a87bad72-97b9-4730-85e6-6c3929350bcb","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ec1adaf8ad1748b5831539f1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { claim, type Policy } from \"./claim.js\";\n\nfunction makePolicy(items: Policy[\"items\"], remainingCap: number): Policy {\n  // Insurance value per item type\n  const insuranceValues: Record<string, number> = {\n    sword: 1000,\n    amulet: 600,\n    staff: 800,\n    potion: 400,\n    rune: 250,\n    moonstone: 250,\n  };\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce(\n    (sum, item) => sum + (insuranceValues[item.type] ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n  return {\n    items,\n    insuranceSum,\n    cap: 2 * insuranceSum,\n    remainingCap,\n  };\n}\n\ndescribe(\"claim\", () => {\n  // Standard reimbursement (no special clauses, no dragon material, enchantment < 8)\n  it.todo(\"regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 -> payout 400 G\");\n\n  // Rune has no enchantment or material, so no special clause applies\n  it.todo(\"damage to rune (200 G) -> payout 100 G\");\n\n  // Deductible per damaged item\n  it.todo(\"sword damaged 500 + amulet damaged 300 -> payout 600 G (deductible per item)\");\n\n  // Enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement then deductible\n  it.todo(\"sword (steel, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (50% rule)\");\n  it.todo(\"sword (steel, enchantment 8) damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (boundary, 50% rule)\");\n\n  // Dragon material: full reimbursement then deductible (only if enchantment < 8)\n  it.todo(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 5), damage 800 -> payout 700 G (dragon clause only)\");\n  it.todo(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 7), damage 1000 -> payout 900 G (dragon clause only)\");\n\n  // Both clauses apply, 50% wins (then deductible)\n  it.todo(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 8), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (both, 50% wins)\");\n  it.todo(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (both, 50% wins)\");\n\n  // Multiple items of the same type, each damage has its own deductible\n  it.todo(\n    \"policy covers two swords, two damage entries (1000 G each) -> payout 1800 G (each separate)\",\n  );\n\n  // Cap exhaustion across multiple claims\n  it.todo(\n    \"sword insured (cap 2000), two successive claims of 1500 G -> first 1400 / remaining 600, second 600 / remaining 0\",\n  );\n\n  // Edge cases / errors\n  it.todo(\"damage references an item not in the policy -> throws\");\n  it.todo(\"negative damage amount -> throws\");\n  it.todo(\"more damage entries of a type than items in policy -> throws\");\n  it.todo(\"damage to rune (100 G) -> payout 0 G (full minus deductible, but cannot go negative)\");\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784943339090},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_6bfc5ba07b954555b6e2e928","toolName":"find","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No files found matching pattern"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784943339090},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's no claim.ts file yet. So the `QuoteResult` in `types.ts` is a placeholder for the claim future work. They are intentionally different - the `quote` function returns a richer result with insuranceSum, while `claim` may use a simpler one.\n\nOK so the duplication concern is moot. Let me focus on the actual refactor of quote.ts.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\nexport function quote(\n  _items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  return { premium: 5, insuranceSum: 0 };\n}\n```\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 2 (5, 0) = 2\n- Bindings: 5 (_items, _customer, _quoteIndex, premium, insuranceSum) = 5\n- Invocations: 0 = 0\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nWait, I should also consider type declarations. The type `QuoteResult` is a type alias which has 2 fields (bindings), and the export keyword is not really an executable thing.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The mass calculation should focus on runtime complexity. Type declarations don't really add runtime mass. Let me think about this more carefully:\n\nActually looking at the example in the system prompt:\n```\nfunction calculate(numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);\n}\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (number