tch-based using item.type?\n\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 0) {\n  return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n}\n// For now, only handle length === 1; future tests will expand this\nif (items.length === 1) {\n  return singleItemQuote(items[0]!);\n}\nreturn { premium: SWORD_PREMIUM, insuranceSum: SWORD_INSURANCE_SUM }; // default\n\nfunction singleItemQuote(item: Item): QuoteResult {\n  switch (item.type) {\n    case \"amulet\":\n      return AMULET_QUOTE;\n    default:\n      return SWORD_QUOTE;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis adds abstraction. Hmm. Maybe premature.\n\nLet me just stick with the simpler consolidation of premium/insurance pairs. That's a meaningful DRY improvement without over-engineering.\n\nActually, I realize there's a subtle issue. The original code uses `items[0]?.type` with optional chaining because items[0] could be undefined. With the consolidation refactor, we still use `items[0]?.type`. We should keep this for safety.\n\nOK let me proceed with the refactor:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Hardcoded quote for a single plain sword (100 base + 10 first insurance + 5 fee). */\nconst SWORD_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 };\n\n/** Hardcoded quote for a single plain amulet (60 base + 6 first insurance + 5 fee). */\nconst AMULET_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 };\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"amulet\") {\n    return AMULET_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, I'm second-guessing myself. Let me reconsider the structure.\n\nActually, I think there's an issue. The \"default\" case is conceptually \"single sword\" but it's labeled as the fallback for any non-amulet case. The naming `SWORD_QUOTE` reflects the assumption that the next single items added will be sword variants. But once staff/potion get added, this constant would need to be renamed.\n\nActually, looking at the test list, the next tests will be:\n- single staff -> premium 93 G\n- single potion -> premium 49 G\n- single rune -> premium 33 G\n- ...\n\nSo the current \"sword default\" pattern will need to expand. Maybe the constant should be more neutral. But for now, with 3 passing tests, naming it `SWORD_QUOTE` is fine.\n\nOK let me also consider: should I extract a helper function for the single-item case? Like:\n\n```typescript\nfunction quoteForSingleItem(item: Item): QuoteResult {\n  if (item.type === \"amulet\") return AMULET_QUOTE;\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nBut this is just two branches. Inlining is fine.\n\nLet me finalize my refactor. Actually, I want to think about one more thing: should I use a switch statement on item.type?\n\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 0) {\n  return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n}\nif (items.length === 1) {\n  switch (items[0]?.type) {\n    case \"amulet\": return AMULET_QUOTE;\n    default: return SWORD_QUOTE;\n  }\n}\nreturn SWORD_QUOTE; // multi-item case (not yet implemented)\n```\n\nHmm, but the multi-item case isn't really \"sword quote\" - it's just a placeholder. This is getting more complex.\n\nLet me just go with the simplest refactor: consolidate premium/insurance pairs into QuoteResult constants.\n\nLet me also think about whether to refactor anything else:\n\n1. The `_customer` and `_quoteIndex` parameters are prefixed with underscore to indicate they're unused. This is fine - they'll be used in future tests.\n\n2. The `QuoteResult` type is simple and clear. No refactor needed.\n\n3. The constant comments are explanatory. No need to change.\n\nLet me also consider Rule 3 (DRY): Is there any duplication?\n\nThe pattern:\n```typescript\nreturn { premium: X, insuranceSum: Y };\n```\n\nAppears 3 times. But each X, Y is different. The structure is similar but the values are different. Extracting a helper that takes premium and insurance sum would be over-engineering.\n\nThe other pattern: `SWORD_QUOTE` is used in two places (with different structure). Wait, no - in my refactored version, `SWORD_QUOTE` is only used once (the default return). Let me re-check.\n\nOriginal:\n```typescript\nreturn { premium: SWORD_PREMIUM, insuranceSum: SWORD_INSURANCE_SUM };  // 1 use of SWORD_PREMIUM, 1 of SWORD_INSURANCE_SUM\nreturn { premium: AMULET_PREMIUM, insuranceSum: AMULET_INSURANCE_SUM };  // 1 use of AMULET_PREMIUM, 1 of AMULET_INSURANCE_SUM\n```\n\nRefactored:\n```typescript\nreturn SWORD_QUOTE;  // 1 use\nreturn AMULET_QUOTE;  // 1 use\n```\n\nThis is cleaner. Less bindings, less duplication of structure.\n\nOK I'm going to do the refactor. Let me also keep in mind that there's a `singleItemQuote` helper opportunity, but I think it's premature for now.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the code one more time:\n\n```typescript\nconst SWORD_PREMIUM = 115;\nconst SWORD_INSURANCE_SUM = 1000;\nconst AMULET_PREMIUM = 71;\nconst AMULET_INSURANCE_SUM = 600;\n```\n\nThese 4 constants are paired. Consolidating them into 2 constants (`SWORD_QUOTE`, `AMULET_QUOTE`) reduces:\n- 4 constants → 2 constants (but each constant has 2 fields, so the count is similar)\n\nWait, in the original:\n- 4 binding declarations (SWORD_PREMIUM, SWORD_INSURANCE_SUM, AMULET_PREMIUM, AMULET_INSURANCE_SUM)\n- 4 literal constants (115, 1000, 71, 600)\n\nIn refactored:\n- 2 binding declarations (SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE)\n- 4 literal constants (115, 1000, 71, 600)\n\nBindings reduced by 2 (4 → 2). Constants stay same.\n\nPlus:\n- Original: 2 object literals for return statements (one for empty, one for amulet, one for default - 3 actually)\n- Refactored: 2 named object constants + 1 inline for empty = same number of object literals but 2 are named\n\nThe DRY improvement: the sword and amulet quotes are now defined once as named constants, rather than spread across multiple definitions.\n\nOK let me just do the refactor.\n\nActually, one more thought: should I also consolidate the `PROCESSING_FEE` into the empty quote pattern? Like:\n\n```typescript\nconst EMPTY_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n```\n\nBut the empty quote has `0` for insurance sum, which is conceptually different. Let me think about whether `EMPTY_QUOTE` is a meaningful name.\n\n\"Empty quote\" is meaningful: it's the quote for no items (just the processing fee). Yes, this could be a constant.\n\n```typescript\nconst EMPTY_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\nconst SWORD_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 };\nconst AMULET_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 };\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 0) return EMPTY_QUOTE;\nif (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"amulet\") return AMULET_QUOTE;\nreturn SWORD_QUOTE;\n```\n\nThis is even cleaner! Let me count mass for this:\n\nConstants:\n- 5 (PROCESSING_FEE value)\n- 115, 1000 (SWORD_QUOTE)\n- 71, 600 (AMULET_QUOTE)\n- 0 (in EMPTY_QUOTE)\n\nTotal constants: 6\n\nBindings:\n- PROCESSING_FEE, EMPTY_QUOTE, SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE\n- items, _customer, _quoteIndex\n\nTotal bindings: 7\n\nInvocations: \n- items.length (×2) = 4\n- items[0] (×1) = 2\n- items[0]?.type (×1) = 2\n\nTotal invocations: 8\n\nConditionals: 2 ifs = 8\n\nLoops: 0\nAssignments: 0\n\nTotal: 6 + 7 + 8 + 8 + 0 + 0 = 29\n\nHmm, that's worse than the previous refactor (28). Because we added EMPTY_QUOTE (one more binding) but the constants are the same.\n\nLet me revert to the previous refactor without EMPTY_QUOTE:\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst SWORD_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 };\nconst AMULET_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 };\n```\n\nBindings: 5 (items, _customer, _quoteIndex, PROCESSING_FEE, SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE) - wait that's 6.\nLet me count again: items, _customer, _quoteIndex = 3. PROCESSING_FEE, SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE = 3. Total = 6.\n\nConstants: 5 (PROCESSING_FEE = 5, 115, 1000, 71, 600) + 0 in empty case = 6.\n\nInvocations: 8 (same as before).\n\nConditionals: 8.\n\nTotal: 6 + 6 + 8 + 8 + 0 + 0 = 28.\n\nOK 28 is better than original 30.\n\nAlternatively, what if we don't bother with `PROCESSING_FEE` as a separate constant? Then we save a binding:\n\n```typescript\nconst SWORD_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 };\nconst AMULET_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 };\n```\n\nAnd inline 5:\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 0) {\n  return { premium: 5, insuranceSum: 0 };\n}\n```\n\nConstants: 6 (5, 115, 1000, 71, 600, 0)\nBindings: 5 (items, _customer, _quoteIndex, SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE)\nInvocations: 8\nConditionals: 8\n\nTotal: 6 + 5 + 8 + 8 + 0 + 0 = 27\n\nEven better! But we lose the named PROCESSING_FEE constant.\n\nHmm, the question is: is `PROCESSING_FEE = 5` a meaningful named constant or premature naming?\n\nLooking at the test:\n```typescript\nit(\"empty item list -> premium 5 G (only processing fee)\", () => {\n  expect(quote([], customer, 0)).toEqual({ premium: 5, insuranceSum: 0 });\n});\n```\n\nThe test description explicitly mentions \"processing fee\" as the meaning of the value 5. This is meaningful domain terminology. Keeping `PROCESSING_FEE` adds clarity.\n\nBut from a pure mass perspective, inlining saves mass. Rule 2 (clarity) trumps APP, so let's keep PROCESSING_FEE.\n\nActually wait, I want to think about this more carefully. The principle is \"rule 2 trumps APP\", but that means clarity is more important than low mass. If inlining 5 reduces mass but loses clarity, prefer the named constant. Yes, that's the right call.\n\nBut this is also about future flexibility. When we implement the actual processing fee logic (not hardcoded), it'll be a function call or something. Keeping PROCESSING_FEE as a separate constant makes that transition easier.\n\nOK final plan: keep PROCESSING_FEE, consolidate SWORD_* and AMULET_* into SWORD_QUOTE and AMULET_QUOTE.\n\nLet me also re-examine: should I use a Map or dictionary instead? Like:\n\n```typescript\nconst SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES: Partial<Record<Item[\"type\"], QuoteResult>> = {\n  amulet: AMULET_QUOTE,\n  // sword will be added as default\n};\n```\n\nBut this doesn't help much with only 2 items. Premature.\n\nActually, I think a switch statement could be slightly more idiomatic and prepares for future expansion:\n\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    switch (items[0]?.type) {\n      case \"amulet\":\n        return AMULET_QUOTE;\n      default:\n        return SWORD_QUOTE;\n    }\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE; // multi-item placeholder\n}\n```\n\nThis adds a switch (conditional with mass = 4), but allows for easier expansion.\n\nHmm, but the switch is essentially equivalent to the if/else structure. Mass-wise:\n\nOriginal refactored:\n- 2 conditionals = 8\n- 6 bindings = 6\n- 8 invocations = 8\n- 6 constants = 6\n- Total: 28\n\nSwitch-based:\n- 1 if + 1 switch = 8\n- Same bindings/invocations/constants\n- Total: 28\n\nSame mass. But the switch is more \"future-proof\" for adding more item types.\n\nHowever, the current code uses if/else if/else structure implicitly. The refactor should not introduce new abstractions that aren't justified by current tests. Let me stick with the simpler if/else approach.\n\nHmm wait, let me re-examine. The current code is:\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 0) return ...;\nif (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"amulet\") return ...;\nreturn ...;\n```\n\nIt's not a true if/else chain. Each condition is independent. The structure reads as:\n1. If empty, return empty quote\n2. If single amulet, return amulet quote\n3. Otherwise, return default (sword) quote\n\nThis is clear and works for current tests. Adding more types will require either:\n- More `if (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"X\")` chains (gets ugly fast)\n- A switch on items[0]?.type (cleaner for many types)\n\nThe switch would be the natural refactor when more types are added. For now, the current structure works.\n\nOK let me finalize: my refactor will:\n1. Consolidate `SWORD_PREMIUM` + `SWORD_INSURANCE_SUM` → `SWORD_QUOTE`\n2. Consolidate `AMULET_PREMIUM` + `AMULET_INSURANCE_SUM` → `AMULET_QUOTE`\n3. Update comments accordingly\n\nLet me also double-check: in the refactored version, the JSDoc comment for SWORD_QUOTE says \"(100 base + 10 first insurance + 5 fee)\". This is a hint about the future calculation. It's still meaningful.\n\nHmm wait, I want to also check: is there any test helper or test fixture we should add? No, the tests are clean.\n\nLet me also think about the test file:\n```typescript\nit(\"empty item list -> premium 5 G (only processing fee)\", () => {\n  const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n  expect(quote([], customer, 0)).toEqual({ premium: 5, insuranceSum: 0 });\n});\n```\n\nThere's a `const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 }` repeated in each test. That's some duplication in the tests, but tests are not the focus of this refactor.\n\nOK let me run the tests first to make sure they pass, then do the refactor.\n\nActually, before I do anything, let me run the tests to verify the current state.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll examine the current implementation and refactor it while keeping all tests green."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_587e3a8457a1471f9bc6808c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions"