AMULET_QUOTE,\n  staff: STAFF_QUOTE,\n};\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const type = items[0]?.type ?? \"\";\n    return SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES[type] ?? SWORD_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Consolidates the magic into a lookup table\n- Reduces conditional count from 3 to 2\n- Adds one invocation (the lookup with `??`) - but that's basically nil\n- Removes the duplicated `items.length === 1` checks\n- More extensible: just add a new entry to the table when new types are added\n\nMass calculation:\n- Constants: PROCESSING_FEE (1), 3 QuoteResult literals (6), 3 keys \"sword\"/\"amulet\"/\"staff\" (3) = 10\n  Wait, the constants in SWORD_QUOTE etc. don't change. And we're adding the keys for the lookup table.\n  - PROCESSING_FEE = 5 → 1 constant\n  - SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE, STAFF_QUOTE (each with 2 literals) → 6 constants  \n  - Lookup table keys: \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\" → 3 constants\n  - \"\" empty string fallback → 1 constant\n  - Total constants: 1 + 6 + 3 + 1 = 11\n- Bindings: PROCESSING_FEE, SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE, STAFF_QUOTE, SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES, quote function, items, _customer, _quoteIndex, type = 10\n- Invocations: `items[0]?.type ?? \"\"` (no invocations) - `SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES[type]` (no invocation, just lookup) - `??` (no invocation). Maybe count `items[0]?.type` as a binding access. The `?? SWORD_QUOTE` doesn't invoke. So 0 invocations.\n- Conditionals: 2 (items.length === 0, items.length === 1) = 8\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0\n\nTotal mass: 11 + 10 + 0 + 8 + 0 + 0 = 29\n\nHmm, mass is similar. But the code is cleaner and more DRY.\n\nActually let me think about this differently. The current code has 3 if statements. If I use a lookup table, I have 2 if statements. So I save 1 conditional (mass 4). I add some lookup table constants (mass ~4-5 for the keys).\n\nNet change: about even or slightly less.\n\nThe bigger win is the DRY improvement and the extensibility. When we add more item types, we just add a row to the table.\n\nLet me also consider: should I just keep the current structure but extract the magic strings as constants? That might be a smaller change.\n\nActually, looking at this carefully, the current code is already pretty clean for what it needs to do. The \"duplication\" of `items.length === 1` is minor - it's the same condition evaluated twice, which is cheap and clear. Each branch handles a different type, so they're not really duplicating logic.\n\nLet me think about what's actually a meaningful refactoring here.\n\n**Option A: Keep as-is, just minor cleanup**\nRemove the comment duplication, maybe.\n\n**Option B: Extract a lookup table for single-item quotes**\n- More extensible\n- Slight reduction in mass\n- Cleaner for future item types\n\n**Option C: Extract the single-item branch into a helper function**\n```typescript\nfunction quoteSingleItem(item: Item | undefined): QuoteResult {\n  if (item?.type === \"amulet\") return AMULET_QUOTE;\n  if (item?.type === \"staff\") return STAFF_QUOTE;\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n\nexport function quote(...): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  return quoteSingleItem(items[0]);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this adds a function (binding) and an invocation but reduces conditionals.\n\nI think Option B (lookup table) is the cleanest. It eliminates the repeated `items.length === 1` check and provides a single place to add new item types.\n\nLet me also consider naming. The function `quote` is fine. The constants are well-named. The lookup table name... `SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES` is clear. Or `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES` since they're for unmodifed items.\n\nWait, the test data:\n- single sword → premium 115\n- single amulet → premium 71\n- single staff → premium 93\n\nThese are PLAIN items (no enchantment, no curse). So `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES` is more descriptive.\n\nBut maybe it's better to keep it neutral since future tests might involve non-plain items and we may add more sophisticated logic. Let me think...\n\nActually, the spec implies that as we add more tests, the implementation will need to handle modifiers (cursed, enchantment), discount (loyalty), etc. So `SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES` might be too narrow.\n\nA better name might be `BASE_QUOTES` since these represent the base quotes for each type. Then later we can layer modifiers on top.\n\nOr just `ITEM_QUOTES` since for now they ARE the quotes per type.\n\nLet me go with `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES` since the current values are all for plain items, and the table will need to be restructured when modifiers come in anyway. Actually no - let me not over-think the naming. The current naming is fine.\n\nLet me settle on `SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES` as it most accurately describes what the table represents: the quote for a single item of each type.\n\nActually, I realize I should think about this more carefully. The current code has hardcoded values that will need to be REPLACED as we add more tests. Once we add the loyalty discount test, for example, the hardcoded `premium: 115` won't work - we need to compute it.\n\nSo this refactoring should focus on the IMMEDIATE state: 4 passing tests, 17 todo tests. The implementation is hardcoded because we haven't built the real calculation logic yet.\n\nIn that context, the refactoring should:\n1. Clean up the if/return chain\n2. Make it easy to add more item types\n3. NOT add premature complexity for modifiers we haven't tested yet\n\nI think the lookup table approach is right. Let me code it up.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider one more time. The current structure:\n\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 0) { return ...; }\nif (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"amulet\") { return AMULET_QUOTE; }\nif (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"staff\") { return STAFF_QUOTE; }\nreturn SWORD_QUOTE;\n```\n\nvs.\n\n```typescript\nconst SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n};\n\nexport function quote(...): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) { return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 }; }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const type = items[0]?.type;\n    return SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES[type ?? \"\"] ?? SWORD_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this removes the separate AMULET_QUOTE / STAFF_QUOTE / SWORD_QUOTE constants and inlines them into the table. That actually eliminates the naming overhead. Let me think...\n\nThe current names like `AMULET_QUOTE` are nice because they read like English: \"return the amulet quote\". But once they're inside a table, they're just entries. The table name `SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES.amulet` still reads okay.\n\nOK let me just do this refactoring. Final plan:\n\n1. Replace `SWORD_QUOTE`, `AMULET_QUOTE`, `STAFF_QUOTE` with a `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES` lookup table\n2. Simplify the `quote` function to use the table\n3. Keep `PROCESSING_FEE` for the empty case\n4. Keep all tests green\n\nLet me write the refactored code:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base quotes for a single plain item of each type (base premium + first insurance + fee). */\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n};\n\n/** Fallback for a single item whose type has no entry yet (currently defaults to sword). */\nconst DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE: QuoteResult = PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword!;\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const type = items[0]?.type;\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[type ?? \"\"] ?? DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nWait, this might be over-engineering. Let me try a simpler approach.\n\nActually, the simplest improvement is just to consolidate the duplicated `items.length === 1` check. That's the actual duplication the prompt is hinting at.\n\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const itemType = items[0]?.type;\n    if (itemType === \"amulet\") return AMULET_QUOTE;\n    if (itemType === \"staff\") return STAFF_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Eliminates the `items.length === 1` duplication\n- Uses a more descriptive local binding `itemType` instead of inline `items[0]?.type`\n- Slightly more conditionals but clearer structure\n- No new constants or abstractions\n\nTrade-off:\n- Mass: 3 conditionals → 4 conditionals (mass +4)\n- Plus we add 1 binding (`itemType`)\n- But we eliminate the `items[0]?.type` repeated twice\n\nActually wait, when we wrote `items[0]?.type === \"amulet\"` we had inline property access. Now we extract to `itemType`. The mass for `itemType` is 1 (binding). The original `items[0]?.type` is 0 mass (property access doesn't count). So:\n\nNet change: +4 mass (1 conditional more) + 1 binding = +5 mass.\n\nHmm, that goes the wrong way. Let me think about what truly improves this.\n\nLooking at it again, the current code has:\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"amulet\") {\n  return AMULET_QUOTE;\n}\nif (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"staff\") {\n  return STAFF_QUOTE;\n}\nreturn SWORD_QUOTE;\n```\n\nThe duplication is `items.length === 1` appearing twice. The natural cleanup is to nest:\n```typescript\nif (items.length === 1) {\n  const itemType = items[0]?.type;\n  if (itemType === \"amulet\") return AMULET_QUOTE;\n  if (itemType === \"staff\") return STAFF_QUOTE;\n}\nreturn SWORD_QUOTE;\n```\n\nBut this has more mass. APP says lower is better, but Rule 2 (clarity) trumps APP. And Rule 3 (DRY) trumps Rule 4 (fewest elements).\n\nSo DRY-wise, this is a win. APP-wise, it's a loss. The question is: is the DRY win worth it?\n\nI think yes, because:\n1. It removes duplication (Rule 3)\n2. It's slightly clearer (Rule 2)\n3. When we add more types later, we just add another `if` line in the same block, not another `if (... length === 1 ...)` chain\n\nAlternatively, the lookup table approach has even less duplication and is more extensible:\n\n```typescript\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: SWORD_QUOTE,\n  amulet: AMULET_QUOTE,\n  staff: STAFF_QUOTE,\n};\n\nexport function quote(...): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"] ?? SWORD_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Eliminates the duplicate `items.length === 1` (saves 1 conditional, mass -4)\n- Adds lookup table entries (3 string keys = 3 constants, mass +3)\n- Adds 1 invocation (`PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[...]`, mass +2)... actually, property/index access might not count as invocation. Let me not count it.\n- No new bindings (the table is the binding)\n\nNet change: -4 conditionals + 3 constants = -1 mass. Marginal improvement.\n\nBut also we keep the named constants SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE, STAFF_QUOTE for documentation purposes. The table is a secondary structure that maps type → quote.\n\nHmm, that's a bit redundant though - the constants are only