1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n};\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const quoteForType = PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"];\n    if (quoteForType) return quoteForType;\n  }\n  return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword!;\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Eliminates the 3 separate hardcoded quote constants\n- Eliminates the duplicate `items.length === 1` check\n- Adds a single lookup table that's easy to extend\n- Reduces conditionals from 3 to 2 (saved 4 mass)\n- Adds 3 constant strings (table keys) - actually they're keys in an object literal, not free-standing constants, but they are string literals\n- The fallback to `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword!` is a bit ugly with the `!` but it's clear\n\nWait, do I need the `!`? TypeScript's `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is probably on. Let me check the tsconfig.\n\nLet me think about mass more carefully:\n\nBefore:\n- 1 constant literal (PROCESSING_FEE = 5)\n- 6 constant literals (in SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE, STAFF_QUOTE)\n- 4 bindings (3 const declarations + 1 function)\n- 3 bindings (parameters)\n- 3 conditionals\n- 0 invocations\n- 0 loops\n- 0 assignments\n\nTotal = 1 + 6 + 4 + 3 + 12 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 26\n\nAfter (with lookup table approach):\n- 1 constant (PROCESSING_FEE = 5)\n- 6 constants (in PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES object: 115, 1000, 71, 600, 93, 800)\n- 3 constants (keys: \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\")\n- 2 bindings (PROCESSING_FEE, PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES)\n- 1 binding (function quote)\n- 3 bindings (parameters)\n- 1 binding (quoteForType inside function)\n- 2 conditionals (items.length === 0, items.length === 1)\n- 1 conditional (if quoteForType)\n- 0 invocations\n- 0 loops\n- 0 assignments\n\nTotal = 1 + 6 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 3 + 1 + 8 + 4 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 29\n\nHmm, that's MORE mass. The new code is actually heavier by APP count.\n\nBut it has:\n- Less duplication (Rule 3 win)\n- Same clarity or slightly better (Rule 2 neutral/slight win)\n- Fewer conditionals (but more constants)\n\nI think Rule 3 trumps APP here. The duplication of `items.length === 1` is a real code smell, and the lookup table makes the intent clearer (\"look up the quote for this type\").\n\nOK let me think differently. The current code structure is:\n```\nif (empty) return empty quote\nif (1 + amulet) return amulet quote\nif (1 + staff) return staff quote\nreturn sword quote  (fallback for \"everything else is sword\")\n```\n\nThe \"fallback to sword\" pattern is actually the bug/issue. It implies sword is the default for unknown types AND for multi-item lists. This is implicit and unclear.\n\nA cleaner pattern:\n```\nif (empty) return empty quote\nif (1 item) {\n  look up quote for item type\n  return it (with fallback)\n}\nreturn \"default for multi-item\"  (TODO: implement)\n```\n\nThe fallback for multi-item is currently SWORD_QUOTE, which is technically wrong (a multi-item policy shouldn't have the same quote as a single sword). But that's a future concern.\n\nLet me look at it from a different angle. What's the BIGGEST refactoring improvement I can make while:\n1. Keeping all tests green\n2. Improving some aspect (naming, DRY, simplicity)\n3. Not over-engineering for future requirements\n\nI think the lookup table is a clean win. Let me commit to it.\n\nActually, let me try a slightly different approach to minimize changes:\n\n**Approach: Keep named constants, but consolidate the check**\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Hardcoded quote for a single plain sword (100 base + 10 first insurance + 5 fee). */\nconst SWORD_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 };\n\n/** Hardcoded quote for a single plain amulet (60 base + 6 first insurance + 5 fee). */\nconst AMULET_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 };\n\n/** Hardcoded quote for a single plain staff (80 base + 8 first insurance + 5 fee). */\nconst STAFF_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 };\n\n/** Lookup table: type -> hardcoded quote for a single plain item. */\nconst SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: SWORD_QUOTE,\n  amulet: AMULET_QUOTE,\n  staff: STAFF_QUOTE,\n};\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return SINGLE_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"] ?? SWORD_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return SWORD_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but the named constants are now only referenced by the table. They're redundant.\n\nOK let me just inline them into the table:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Hardcoded base quotes for a single plain item of each type\n *  (base premium + first insurance + processing fee). */\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n};\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const quoteForType = PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"];\n    if (quoteForType !== undefined) {\n      return quoteForType;\n    }\n  }\n  return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword!;\n}\n```\n\nWait, the `!` at the end is the non-null assertion. Since `sword` is literally a key in the object, this should always exist. But TypeScript might not know that.\n\nLet me check tsconfig:\n\nActually, let me check that.\n\nActually, I'll use a different fallback:\n\n```typescript\nconst DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE: QuoteResult = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 };\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"] ?? DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE;\n  }\n  return DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE;\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids the `!` and is cleaner.\n\nBut the duplication between the table and DEFAULT_PLAIN_QUOTE is a problem - the magic numbers 115 and 1000 appear twice.\n\nBetter: just make sword the explicit fallback, and let TypeScript figure it out:\n\n```typescript\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 } as QuoteResult,\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 } as QuoteResult,\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 } as QuoteResult,\n} satisfies Record<string, QuoteResult>;\n\nexport function quote(...): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type as keyof typeof PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n  }\n  return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but this doesn't help TypeScript infer `?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword` as the right type. Let me think.\n\nActually, with `as const` the type is:\n```\n{\n  readonly sword: { readonly premium: 115; readonly insuranceSum: 1000 };\n  readonly amulet: { readonly premium: 71; readonly insuranceSum: 600 };\n  readonly staff: { readonly premium: 93; readonly insuranceSum: 800 };\n}\n```\n\nIndexing wi