his is getting ugly. Let me just go with the simple `??` approach:\n\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n  }\n  return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n}\n```\n\nThe `??` is defensive and harmless. This is clean.\n\nLet me go with this. Final plan locked in.\n\nWait, one more thought. The `??` is technically dead code from TypeScript's perspective because `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[\"\"]` is typed as `QuoteResult`, not `QuoteResult | undefined`. TypeScript might complain about the right side of `??` being unreachable, OR it might just compile fine because `??` accepts the same type on both sides.\n\nLet me check by writing and running. If there's an error, I'll adjust.\n\nNow let me also calculate mass carefully.\n\n## Mass Calculation\n\n### Before Refactor\n\n**Constants**:\n- `PROCESSING_FEE = 5` → 1\n- `SWORD_QUOTE = { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 }` → 2 (115, 1000)\n- `AMULET_QUOTE = { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 }` → 2 (71, 600)\n- `STAFF_QUOTE = { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 }` → 2 (93, 800)\n- \"amulet\" string in conditional → 1\n- \"staff\" string in conditional → 1\n\nTotal constants: 9\n\n**Bindings**:\n- 4 constants: PROCESSING_FEE, SWORD_QUOTE, AMULET_QUOTE, STAFF_QUOTE → 4\n- 1 function: quote → 1\n- 3 parameters: items, _customer, _quoteIndex → 3\n\nTotal bindings: 8\n\n**Invocations**:\n- `items.length` is property access (not invocation in standard APP)\n- `items[0]?.type` is property access\n- No actual function calls\n\nTotal invocations: 0\n\n**Conditionals**:\n- `if (items.length === 0)` → 1\n- `if (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"amulet\")` → 1\n- `if (items.length === 1 && items[0]?.type === \"staff\")` → 1\n\nTotal conditionals: 3\n\n**Loops**: 0\n**Assignments**: 0\n\n**Total Mass Before**: 9 + 8 + 0 + 12 + 0 + 0 = 29\n\n### After Refactor\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n};\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0]?.type ?? \"\"] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n  }\n  return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n}\n```\n\n**Constants**:\n- `PROCESSING_FEE = 5` → 1\n- 3 object literals each with 2 numeric constants → 6\n- 3 string keys: \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\" → 3\n- \"\" empty string fallback → 1\n\nTotal constants: 11\n\n**Bindings**:\n- 2 constants: PROCESSING_FEE, PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES → 2\n- 1 function: quote → 1\n- 3 parameters → 3\n\nTotal bindings: 6\n\n**Invocations**:\n- No function calls\n- 0 invocations\n\n**Conditionals**:\n- `if (items.length === 0)` → 1\n- `if (items.length === 1)` → 1\n\nTotal conditionals: 2\n\n**Loops**: 0\n**Assignments**: 0\n\n**Total Mass After**: 11 + 6 + 0 + 8 + 0 + 0 = 25\n\nMass Change: 29 → 25 (saved 4 mass). \n\nThe refactor saves 4 mass AND eliminates duplication AND makes the code more extensible.\n\nLet me write the code now and run the tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me analyze the current code and apply a refactoring following the Simple Design rules.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n**Current naming**:\n- Function `quote` — generic but appropriate at this API level\n- Constants `SWORD_QUOTE`, `AMULET_QUOTE`, `STAFF_QUOTE` — descriptive\n- `PROCESSING_FEE` — descr