sively now to start the real computation. The pattern is clear: I need to compute base premium for each item, sum them, apply policy-wide modifiers, add fee, round.\n\nLet me write a proper implementation now. I'll keep it minimal but real.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"8 passing. Let me refactor to do real computation now — the pattern is clear."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bde318c13a994ff0802c2acd","name":"subagent","arguments":{"agent":"refactor","agentScope":"both","task":"Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts\nPassing tests: 8\nRecent Green phase: Added another hardcoded branch for 3 runes (block applies) returning { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 750 }.\n\nThe implementation now has hardcoded stubs for: empty, single item lookup table, 2 runes (no block), 3 runes (block). This is unsustainable as more component-block combinations and modifiers come.\n\nPlease refactor the implementation by writing a real (but minimal) computation. The next tests will exercise:\n- 4 runes (no block, individual pricing)\n- 7 runes (no block, rounding)\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone (different types, no block)\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones (two separate blocks)\n- cursed sword (integration example 1)\n- sword with enchantment 5 (boundary)\n- ...and many more\n\nThe algorithm should:\n1. Compute item base premium: 100/60/80/40 for sword/amulet/staff/potion; 25 for components EXCEPT apply block: if a component type has EXACTLY 3 alike, charge 60 instead of 3*25=75 for that type.\n2. Compute insurance sum: 1000/600/800/400 for sword/amulet/staff/potion; 250 per component (block affects premium only).\n3. Item-specific modifiers apply to item's base premium (cursed: +50%, enchantment >=5: +30%). These do NOT apply to components.\n4. Policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base (sum of item bases): loyalty (>=2 years: -20%), first insurance (always: +10%), follow-up contract (quoteIndex>0: -15%).\n5. Final premium = (sum of item adjusted premiums) + (policy base * net policy-wide modifier) + 5 G fee, rounded UP.\n\nKnown spec details:\n- Block rule: \"block requires exactly 3\" -- 2 runes = 50, 3 runes = 60, 4 runes = 100, 7 runes = 175. So block only applies when count of a single type is exactly 3.\n- \"Alike\" means same type (rune with rune, not rune with moonstone).\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones -> two separate blocks -> 120 G base premium.\n\nRefactor to a clean real implementation. Keep all 8 tests green. Use TDD's \"minimal but real\" principle -- the implementation should correctly compute for cases not yet tested, since the patterns are clear.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":244,"output":772,"cacheRead":75520,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":69,"totalTokens":76536,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784945223649,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-cbef18bc-76f0-446e-8abd-a318d00ac7de","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_bde318c13a994ff0802c2acd","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 8 existing tests pass. The refactored implementation handles every documented upcoming test case correctly.\n\n## Refactor Report\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n**Current name**: `quote`\n**Function purpose**: Computes a customer's insurance quote (premium + insurance sum) from a list of items, customer info, and quote index.\n**Assessment**: `quote` is exactly right — the function returns a quote (in insurance domain terminology). The previous code wasted this clean name with hardcoded stubs that returned pre-baked numbers instead of computing.\n\n**Decision**: Keep `quote`. Rename internal helpers for clarity:\n- `applyItemModifiers` → `applyMainItemModifiers` (explicit that this is only for main items; components never receive it)\n- Module-level numbers like `115`, `71`, `93`, `49` (the old hardcoded stubs) → extracted as named constants tied to the actual algorithm (`MAIN_ITEM_BASES`, `COMPONENT_PREMIUM`, `BLOCK_PREMIUM`, `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE`, etc.)\n\n### Step 2: Current (pre-refactor) APP Mass — `quote.ts`\n\n```\nConstants (×1):\n  5, 115, 1000, 71, 600, 93, 800, 49, 400, 33, 250  (PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES)\n  0, 0  (empty-items return)\n  3, 71, 750  (3-runes branch)\n  60, 500  (catch-all)\n  → 19\n\nBindings (×1): ~12\n\nInvocations (×2): every() = 2\n\nConditionals (×4):\n  items.length === 0, items.length === 1,\n  items.length === 3, ??, &&\n  → 5\n\nLoops (×5): every = 5\n\nAssignments (×6): premium/insuranceSum writes = ~10\n```\n\n**Total ≈ 19 + 12 + 4 + 20 + 5 + 60 = 120**\n\n(Plus algorithmic incorrectness: would fail on 4+ runes, multi-type, modifiers, etc.)\n\n### Step 3: New (post-refactor) APP Mass — `quote.ts`\n\n```\nConstants (×1):\n  PROCESSING_FEE, MAIN_ITEM_BASES (8 numbers),\n  COMPONENT_PREMIUM, COMPONENT_INSURANCE,\n  BLOCK_SIZE, BLOCK_PREMIUM,\n  FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE, LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE, LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS, FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE,\n  CURSED_RATE, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n  → 19\n\nBindings (×1): ~18 (typeBase fields, intermediate vars in helpers)\n\nInvocations (×2):\n  groupByType, computeTypeBase, applyMainItemModifiers (per main item), computePolicyModifier, Math.ceil\n  → ~6 invocations × 2 = 12\n\nConditionals (×4):\n  MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type] check, BLOCK_SIZE check (inside computeTypeBase),\n  isMainItem check (in main loop), CURSED_RATE check, enchantment threshold check,\n  loyalty threshold check, follow-up check\n  → 7\n\nLoops (×5):\n  groupByType loop, main loop over groups, inner loop over main items\n  → 3\n\nAssignments (×6): ~15 (premium/insurance/adjusted accumulators, etc.)\n```\n\n**Total ≈ 19 + 18 + 12 + 28 + 15 + 90 = 182**\n\nMass is **higher in raw terms** — but Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps APP mass, and the old code is *not equivalent*: it silently returns wrong answers for every case except the four hardcoded ones.\n\n### Step 4: Rules Applied\n\n**Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: ✓ 8/8 passing after refactor; also verified all 12 upcoming todos produce the documented expected values.\n\n**Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**:\n- Old code: a switch on `items.length` with precomputed stubs. Reading it tells you nothing about *why* the premium is 71 for an amulet or 60 for 3 runes.\n- New code: the algorithm is visible. `computeTypeBase` reveals the block rule. `computePolicyModifier` reveals the additive nature of policy-wide modifiers. `applyMainItemModifiers` reveals which item flags actually affect pricing.\n\n**Rule 3 (No Duplication)**:\n- The 3-rune-vs-2-rune logic was duplicated as separate hardcoded stubs; now it falls out of `BLOCK_SIZE`.\n- The \"first insurance +10%, loyalty, follow-up\" logic was implicit; now `computePolicyModifier` is the single source.\n\n**Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: I considered several alternatives (precomputing counts vs. grouping, returning single `premium` vs `QuoteResult`, etc.) and chose grouping + returning both `premium` and `insuranceSum` because removing either would re-introduce hardcoding or call-site duplication.\n\n### Refactoring Summary\n\n| Aspect | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Implementation | 4 hardcoded stubs | Real algorithm |\n| Handles 4+ runes | ✗ | ✓ |\n| Handles multiple component types | ✗ | ✓ |\n| Handles item modifiers | ✗ | ✓ |\n| Handles policy modifiers | ✗ | ✓ |\n| Round-up behavior | implicit (precomputed) | explicit (`Math.ceil`) |\n| Tests | 8 pass | 8 pass + 12 upcoming verified |\n\n**All 8 existing tests green. The implementation now generalizes to every documented case and the mass increase is justified by Rule 2.**"}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts\nPassing tests: 8\nRecent Green phase: Added another hardcoded branch for 3 runes (block applies) returning { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 750 }.\n\nThe implementation now has hardcoded stubs for: empty, single item lookup table, 2 runes (no block), 3 runes (block). This is unsustainable as more component-block combinations and modifiers come.\n\nPlease refactor the implementation by writing a real (but minimal) computation. The next tests will exercise:\n- 4 runes (no block, individual pricing)\n- 7 runes (no block, rounding)\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone (different types, no block)\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones (two separate blocks)\n- cursed sword (integration example 1)\n- sword with enchantment 5 (boundary)\n- ...and many more\n\nThe algorithm should:\n1. Compute item base premium: 100/60/80/40 for sword/amulet/staff/potion; 25 for components EXCEPT apply block: if a component type has EXACTLY 3 alike, charge 60 instead of 3*25=75 for that type.\n2. Compute insurance sum: 1000/600/800/400 for sword/amulet/staff/potion; 250 per component (block affects premium only).\n3. Item-specific modifiers apply to item's base premium (cursed: +50%, enchantment >=5: +30%). These do NOT apply to components.\n4. Policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base (sum of item bases): loyalty (>=2 years: -20%), first insurance (always: +10%), follow-up contract (quoteIndex>0: -15%).\n5. Final premium = (sum of item adjusted premiums) + (policy base * net policy-wide modifier) + 5 G fee, rounded UP.\n\nKnown spec details:\n- Block rule: \"block requires exactly 3\" -- 2 runes = 50, 3 runes = 60, 4 runes = 100, 7 runes = 175. So block only applies when count of a single type is exactly 3.\n- \"Alike\" means same type (rune with rune, not rune with moonstone).\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones -> two separate blocks -> 120 G base premium.\n\nRefactor to a clean real implementation. Keep all 8 tests green. Use TDD's \"minimal but real\" principle -- the implementation should correctly compute for cases not yet tested, since the patterns are clear.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts\nPassing tests: 8\nRecent Green phase: Added another hardcoded branch for 3 runes (block applies) returning { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 750 }.\n\nThe implementation now has hardcoded stubs for: empty, single item lookup table, 2 runes (no block), 3 runes (block). This is unsustainable as more component-block combinations and modifiers come.\n\nPlease refactor the implementation by writing a real (but minimal) computation. The next tests will exercise:\n- 4 runes (no block, individual pricing)\n- 7 runes (no block, rounding)\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone (different types, no block)\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones (two separate blocks)\n- cursed sword (integration example 1)\n- sword with enchantment 5 (boundary)\n- ...and many more\n\nThe algorithm should:\n1. Compute item base premium: 100/60/80/40 for sword/amulet/staff/potion; 25 for components EXCEPT apply block: if a component type has