hile main items vary. Let me think...\n\nActually, the cleanest approach might be:\n- Main items lookup: sword=100, amulet=60, staff=80, potion=40 (premium), and same for insurance.\n- Components: base 25, insurance 250.\n- For components, apply the block rule (3 alike -> 60).\n\nOr even simpler:\n- All items have a base premium and insurance.\n- For components, the \"base premium\" depends on whether the type forms a block of 3.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The cleanest formulation:\n\n```typescript\n// Per item, compute base premium\nfunction itemBasePremium(item, typeCount) {\n  if (isMainItem(item.type)) {\n    return MAIN_ITEM_PREMIUMS[item.type];\n  }\n  // Component\n  if (typeCount === 3) {\n    return 60; // Block\n  }\n  return 25;\n}\n\nfunction itemBaseInsurance(item) {\n  if (isMainItem(item.type)) {\n    return MAIN_ITEM_INSURANCE[item.type];\n  }\n  return 250;\n}\n\nfunction itemAdjustedPremium(item, basePremium) {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but for components, do modifiers apply? The user said: \"These do NOT apply to components.\"\n\nSo:\n```typescript\nfunction itemAdjustedPremium(item, basePremium, isComponent) {\n  if (isComponent) return basePremium;\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nOr alternatively, I can detect components vs main items in a different way.\n\nLet me think about the structure:\n\n```typescript\nconst MAIN_ITEMS = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_BASE_INSURANCE = 250;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRE