e implementation should support unknown types. Looking at the test list, there's a todo: \"unknown item type -> throws\". So I should throw on unknown types.\n\nBut wait, if I use a Record lookup, accessing an unknown type returns undefined. I need to handle this case explicitly.\n\nLet me restructure:\n\n```typescript\nconst MAIN_ITEMS: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\nfunction isKnownItem(type: string): boolean {\n  return type in MAIN_ITEMS; // for now, only known main items; components also \"known\"\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but how do we distinguish components from unknown types? In the original code, the test was only for \"rune\", \"moonstone\", etc. as components. The user said:\n- Main items: sword, amulet, staff, potion\n- Components: rune, moonstone, etc.\n\nI think for now, the implementation can assume:\n- If type is in MAIN_ITEMS, it's a main item.\n- Otherwise, it's a component (with base 25, insurance 250).\n- An unknown type (neither) is still treated as a component for now, but the \"unknown item type -> throws\" test will define this behavior.\n\nActually, looking at the user's test list: \"unknown item type -> throws\". This suggests that there's some validation. Let me think about how to handle this.\n\nFor now, since the \"unknown\" test is a todo, I don't need to implement it yet. But I should structure the code so it's easy to add later.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest design:\n\n```typescript\n// Configuration: known item types and their base values\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n// Components have a uniform base value (insurable for 250 G each, premium 25 G each)\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\n\n// Block discount: 3 of the same component type cost 60 instead of 75\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```typescript\nfunction getItemTypeBase(type: string, count: number): { premium: number; insurance: number; isMain: boolean } {\n  if (type in MAIN_ITEM_BASES) {\n    return { ...MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type], isMain: true };\n  }\n  // Component\n  const premium = count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n  return {\n    premium,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    isMain: false,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction getItemAdjustedPremium(item: Item, basePremium: number, isMain: boolean): number {\n  if (!isMain) return basePremium;\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, let me think about this in a different way. The \"base premium\" for a single item (or type) is what gets summed for the policy base. The \"adjusted premium\" is what's used in the sum of adjusted item premiums.\n\nFor a main item, the adjusted premium is per-item. For a component, no modifiers.\n\nLet me write it:\n\n```typescript\nexport function quote(items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): QuoteResult {\n  // Group items by type\n  const groupedByType = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const group = groupedByType.get(item.type) ?? [];\n    group.push(item);\n    groupedByType.set(item.type, group);\n  }\n  \n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n  \n  for (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n    const typeBase = getTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    \n    if (typeBase.isMain) {\n      // Per-item adjustments\n      for (const item of group) {\n        adjustedSum += adjustedItemPremium(item, typeBase.premium);\n      }\n    } else {\n      // Components have no per-item modifiers\n      adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Policy-wide modifiers (additive)\n  let policyModifier = 0.10; // First insurance\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2) policyModifier -= 0.20;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) policyModifier -= 0.15;\n  \n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nThis looks good. Let me verify with all the tests one more time:\n\n**Test 1: empty -> 5**\n- No items, policyBase = 0, adjustedSum = 0, insuranceSum = 0.\n- Premium = 0 + 0 + 5 = 5. ✓\n\n**Test 2: single sword -> 115**\n- 1 sword: typeBase = (100, 1000, main). policyBase = 100, insurance = 1000.\n- Adjusted = 100 * 1 = 100.\n- Policy mod = 0.10.\n- Premium = 100 + 10 + 5 = 115. ✓\n\n**Test 3: single amulet -> 71**\n- 1 amulet: typeBase = (60, 600, main). policyBase = 60, insurance = 600.\n- Adjusted = 60.\n- Premium = 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. ✓\n\n**Test 4: single staff -> 93**\n- 1 staff: typeBase = (80, 800, main). policyBase = 80, insurance = 800.\n- Premium = 80 + 8 + 5 = 93. ✓\n\n**Test 5: single potion -> 49**\n- 1 potion: typeBase = (40, 400, main). policyBase = 40, insurance = 400.\n- Premium = 40 + 4 + 5 = 49. ✓\n\n**Test 6: single rune -> 33**\n- 1 rune: typeBase = (25, 250, not main). policyBase = 25, insurance = 250.\n- Adjusted = 25 (no modifiers for components).\n- Premium = 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 -> 33. ✓\n\n**Test 7: 2 runes -> 60**\n- 2 runes: typeBase = (50, 500, not main). policyBase = 50, insurance = 500.\n- Adjusted = 50.\n- Premium = 50 + 5 + 5 = 60. ✓\n\n**Test 8: 3 runes -> 71**\n- 3 runes: typeBase = (60, 750, not main). policyBase = 60, insurance = 750.\n- Premium = 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. ✓\n\nAll existing tests pass. Now let me verify the upcoming tests:\n\n**4 runes -> 115**\n- 4 runes: type