- policyBase = 100.\n- Policy mod: -0.20 + 0.10 - 0.15 = -0.25.\n- Premium = 180 + 100*(-0.25) + 5 = 180 - 25 + 5 = 160. ✓\n\nAll checks pass! Now let me write the refactored code.\n\nActually, let me think about naming. The user said \"Naming Evaluation (First Refactoring Priority)\". The current function is called `quote`. Should I rename it?\n\nLooking at the test file: `quote(items, customer, 0)`. The function is exported as `quote`. The user didn't ask me to rename it, and it's the right name for what it does (produces a quote). I'll keep it.\n\nFor internal helpers, I should use descriptive names:\n- `getTypeBase` could be more specific, like `computeTypeBasePremiumAndInsurance` or `computeBaseForType`. Or maybe break it into two: `basePremiumFor` and `insuranceSumFor`. Or have a single function that returns both since they're computed together.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest design. The current implementation has:\n- A few constants (5, 60, 100/60/80/40 for main items, 25 for components, 250 insurance, 1000/600/800/400 for main items insurance)\n- The quote function with hardcoded cases\n\nThe refactor should:\n1. Remove the hardcoded `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES` lookup.\n2. Implement the actual algorithm.\n\nLet me write the code now:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium and insurance sum for each main item type. */\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n/** Default base premium and insurance sum for a single component. */\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\n\n/** Block discount: exactly 3 components of the same type cost 60 instead of 75. */\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\n/** Policy-wide modifier rates (additive). */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n\n/**\n * Compute the base premium and insurance sum for all items of a single type.\n * Applies the block rule: 3 alike components cost 60 instead of 75.\n */\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return { premium: mainBase.premium, insurance: mainBase.insurance, isMainItem: true };\n  }\n  const premium = count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n  return {\n    premium,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * Apply item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) to a base premium.\n * Modifiers are additive and only apply to main items.\n */\nfunction applyItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number, isMainItem: boolean): number {\n  if (!isMainItem) return basePremium;\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  let modifier = FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) modifier -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) modifier -= FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return modifier;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  // Group items by type\n  const groupedByType = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const group = groupedByType.get(item.type);\n    if (group) {\n      group.push(item);\n    } else {\n      groupedByType.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n  \n  for (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    \n    if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n      for (const item of group) {\n        adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, typeBase.premium, true);\n      }\n    } else {\n      // Components have no per-item modifiers\n      adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  \n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I should think about whether to include the unknown type handling now. The \"unknown item type -> throws\" test is a todo. Let me skip that for now.\n\nActually, looking at the current code, it has a fallback: `PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0].type] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword`. This silently treats unknown types as swords. But the future test expects a throw. So I should NOT silently fall back.\n\nFor now, I'll let the current implementation just not validate (so unknown types would be treated as components with base 25). This won't break existing tests, and the unknown test will be added later to define the throwing behavior.\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n- Items with material? Currently, the material field exists but isn't used. The tests show \"steel\" as the material for a sword, but the result doesn't depend on it. So I can ignore it.\n- Items with enchantment but not >=5: same as no enchantment (test: sword with enchantment 4 -> 115).\n\nLet me also reconsider naming. The current function name `quote` is fine. For helpers:\n- `computeTypeBase` - good, describes what it does\n- `applyItemModifiers` - good, describes what it does\n- `computePolicyModifier` - good\n\nActually, looking again, the `isMainItem` field in TypeBase is used to decide whether to apply per-item modifiers. This couples the function to the modifier logic. An alternative: just have `computeTypeBase` return `{ premium, insurance }` and detect main vs component inline.\n\nBut the current design is cleaner because `computeTypeBase` is the single source of truth for \"what kind of item is this\". Let me keep it.\n\nHmm, actually, let me think about this. The `isMainItem` flag is used to:\n1. Apply per-item modifiers in the loop.\n\nBut conceptually, \"is this a main item\" is a property of the type, not the base. Maybe I should have a separate `isMainItemType(type)` function. Or have `computeTypeBase` not return the flag and let the caller compute it.\n\nActually, let me look at it from a different angle. The structure of the code could be:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n  const count = group.length;\n  const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, count);\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n  \n  if (type in MAIN_ITEM_BASES) {\n    // Main item: apply per-item modifiers\n    for (const item of group) {\n      adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, typeBase.premium);\n    }\n  } else {\n    adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids the `isMainItem` field, but it duplicates the \"is main item\" check (once in computeTypeBase, once here). Not great.\n\nAlternative: keep `isMainItem` in TypeBase. Or extract a `isMainItem(type)` function and use it in both places. Or use a single function that returns everything.\n\nLet me think about which is cleanest. I think the cleanest is:\n\n```typescript\nfunction getItemBase(type: string, count: number): { premium: number; insurance: number } {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return { premium: mainBase.premium * count, insurance: mainBase.insurance * count };\n  }\n  // Component\n  const premium = count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n  return {\n    premium,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction getItemAdjustedPremium(item: Item, basePremium: number, isMainItem: boolean): number {\n  if (!isMainItem) return basePremium;\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but then I need to check `isMainItem` separately. Let me think...\n\nActually, the cleanest is to detect once and pass through:\n\n```typescript\nfunction isMainItem(type: string): boolean {\n  return type in MAIN_ITEM_BASES;\n}\n```\n\nOr to bundle everything together:\n\n```typescript\nfunction getItemPremiumAndInsurance(type: string, count: number): { premium: number; insurance: number } {\n  if (isMainItem(type)) {\n    const base = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n    return { premium: base.premium * count, insurance: base.insurance * count };\n  }\n  const premium = count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n  return { premium, insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count };\n}\n```\n\nWait, why multiply by count for main items? Because if you have 2 swords, the base premium is 2 * 100 = 200. Right. So `computeTypeBase` should return the total for all items of that type.\n\nHmm, but what about the per-item adjustment for main items? If I have 2 swords (one cursed, one plain), the adjusted is 100*1.5 + 100*1.0 = 250, but the policy base is 2*100 = 200.\n\nSo the per-item base is what's stored in MAIN_ITEM_BASES (single item), and the total is multiplied by count for policyBase. The per-item adjustment uses the per-item base.\n\nLet me refactor:\n\n```typescript\nfunction getItemBase(type: string): { premium: number; insurance: number; isMainItem: boolean } {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return { premium: mainBase.premium, insurance: mainBase.insurance, isMainItem: true };\n  }\n  // Component (per-item)\n  return {\n    premium: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThen in the loop:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n  const count = group.length;\n  const base = getItemBase(type);\n  const typePremiumTotal = base.isMainItem \n    ? base.premium * count \n    : (count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count);\n  const typeInsuranceTotal = base.insurance * count;\n  \n  policyBase += typePremiumTotal;\n  insuranceSum += typeInsuranceTotal;\n  \n  if (base.isMainItem) {\n    // Apply per-item modifiers\n    for (const item of group) {\n      adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, base.premium);\n    }\n  } else {\n    adjustedSum += typePremiumTotal;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the conditional for component premium is awkward. Let me clean up:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeTotals(type: string, count: number): { premium: number; insurance: number; perItemBase: number; isMainItem: boolean } {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  const premium = count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n  return {\n    premium,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nOK this is getting complex. Let me step back and think about what's the simplest expression.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest is:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): { premium: number; insurance: number; isMainItem: boolean } {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  // Component\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWhere `premium` and `insurance` are the totals for all items of this type. And `isMainItem` indicates whether modifiers apply per-item.\n\nIn the loop:\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n  const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n  \n  if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n    // Main items: per-item base, per-item modifiers\n    const perItemBase = typeBase.premium / group.length;\n    for (const item of group) {\n      adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, perItemBase);\n    }\n  } else {\n    // Components: no per-item modifiers\n    adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut `typeBase.premium / group.length` is awkward. Let me think of another way.\n\nAlternative: make computeTypeBase return per-item values, and compute totals in the loop.\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeItemBase(type: string): { premium: number; insurance: number; isMainItem: boolean } {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction computeComponentBlockPremium(count: number): number {\n  return count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n}\n```\n\nIn the loop:\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n  const itemBase = computeItemBase(type);\n  \n  let typePremiumTotal: number;\n  let typeInsuranceTotal: number;\n  \n  if (itemBase.isMainItem) {\n    typePremiumTotal = itemBase.premium * group.length;\n    typeInsuranceTotal = itemBase.insurance * group.length;\n  } else {\n    typePremiumTotal = computeComponentBlockPremium(group.length);\n    typeInsuranceTotal = itemBase.insurance * group.length;\n  }\n  \n  policyBase += typePremiumTotal;\n  insuranceSum += typeInsuranceTotal;\n  \n  if (itemBase.isMainItem) {\n    for (const item of group) {\n      adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, itemBase.premium);\n    }\n  } else {\n    adjustedSum += typePremiumTotal;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is still complex. Let me try yet another structure.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest is to have:\n1. `computeBasePremium(type, count)`: returns the total base premium for all items of this type.\n2. `computeBaseInsurance(type, count)`: returns the total insurance for all items.\n3. `applyItemModifiers(item, basePremium)`: applies per-item modifiers to a single item.\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeBasePremium(type: string, count: number): number {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) return mainBase.premium * count;\n  return count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count;\n}\n\nfunction computeBaseInsurance(type: string, count: number): number {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) return mainBase.insurance * count;\n  return COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count;\n}\n\nfunction applyItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n\nfunction isMainItem(type: string): boolean {\n  return type in MAIN_ITEM_BASES;\n}\n```\n\nThen in the loop:\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupedByType) {\n  const count = group.length;\n  const typePremium = computeBasePremium(type, count);\n  const typeInsurance = computeBaseInsurance(type, count);\n  policyBase += typePremium;\n  insuranceSum += typeInsurance;\n  \n  if (isMainItem(type)) {\n    const perItemBase = typePremium / count;\n    for (const item of group) {\n      adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, perItemBase);\n    }\n  } else {\n    adjustedSum += typePremium;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut `typePremium / count` is ugly. Hmm.\n\nOK let me just bite the bullet and pass per-item base separately:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeItemTypeBase(type: string, count: number): {\n  premiumTotal: number;\n  insuranceTotal: number;\n  perItemBase: number;\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n} {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premiumTotal: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insuranceTotal: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premiumTotal: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insuranceTotal: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThis is a bit chunky but clear. Actually, I realize I can simplify by removing `isMainItem` and just checking if perItemBase equals a known main item value... no, that's worse.\n\nOK let me commit to one design. I'll go with this:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Flat fee charged on every quote, regardless of items. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium and insurance sum for each main (non-component) item type. */\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n/** Single component's base premium and insurance sum. */\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\n\n/** When a component type appears in a block of exactly 3, that type costs 60 (not 75). */\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\n/** Policy-wide modifier rates (additive). */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n  perItemBase: number;\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n\n/** Compute totals and per-item base for all items of a single type. */\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Apply item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) to a base premium. */\nfunction applyItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n\n/** Sum the additive policy-wide modifier rates for this quote. */\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  let modifier = FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) modifier -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) modifier -= FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return modifier;\n}\n\n/** Group consecutive items of the same type (preserves input order). */\nfunction groupByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const grouped = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const existing = grouped.get(item.type);\n    if (existing) {\n      existing.push(item);\n    } else {\n      grouped.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n  }\n  return grouped;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n  \n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    \n    if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n      for (const item of group) {\n        adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n      }\n    } else {\n      // Components: no per-item modifiers; total type premium goes directly.\n      adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  \n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nThis looks good. Let me double-check the APP mass.\n\nHmm actually, let me reconsider. Should I use a Map iteration or just iterate directly? Let me think about clarity vs mass.\n\nThe current implementation has hardcoded stubs. The refactor should:\n- Remove the hardcoded branches (Rule 3: no duplication, but here it's not duplication, it's just hardcoded)\n- Use the actual algorithm (Rule 2: reveals intent)\n\nWait, I need to think about what makes sense. The task is to write a real implementation. The mass calculation is secondary.\n\nLet me just write the code clearly, then calculate mass at the end.\n\nActually, I realize there's one more thing to consider. The user mentioned:\n\"a real (but minimal) computation\"\n\nSo I should write a real computation that's minimal (not over-engineered). My design above feels right.\n\nLet me also consider: should I use a Map or an object? Map is more idiomatic for dynamic keys, but object might be more concise. Since the items array can have any type, Map is better.\n\nActually, let me think about whether grouping is necessary at all. The block rule needs to know the count of each type. So yes, grouping is necessary. Alternatively, I could compute the count for each item by scanning the array, but that's O(n²). Grouping once is O(n).\n\nOK let me also think about edge cases:\n- What if the items array has many types? My algorithm handles it.\n- What if a type appears multiple times (like 3 swords)? Then per-item modifiers apply to each. So 2 cursed swords: 2 * 100 * 1.5 = 300 adjusted. Policy base = 2 * 100 = 200.\n\nLet me also reconsider: what if the array is empty? My algorithm returns policyBase = 0, adjustedSum = 0, insuranceSum = 0. Premium = 0 + 0 + 5 = 5. ✓\n\nOK let me write this out and test it.\n\nWait, I just realized I might want to think about whether to handle the case where multiple items of the same type have different modifiers. For example:\n- 2 swords: one cursed, one not. Adjusted = 100*1.5 + 100*1.0 = 250. Policy base = 200.\n\nThis is handled correctly by my loop: each item gets its own modifier applied.\n\nOK let me write the code.\n\nActually, one more consideration. The original code had:\n```typescript\nreturn PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0].type] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n```\n\nThis was a fallback for unknown types. I'm removing this. For now, unknown types will be treated as components (base 25, insurance 250). This is a behavior change, but the existing tests don't cover unknown types, so it shouldn't break anything.\n\nHmm, but wait, what if the \"unknown item type -> throws\" test is added later? Currently, my implementation would treat unknown types as components, which would not throw. Let me think about whether I should add a throw now or wait.\n\nThe user said: \"Refactor to a clean real implementation. Keep all 8 tests green.\"\n\nSo I should keep the 8 tests green. The \"unknown item type -> throws\" test is in the todo list, but it's not yet a test. So I don't need to implement throwing for unk