irst insurance is 0 for empty.\n- My formula: 0 * 0.10 = 0. So premium = 0 + 0 + 5 = 5. ✓\n\nOK, the math works out. Let me proceed.\n\nOne more thing: let me think about whether I should simplify by not computing first insurance when items is empty. Actually, it's mathematically equivalent (0 * anything = 0), so it doesn't matter. I'll keep it simple.\n\nNow let me also calculate the APP mass for the refactored code.\n\nCurrent implementation mass:\n- Constants: PROCESSING_FEE = 5 (1) + PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES object (5 entries: 10 constants: 10) + 71, 60, etc. hardcoded (8 numbers: 8) = ~20\n- Bindings: lots of variable references in PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES + items.length, items[0].type, etc.\n- Invocations: every, etc.\n- Conditionals: if items.length === 0, if items.length === 1, if items.length === 3, etc.\n\nHmm, hard to estimate without a careful count. Let me focus on the refactored version.\n\nRefactored implementation mass:\nConstants:\n- PROCESSING_FEE = 5 (1)\n- MAIN_ITEM_BASES object with 4 entries (8 constants: 8)\n- COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25 (1)\n- COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250 (1)\n- BLOCK_SIZE = 3 (1)\n- BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60 (1)\n- FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10 (1)\n- LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20 (1)\n- LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2 (1)\n- FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15 (1)\nTotal constants: ~17\n\nBindings:\n- typeBase, premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem (in TypeBase) - these are type definitions, not runtime\n- groupByType function: grouped, existing\n- computeTypeBase: count, mainBase, premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem (returned)\n- applyItemModifiers: modifier\n- computePolicyModifier: modifier\n- quote: policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum, typeBase, type, group, perItemBase (returned), policyModifier, premium\nTotal bindings: ~20\n\nInvocations:\n- Object.keys(MAIN_ITEM_BASES) - no wait, that doesn't appear\n- groupByType call: 2\n- get on Map: 1\n- set on Map: 2\n- push: 2\n- computeTypeBase call: 2\n- computePolicyModifier call: 2\n- applyItemModifiers call: 2 (per item in main item group)\n- Math.ceil call: 2\n- arithmetic ops: *, +, / - these are invocations? Let me think...\n\nHmm, the APP mass treats arithmetic operators as... actually, I think operators like +, -, *, /, %, =, ++ are assignments, not invocations. Let me re-read.\n\nLooking at the APP rules:\n- Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\n- Invocation (Mass: 2): Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`)\n\nSo arithmetic operators are assignments (when they mutate) or part of expressions (when they don't). Hmm.\n\nActually, `x = y + z` has one assignment (x = ...) and one + (binary operator, which I think is also an assignment since it produces a new value bound to x).\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me just focus on writing clean code and estimate mass qualitatively.\n\nActually, I realize the APP calculation is meant to be approximate. Let me not worry about exact numbers and just focus on writing clean code.\n\nLet me write the refactored code now.\n\nWait, one more design decision: should I use a Map or a plain object for grouping? Map is more idiomatic for dynamic keys, but Object.create(null) or {} is simpler. I'll use Map since the keys are dynamic.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Do I even need to group? The block rule needs the count of each type. So:\n- For each item, count how many items of the same type exist in the array.\n- Or, group first, then iterate.\n\nThe first approach is O(n²) in the worst case (or O(n) if I precompute counts in a Map). The second is O(n) with one pass for grouping.\n\nLet me use the precomputed counts approach:\n\n```typescript\nfunction countByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n```\n\nThen iterate items once and use counts.get(item.type) when needed.\n\nHmm, but this is similar complexity to grouping. Let me just go with grouping.\n\nActually, for the cleanest code, let me think about which approach minimizes duplication:\n\nApproach 1: Group then iterate\n```typescript\nconst grouped = groupByType(items);\nfor (const [type, group] of grouped) {\n  // process each group\n}\n```\n\nApproach 2: Pre-count, iterate items\n```typescript\nconst counts = countByType(items);\nfor (const item of items) {\n  const count = counts.get(item.type);\n  // process each item\n}\n```\n\nIn approach 2, if there are 3 runes, I'd iterate 3 times. Each iteration would compute \"is this a main item\" and \"what's the block premium\". I'd be repeating the \"is this a block\" check 3 times.\n\nIn approach 1, I'd compute it once per group.\n\nApproach 1 is more efficient and arguably cleaner. Let me go with it.\n\nBut wait, in approach 1, for main items, I still need to iterate each item in the group to apply per-item modifiers. So the structure is:\n- Iterate groups (for type-level computation)\n- For each main item group, iterate items (for per-item modifiers)\n\nThat's two nested loops, which is fine.\n\nLet me write the final code.\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Flat processing fee charged on every quote. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium and insurance sum for a single main item of each type. */\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n/** Base values for one component. A block of exactly 3 alike costs 60 instead of 75. */\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\n/** Policy-wide modifier rates (summed additively). */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n  perItemBase: number;\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n\n/** Totals and per-item base for a single type. Applies the component block rule. */\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Apply per-item modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) to a base premium. */\nfunction applyItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n\n/** Sum the additive policy-wide modifier rates for this quote. */\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  let modifier = FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) modifier -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) modifier -= FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return modifier;\n}\n\n/** Group items by their type, preserving input order. */\nfunction groupByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const groups = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const existing = groups.get(item.type);\n    if (existing) {\n      existing.push(item);\n    } else {\n      groups.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n  \n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    \n    if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n      for (const item of group) {\n        adjustedSum += applyItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n      }\n    } else {\n      adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  \n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I want to double-check one thing. For `cursed sword + plain amulet`:\n- Cursed sword: typeBase.premium = 100 * 1 = 100, isMainItem = true, perItemBase = 100.\n  - applyItemModifiers(cursed_sword, 100) = 100 * 1.5 = 150.\n- Plain amulet: typeBase.premium = 60 * 1 = 60, isMainItem = true, perItemBase = 60.\n  - applyItemModifiers(plain_amulet, 60) = 60 * 1.0 = 60.\n- adjustedSum = 150 + 60 = 210.\n- policyBase = 100 + 60 = 160.\n- policyModifier = 0.10.\n- premium = 210 + 160*0.10 + 5 = 210 + 16 + 5 = 231. ✓\n\nOK, looks good. Let me also verify `long-standing customer (3 years, 2nd quote) + cursed sword enchant 7 -> 160`:\n- Cursed sword enchant 7: typeBase.premium = 100, isMainItem = true, perItemBase = 100.\n  - applyItemModifiers: 100 * (1 + 0.5 + 0.3) = 180.\n- adjustedSum = 180.\n- policyBase = 100.\n- policyModifier = 0.10 - 0.20 - 0.15 = -0.25.\n- premium = 180 + 100*(-0.25) + 5 = 180 - 25 + 5 = 160. ✓\n\nNow let me think about whether there are any issues with this design.\n\nOne thing I notice: the `applyItemModifiers` function applies modifiers to any item, but it should only apply to main items. However, since we only call it inside the `if (typeBase.isMainItem)` branch, this is enforced by the caller. The function itself doesn't check, but the comment makes it clear.\n\nAlternatively, I could add an `isMainItem` parameter to `applyItemModifiers` to enforce it. But that's overkill since the caller already checks.\n\nActually, looking again, the function name is `applyItemModifiers` and it doesn't say \"for main items only\". The caller is responsible. This is fine.\n\nHmm wait, what if I extract the function to be more robust? Like:\n\n```typescript\nfunction applyItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number, isMainItem: boolean): number {\n  if (!isMainItem) return basePremium;\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nThis is more defensive but adds complexity. Let me think about which is better.\n\nFor now, I'll go with the simpler version. The caller is the only place that calls it, and it only calls it for main items.\n\nActually wait, I realize there's another consideration. The function signature suggests it's general-purpose (\"apply item modifiers\"), but it's really only for main items. Let me rename it to make this explicit:\n\n```typescript\nfunction applyMainItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nThis is clearer. The caller only calls it for main items, and the name reflects that.\n\nOK let me write the final version with this rename.\n\nActually, I realize one more thing. Let me reconsider the design. The block rule applies per-type: if a component type has exactly 3 alike, the premium for that type is 60. But what about 6 runes? My code says: 6 runes -> premium = 25*6 = 150 (no block). Is that right?\n\nThe user said: \"block only applies when count of a single type is exactly 3\". So 6 ≠ 3, no block. So 25*6 = 150. That seems right.\n\nBut wait, could 6 runes be interpreted as 2 blocks of 3? The user explicitly said \"block only applies when count of a single type is exactly 3\". So no, 6 runes is not 2 blocks. It's 6 individual components.\n\nBut the test \"3 runes + 3 moonstones (two separate blocks) -> premium 137 G\" confirms that each block is per-type. So 3 runes = 1 block, 3 moonstones = 1 block, total = 60 + 60 = 120.\n\nMy code handles this correctly because I group by type and apply the block rule per group.\n\nOK let me finalize the code.\n\nOne more thought: should I extract the policy modifier computation as a separate concern? It's already in `computePolicyModifier`. Good.\n\nAlright, let me write the final code.\n\nActually, I realize there's one more thing. Let me check the existing code carefully:\n\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe existing code has an early return for empty items. My refactor handles this naturally (no loop iterations). Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether I need the `_customer` underscore prefix. In my refactor, customer is used. So I'll remove the underscore.\n\nOK let me write the code and test it.\n\nActually wait, I want to think about whether there are alternative designs that might be simpler.\n\nAlternative: Instead of grouping, use a `for` loop that computes counts on the fly.\n\n```typescript\nconst counts = new Map<string, number>();\nfor (const item of items) {\n  counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n}\n\nlet policyBase = 0;\nlet insuranceSum = 0;\nlet adjustedSum = 0;\nconst processedTypes = new Set<string>();\n\nfor (const item of items) {\n  if (processedTypes.has(item.type)) continue; // Process each type only once for totals\n  processedTypes.add(item.type);\n  \n  const count = counts.get(item.type)!;\n  // ... process type\n}\n```\n\nThis is uglier. Let me stick with grouping.\n\nAlternative 2: Single pass with per-item computation, but block rule is computed per-type.\n\nThis requires knowing the count when processing each item. So pre-compute counts, then iterate items.\n\n```typescript\nconst counts = new Map<string, number>();\nfor (const item of items) {\n  counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n}\n\nlet policyBase = 0;\nlet insuranceSum = 0;\nlet adjustedSum = 0;\n\nfor (const item of items) {\n  const count = counts.get(item.type)!;\n  const base = computeBase(item.type, count);\n  // But this would double-count policyBase for multiple items of same type!\n}\n```\n\nThe issue: for multiple items of the same type, we'd add to policyBase multiple times. We'd need to divide or pre-divide.\n\nActually, we could pre-divide: for each item, the \"contribution\" to policyBase is `basePremium / count`. But that's ugly.\n\nBetter: handle policyBase/insuranceSum per-type, and adjustedSum per-item.\n\nOK grouping is the cleanest. Let me commit to it.\n\nAlright, final code:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Flat processing fee charged on every quote. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium and insurance sum for a single main item of each type. */\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n/** Component pricing: per-item base, plus a \"block\" discount when exactly 3 alike appear. */\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\n/** Policy-wide modifier rates (summed additively into a single net modifier). */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n  perItemBase: number;\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n\n/** Totals and per-item base for one type group. Applies the component block rule. */\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Apply per-item modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) to a main item's base premium. */\nfunction applyMainItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += 0.5;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) modifier += 0.3;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n\n/** Sum the additive policy-wide modifier rates for this quote. */\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  let modifier = FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) modifier -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) modifier -= FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return modifier;\n}\n\n/** Group items by type, preserving input order. */\nfunction groupByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const groups = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const existing = groups.get(item.type);\n    if (existing) {\n      existing.push(item);\n    } else {\n      groups.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n  \n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    \n    if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n      for (const item of group) {\n        adjustedSum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n      }\n    } else {\n      adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  \n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, let me think about whether the function `quote` is too long or has too many responsibilities. It does:\n1. Group items by type.\n2. Compute totals and adjusted sums.\n3. Compute policy modifier.\n4. Final calculation.\n\nThis could be split, but each step is small. I think it's fine as-is.\n\nLet me also think about whether to add early returns for clarity. For example, if items is empty, we could return early. But the natural loop handles it correctly. Adding an early return is unnecessary complexity.\n\nOK let me write the code to file and run the tests.\n\nWait, I should think about the APP mass calculation more carefully. Let me count.\n\nCurrent implementation (hardcoded stubs):\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 49, insuranceSum: 400 },\n  rune: { premium: 33, insuranceSum: 250 },\n};\n\nexport function quote(items, _customer, _quoteIndex): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0].type] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n  }\n  if (items.length === 3 && items.every((i) => i.type === \"rune\")) {\n    return { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 750 };\n  }\n  return { premium: 60, insuranceSum: 500 };\n}\n```\n\nConstants:\n- 5 (PROCESSING_FEE) = 1\n- 115, 1000, 71, 600, 93, 800, 49, 400, 33, 250 (PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES) = 10\n- 71, 750 (3 runes) = 2\n- 60, 500 (default) = 2\nTotal: 15\n\nBindings:\n- items, _customer, _quoteIndex (params) = 3\n- PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES (module-level) = 1\n- PROCESSING_FEE (module-level) = 1\n- PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword (in fallback) - this is an access, count as binding?\n- items[0].type (in lookup) - access + binding\n- items.length (multiple times) - access + binding\n- items.every (in 3 runes check) - access\nHmm, this is getting hard to count exactly. Let me approximate.\n\nLet me focus on the difference. The refactored version has more constants (named magic numbers), more helper functions, more loops, but no hardcoded branches.\n\nRefactored implementation:\nConstants (named):\n- 5 (PROCESSING_FEE) = 1\n- 100, 1000 (sword) = 2\n- 60, 600 (amulet) = 2\n- 80, 800 (staff) = 2\n- 40, 400 (potion) = 2\n- 25 (COMPONENT_PREMIUM) = 1\n- 250 (COMPONENT_INSURANCE) = 1\n- 3 (BLOCK_SIZE) = 1\n- 60 (BLOCK_PREMIUM) = 1\n- 0.10 (FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) = 1\n- 0.20 (LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE) = 1\n- 2 (LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) = 1\n- 0.15 (FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE) = 1\nTotal: 17\n\nPlus computed constants in the function body: 1 (each \"0\" for initialized sums? Let me not count initializations).\n\nBindings:\n- typeBase (4 fields), premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem, etc. (lots)\n\nHonestly, the mass comparison is messy. Let me focus on the qualitative comparison:\n- The refactored code has more named constants (lower cognitive load).\n- The refactored code has more functions (better organization).\n- The refactored code has no hardcoded stubs (more general).\n- The refactored code has the same number of \"logical\" branches (one for main item, one for component).\n\nI think the refactored code is cleaner even if the raw mass is higher. Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps APP.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nActually, I realize one thing. Let me check if `existing` is shadowed or anything weird in `groupByType`. No, it's fine.\n\nAlso, I want to make sure I don't have any TypeScript issues. The `MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type]` could be undefined, which I'm handling with the `if (mainBase)` check. Good.\n\nOK let me write it.\n\nWait, one more consideration. I should make sure the test for \"single amulet\" still passes. Let me re-verify:\n\nTest 3: single amulet -> premium 71 G\n- items = [{ type: \"amulet\" }], customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 }, quoteIndex = 0\n- groupByType: { amulet: [amulet] }\n- computeTypeBase(\"amulet\", 1): mainBase = { premium: 60, insurance: 600 }. Returns { premium: 60, insurance: 600, perItemBase: 60, isMainItem: true }.\n- policyBase += 60, insuranceSum += 600.\n- isMainItem: applyMainItemModifiers(amulet, 60) = 60 * 1 = 60.\n- adjustedSum = 60.\n- policyModifier = 0.10 (no loyalty, no follow-up).\n- premium = 60 + 60*0.10 + 5 = 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. ✓\n\nAll good.\n\nLet me also think about the case where there are multiple items of the same main type. E.g., 2 swords (one cursed, one plain):\n- computeTypeBase(\"sword\", 2): returns { premium: 100*2 = 200, insurance: 1000*2 = 2000, perItemBase: 100, isMainItem: true }.\n- policyBase = 200, insuranceSum = 2000.\n- For each sword: applyMainItemModifiers(sword, 100).\n  - Cursed: 100 * 1.5 = 150.\n  - Plain: 100 * 1.0 = 100.\n- adjustedSum = 250.\n- premium = 250 + 200*0.10 + 5 = 250 + 20 + 5 = 275.\n\nThat seems right.\n\nOK let me write the code now.\n\nActually, I want to also think about one more thing: the type of `quote`. Should it take `Item[]` or a more specific type? The current signature is fine. The Item type has optional `material`, `enchantment`, `cursed`. My code handles these correctly.\n\nLet me also verify: what if an item has `enchantment: 0`? Then `enchantment >= 5` is false. So no modifier. Correct.\n\nWhat if `enchantment: undefined`? Then `enchantment !== undefined` is false. So no modifier. Correct.\n\nWhat if `cursed: false`? Then `item.cursed` is false, so no modifier. Correct.\n\nWhat if `cursed: undefined`? Then `item.cursed` is undefined (falsy), so no modifier. Correct.\n\nOK the logic is robust.\n\nLet me finalize and write the code.\n\nWait, I realize I want to do a final sanity check on the entire algorithm. Let me trace through test 8 (3 runes -> 71):\n\n- items = [rune, rune, rune], customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 }, quoteIndex = 0\n- groupByType: { rune: [rune, rune, rune] }\n- computeTypeBase(\"rune\", 3): mainBase = undefined (rune is not in MAIN_ITEM_BASES).\n  - premium = 3 === 3 ? 60 : 75 = 60.\n  - insurance = 250 * 3 = 750.\n  - perItemBase = 25.\n  - isMainItem = false.\n- policyBase += 60 = 60.\n- insuranceSum += 750 = 750.\n- isMainItem is false, so adjustedSum += typeBase.premium = 60.\n- adjustedSum = 60.\n- policyModifier = 0.10.\n- premium = 60 + 60*0.10 + 5 = 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. ✓\n\nInsurance = 750. ✓\n\n\n\nOne more: test 7 (2 runes -> 60):\n- items = [rune, rune], customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 }, quoteIndex = 0\n- groupByType: { rune: [rune, rune] }\n- computeTypeBase(\"rune\", 2): premium = 2 === 3 ? 60 : 50 = 50. insurance = 500. isMainItem = false.\n- policyBase = 50, insuranceSum = 500.\n- adjustedSum = 50.\n- premium = 50 + 50*0.10 + 5 = 60. ✓\n\n\n\nNow let me write the code to the file.\n\nHmm wait, I realized I should also verify the round-up behavior. Test 6: single rune -> 33 (25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 -> 33).\n\nMy code: premium = 25 + 25*0.10 + 5 = 32.5. Math.ceil(32.5) = 33. ✓\n\n\n\nAnd test \"7 runes -> 198\": premium = 175 + 175*0.10 + 5 = 197.5. Math.ceil(197.5) = 198. ✓\n\n\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nActually one final thought: I should think about whether to add documentation comments. The current implementation has a few comments. My refactored version should also have comments for clarity. I've already added some.\n\nOK let me write it.\n\nI'll write the new file then run tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll start by reading the refactor agent skill and exploring the current state, then refactor the implementation."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4051b9b14d174979af2f274a","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/skills/refactor/SKILL.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_07d07904869f49e79987f2e7","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents/ 2>/dev/null || echo \"no agents dir\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":565,"output":22466,"cacheRead":8704,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":22284,"totalTokens":31735,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784945256920,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-36af90ea-6750-4a57-b689-d98a31cdaa90","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4051b9b14d174979af2f274a","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ENOENT: no such file or directory, access '/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/skills/refactor/SKILL.md'"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1784945816787},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_07d07904869f49e79987f2e7","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"refactor.md\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784945816787},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7aed35fdeca84279adefba50","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents/refactor.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":209,"output":77,"cacheRead":31616,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":31902,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784945816789,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-adfa710e-e9d5-4ef2-a4c7-609b1488a299","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7aed35fdeca84279adefba50","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: refactor\ndescription: Refactoring specialist. Applies Simple Design Rules and the Absolute Priority Premise (APP) to improve code while keeping all tests green. Returns a summary of what changed and why.\ntools: read, write, edit, bash, grep, find, ls\n---\n\n\n## Your Mission\n\nGuide the requester through a refactoring pass by helping them:\n1. **MUST attempt at least one refactoring** - mandatory, not optional\n2. Apply the Four Rules of Simple Design in priority order\n3. Use Absolute Priority Premise (APP) to measure code improvements\n4. Improve code quality while keeping all tests green\n5. Document refactoring decisions and mass calculations\n6. If no improvement is possible, explicitly document why\n\n## Refactoring Rules\n\n- **Mandatory refactoring attempt**: MUST try at least one improvement\n- **Tests must stay green**: Never break passing tests\n- **Apply Simple Design Rules**: In priority order (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4)\n- **Calculate APP mass**: Before and after refactoring\n- **Document decisions**: Explain improvements or why none were possible\n- **Naming is first priority**: Evaluate if function name still fits its purpose\n\n### Simple Design Rules (Priority Order)\n\n#### Rule 1: Tests Pass\n- **Highest priority** - never compromise working code\n- All tests must pass before and after refactoring\n- If tests fail, revert and try different approach\n\n#### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n- **Second priority** - clarity trumps everything else (including APP)\n- Use meaningful names for variables, functions, classes\n- Structure code to be self-documenting\n- Prefer explicit over clever code\n- **Naming Evaluation (First Refactoring Priority)**:\n  - Ask: \"Does this name clearly describe what the function actually does based on all tests we have so far?\"\n  - Ask: \"Has the function's purpose become clearer/more specific through the latest test?\"\n  - Rename if the name doesn't capture the current full intent\n  - Especially critical when new functionality changes the nature of what the function does\n\n#### Rule 3: No Duplication (DRY)\n- **Third priority** - extract common functionality\n- Look for obvious and conceptual duplication\n- Knowledge should have single representation\n- **Balance with Rule 2**: If DRY hurts clarity, choose clarity\n\n#### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- **Lowest priority** - minimize code elements\n- Remove unnecessary abstractions\n- Keep it simple - don't over-engineer\n- Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose\n\n### Absolute Priority Premise (APP)\n\n#### Mass Calculation\n```\nTotal Mass = (constants x 1) + (bindings x 1) + (invocations x 2) +\n             (conditionals x 4) + (loops x 5) + (assignments x 6)\n```\n\n#### Component Values\n- **Constant** (Mass: 1): Literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`)\n- **Binding/Scalar** (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters (`amount`, `result`)\n- **Invocation** (Mass: 2): Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`)\n- **Conditional** (Mass: 4): Control flow (`if`, `switch`, `?:`)\n- **Loop** (Mass: 5): Iteration (`for`, `forEach`, `map`)\n- **Assignment** (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\n\n#### Guidelines\n- **Lower mass = Better code** (generally)\n- **Rule 2 trumps APP**: Clarity over low mass\n- **Use during refactoring**: Compare before/after mass\n- **Context matters**: Don't sacrifice readability for mass\n\n## Refactoring Process\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\nBefore anything else, evaluate the naming:\n```\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- Current name: `calculate`\n- Function purpose: \"adds numbers from an array\"\n- Question: Does \"calculate\" clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: Too generic - \"calculate\" could mean anything\n- Recommendation: Rename to `sumNumbers` or keep if name fits\n\nDecision: [Rename to X] or [Keep current name because Y]\n```\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\nBefore making changes, calculate current code mass:\n```\n**Current Code Mass**:\nfunction calculate(numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);\n}\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (numbers, sum, num) = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass: 13\n```\n\n### Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules (in order)\nSystematically evaluate each rule:\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 1: Tests Pass\n- Are all tests currently passing?\n- If not, fix before refactoring\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n- Are names clear and descriptive?\n- Is code structure self-documenting?\n- Can intent be improved?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Rename variables for clarity\n- Extract explaining variables\n- Rename functions to match purpose\n- Restructure for readability\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 3: No Duplication\n- Is there duplicated code?\n- Is there conceptual duplication?\n- Can common logic be extracted?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Extract helper functions\n- Remove copy-paste code\n- Consolidate similar logic\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- Are there unnecessary abstractions?\n- Can code be simplified?\n- Are all elements necessary?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Remove unused functions/variables\n- Simplify over-engineered solutions\n- Inline unnecessary extractions\n\n### Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n- Make ONE improvement at a time\n- Run tests after each change\n- Ensure tests stay green\n- If tests fail, revert change\n\n### Step 5: Calculate New APP Mass\nAfter refactoring, recalculate mass:\n```\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n[refactored code]\n\nComponent Count:\n[detailed breakdown]\n\nTotal Mass: [new total]\nMass Change: [old mass] -> [new mass] (delta [difference])\n```\n\n### Step 6: Document Decision\nExplain the refactoring outcome:\n\n**If Improvements Made:**\n```\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Naming: Renamed `calculate` to `sumNumbers` (better reveals intent)\n- Mass: Reduced from 13 to 11 (removed conditional)\n- Rule 2: Improved clarity with explaining variable\n\nBenefits:\n- Code now clearly states it sums numbers\n- Reduced complexity (lower mass)\n- More maintainable\n```\n\n**If No Improvements Possible:**\n```\n**Refactoring Evaluation**:\n- Naming: `calculate` already clearly describes purpose\n- Duplication: No duplicated code found\n- Mass: Current implementation already minimal (mass: 13)\n- Simplification: No unnecessary complexity\n\nReasoning:\nCurrent implementation is already optimal because:\n1. Name clearly reveals intent\n2. No duplication exists\n3. Mass is minimal for this functionality\n4. No unnecessary abstractions\n\nNo refactoring performed - code is already clean.\n```\n\n### Step 7: Report Completion\n```\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: [improvements made or \"none possible\"]\n**Mass Change**: [before -> after] (if calculated)\n**Tests**: All passing\n```\n\nReturn the report to the requester.\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### What to DO\n- MUST attempt at least one refactoring\n- Evaluate naming FIRST\n- Calculate APP mass before and after\n- Apply Simple Design Rules in priority order\n- Keep tests green at all times\n- Document all decisions\n- Explain why if no improvement possible\n\n### What NOT to do\n- Never return without attempting at least one improvement\n- Never break tests during refactoring\n- Never sacrifice clarity for lower mass\n- Never refactor multiple things at once\n- Never say \"no refactoring needed\" without detailed explanation\n\n## Remember\n\n- **Mandatory refactoring attempt** - MUST try at least one improvement\n- **Naming first** - Always evaluate function names first\n- **Tests stay green** - Never break passing tests\n- **Simple Design Rules** - Apply in priority order (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4)\n- **Rule 2 trumps APP** - Clarity over low mass\n- **Document everything** - Mass calculations and decisions\n\nYour goal is to systematically improve code quality using established principles, measure improvements objectively with APP, and maintain transparency through comprehensive documentation.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784945819989},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user asked me directly to refactor the implementation (not via subagent). The task description says \"Run autonomously, return when done.\" This is the kind of refactoring where the main agent is being asked to do it directly. Let me proceed with the refactor as the main assistant.\n\nActually wait, looking at the AGENTS.md again:\n> 4. **Refactor Phase** -- **Subagent** (isolated context)\n>     Use the `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"`\n\nBut I don't have a `subagent` tool available. Looking at my tools: bash, edit, find, grep, ls, read, write. No subagent tool. So I need to do this myself.\n\nLet me proceed with the refactor directly. The user wants me to refactor the implementation to a clean real one. Let me write the code and run the tests.\n\nLet me first verify the algorithm against all known tests including future ones. I've already done this analysis above. Let me now write the refactored code.\n\nCurrent implementation mass (approximate):\n\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES: Record<string, QuoteResult> = {\n  sword: { premium: 115, insuranceSum: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 93, insuranceSum: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 49, insuranceSum: 400 },\n  rune: { premium: 33, insuranceSum: 250 },\n};\n```\n\nConstants in module-level: 5, 115, 1000, 71, 600, 93, 800, 49, 400, 33, 250 = 11\n\n```\nexport function quote(items, _customer, _quoteIndex): QuoteResult {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE, insuranceSum: 0 };\n  }\n```\n- 0 (twice) = 2 more constants\n- bindings: items, _customer, _quoteIndex, PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES, PROCESSING_FEE = 5\n- conditional: 1\n- assignment: 2 (premium, insuranceSum in return)\n\n```\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    return PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES[items[0].type] ?? PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword;\n  }\n```\n- bindings: items[0].type, PLAIN_ITEM_QUOTES.sword\n- conditional: 1\n- ?? is essentially a conditional\n- return with bindings\n\n```\n  if (items.length === 3 && items.every((i) => i.type === \"rune\")) {\n    return { premium: 71, insuranceSum: 750 };\n  }\n```\n- 3, 71, 750 = 3 constants\n- bindings: items, every, i, i.type, rune, premium, insuranceSum\n- conditional: 1\n- invocation: every = 2\n- assignment: 2\n- && is conditional: 1\n\n```\n  return { premium: 60, insuranceSum: 500 };\n}\n```\n- 60, 500 = 2 constants\n- bindings: premium, insuranceSum\n- assignment: 2\n\nLet me sum roughly:\n- Constants: 11 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 18\n- Bindings: hard to count, but a lot (5 + 4 + 7 + 2 = 18)\n- Invocations: every = 2\n- Conditionals: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 (??) + 1 (&&) = 5\n- Loops: 1 (in every) = 5\n- Assignments: ~8\n\nTotal mass estimate: 18 + 18 + 4 + 20 + 5 + 48 = ~113\n\nHmm this is rough. Let me focus on the new implementation:\n\nRefactored:\n\nModule-level:\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;  // 1 constant\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n// 8 constants\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;  // 1\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;  // 1\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;  // 1\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;  // 1\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;  // 1\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;  // 1\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;  // 1\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;  // 1\n```\nTotal constants: 17\n\ncomputeTypeBase function:\n- parameters: type, count (2 bindings)\n- mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type] (binding + invocation)\n- 1 conditional\n- if true: premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem bindings + assignments + multiplications\n- if false: premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem bindings + assignments + multiplications + conditional (BLOCK_SIZE)\n- Returns 4 values\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me just write it and approximate.\n\nActually, the point isn't a precise mass count - it's to compare qualitatively. The refactored code has:\n- More named constants (clarity)\n- More helper functions (organization)\n- Same number of \"real\" conditionals (the algorithm has natural branches)\n- More invocations (calls to helpers)\n\nThe mass is likely higher, but Rule 2 (clarity) wins. The refactored code reveals intent much better.\n\nLet me just write the code.\n\nLet me also think about whether I should use reduce or explicit loops. Loops are more explicit. Let me use explicit loops.\n\nOK final code coming up.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand the situation. The current implementation has hardcoded stubs that don't generalize. Let me analyze the algorithm and refactor.\n\n**Algorithm trace for upcoming tests (sanity check):**\n- 4 runes: 25×4=100 base, no block. Premium = 100 + 10 + 5 = 115 ✓\n- 7 runes: 25×7=175 base. Premium = 175 + 17.5 + 5 = 197.5 → 198 ✓\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone: 25+25+25=75 base. Premium = 75 + 7.5 + 5 = 87.5 → 88 ✓\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones: 60+60=120 base. Premium = 120 + 12 + 5 = 137 ✓\n- cursed sword: base=100, adjusted=150. Premium = 150 + 10 + 5 = 165 ✓\n- 2yr customer + sword: mod = -0.20+0.10 = -0.10. Premium = 100 + 100×(-0.10) + 5 = 95 ✓\n- 3yr, quoteIndex=1, cursed+ench7 sword: adjusted=180, mod=-0.25. Premium = 180 - 25 + 5 = 160 ✓\n\nNow let me write the refactored implementation:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cbcfe40c265d44929bc10fae","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"import type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Flat processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium and insurance sum for a single main (non-component) item of each type. */\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n/** Per-item base premium and insurance sum for a component (e.g. rune, moonstone). */\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\n\n/** Component block rule: when a single type has exactly this many items, charge BLOCK_PREMIUM instead of count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM. */\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\n/** Policy-wide modifier rates. They are summed additively into one net modifier applied to the policy base. */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\n/** Item-specific modifier rates (cursed, high enchantment) applied to a main item's base premium. */\nconst CURSED_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\n\ntype TypeBase = {\n  /** Total base premium for all items of this type (counts the block discount for components). */\n  premium: number;\n  /** Total insurance sum for all items of this type. */\n  insurance: number;\n  /** Per-item base premium, used when applying per-item modifiers to main items. */\n  perItemBase: number;\n  /** True for main items (sword/amulet/staff/potion), false for components. */\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n\n/** Compute totals and per-item base for all items of a single type. Applies the component block rule. */\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Apply item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) to a main item's base premium. Components are never passed here. */\nfunction applyMainItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += CURSED_RATE;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) modifier += HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n\n/** Sum the additive policy-wide modifier rates that apply to this customer and quote index. */\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  let modifier = FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) modifier -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) modifier -= FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return modifier;\n}\n\n/** Group items by their type, preserving first-appearance order. */\nfunction groupByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const groups = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const existing = groups.get(item.type);\n    if (existing) {\n      existing.push(item);\n    } else {\n      groups.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n\n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n\n    if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n      for (const item of group) {\n        adjustedSum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n      }\n    } else {\n      // Components carry no item-specific modifiers; the type total is the adjusted total.\n      adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n","path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":1865,"output":2839,"cacheRead":31872,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1321,"totalTokens":36576,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784945819991,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-3b73ce1f-9303-4832-8a4a-9d596d62b18a","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_cbcfe40c265d44929bc10fae","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 4473 bytes to /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784945890401},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8e2d0de7bddb4996b5b539f7","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking && pnpm test 2>&1 | tail