\n};\n\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Damage[];\n  };\n};\n\nexport type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\nexport type Scenario = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: Item[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type Result = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784946069966},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All 12 tests pass. Let me now think about refactoring.\n\nLooking at the code, I want to evaluate it more carefully:\n\n1. **Naming Evaluation**:\n   - `quote` - clear, fits\n   - `computeTypeBase` - good\n   - `applyMainItemModifiers` - clear\n   - `computePolicyModifier` - clear\n   - `groupByType` - clear\n\n2. **App Mass Analysis** of current `quote.ts`:\n\nLet me count components carefully:\n- Constants (literals): Let me list...\n  - In MAIN_ITEM_BASES: 4 entries × 2 numbers = 8 literals\n  - PROCESSING_FEE = 5: 1\n  - COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25: 1\n  - COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250: 1\n  - BLOCK_SIZE = 3: 1\n  - BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60: 1\n  - FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10: 1\n  - LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20: 1\n  - LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2: 1\n  - FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15: 1\n  - CURSED_RATE = 0.5: 1\n  - HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3: 1\n  - HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5: 1\n  - Total constants: ~21 literals\n\n- Bindings: \n  - In `computeTypeBase`: type, count, mainBase = 3\n  - In `applyMainItemModifiers`: item, basePremium, modifier = 3\n  - In `computePolicyModifier`: customer, quoteIndex, modifier = 3\n  - In `groupByType`: items, groups, item, existing = 4\n  - In `quote`: items, customer, quoteIndex, policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum, type, group, typeBase, policyModifier, premium = 11\n  - TypeBase fields: premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem = 4\n  - All the constants = ~13\n  - Total bindings: ~39\n\n- Invocations:\n  - MAIN_ITEM_BASES access: 1 (in computeTypeBase)\n  - computeTypeBase call: 1\n  - applyMainItemModifiers calls: 1 per main item (loops so depends on count)\n  - + operations: many\n  - groupByType: 1\n  - computePolicyModifier: 1\n  - Map operations: groups.get, groups.set, etc.\n  - Math.ceil: 1\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me focus on the actual structural improvements rather than micro-optimizing mass.\n\n3. **Improvements I can think of**:\n\na. The `quote` function has this dual-loop structure that could be simplified. Let me see if there's a way to make it cleaner.\n\nb. The `TypeBase` type has both `premium` (total) and `perItemBase` (single item). For components, `perItemBase` is just `COMPONENT_PREMIUM`, but the total premium uses the block rule. We could potentially remove `perItemBase` from `TypeBase` and just pass the appropriate value when iterating.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n`perItemBase` is `COMPONENT_PREMIUM` for components, but the actual per-item premium for components depends on whether the block rule applies. So if we had a single component, per-item premium is 25. If 3 components, total is 60 (so per-item is 20). If 4 components, total is 100 (so per-item is 25).\n\nThis means we can't just use `perItemBase` for components without applying the block rule somehow. So the `isMainItem` distinction is necessary.\n\nHmm, can I simplify by extracting `perItemBase` from `TypeBase`? The only use is `applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase)`. We could just pass the main item's per-item base directly.\n\nLet me see... actually, we could return different shapes. Or we could compute the per-item base outside.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach might be:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nAnd then in `quote`:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n  const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n\n  if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n    const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n    const perItemBase = mainBase.premium;\n    for (const item of group) {\n      adjustedSum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, perItemBase);\n    }\n  } else {\n    adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut this requires re-looking up `MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type]` in the main loop, which is a bit awkward. Let me think differently.\n\nWhat if `TypeBase` includes the per-item base for main items only?\n\n```typescript\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n  perItemBase: number | null;  // null for components\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```typescript\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n} & (\n  | { kind: \"main\"; perItemBase: number }\n  | { kind: \"component\" }\n);\n```\n\nThis uses a discriminated union to make the type safer. But it's more complex.\n\nActually, the simplest cleanup might be