ype-total (which already accounts for block rule) added directly\n\nWhat about making `applyMainItemModifiers` also handle the per-item base for components? No, that's weird because components don't have modifiers.\n\nLet me try another angle: maybe simplify `groupByType` to use `Object.groupBy` if available. Or use a more functional approach.\n\nActually, looking at the `groupByType` function, it's pretty simple. It could be:\n\n```typescript\nfunction groupByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  return items.reduce((groups, item) => {\n    const existing = groups.get(item.type);\n    if (existing) {\n      existing.push(item);\n    } else {\n      groups.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n    return groups;\n  }, new Map<string, Item[]>());\n}\n```\n\nBut that's not really cleaner.\n\nOK let me focus on what might be the most impactful cleanup:\n\n**Idea: Use reduce in the main loop**\n\n```typescript\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  const { policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum } = Array.from(groupByType(items)).reduce(\n    (acc, [type, group]) => {\n      const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n      acc.policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n      acc.insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n      if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n        for (const item of group) {\n          acc.adjustedSum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n        }\n      } else {\n        acc.adjustedSum += typeBase.premium;\n      }\n      return acc;\n    },\n    { policyBase: 0, insuranceSum: 0, adjustedSum: 0 },\n  );\n\n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nThis doesn't really improve clarity, just changes the syntax.\n\n**Idea: Lift common computations**\n\nWhat if we always pass a \"type total\" to a function that knows how to compute per-item adjustments?\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase: TypeBase, group: Item[]): number {\n  if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n    return group.reduce((sum, item) => sum + applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase), 0);\n  }\n  return typeBase.premium;\n}\n```\n\nThen the main loop becomes:\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n  const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n  adjustedSum += computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n}\n```\n\nThis extracts a concept nicely.\n\n**Idea: Make TypeBase more focused**\n\nWhat if TypeBase is split into two concepts?\n\nActually, let me think about whether `policyBase` and `adjustedSum` need to be separate. Looking at the formula:\n\n```\npremium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE\n```\n\nWhere:\n- `policyBase` = sum of base premiums (unmodified)\n- `adjustedSum` = sum of premiums after item-specific modifiers\n- `policyModifier` is applied to `policyBase` only\n\nThis is a real distinction. For main items:\n- adjustedSum contribution = sum of (per-item base * (1 + item modifiers))\n- policyBase contribution = per-item base * count\n\nFor components:\n- adjustedSum contribution = total premium (with block rule)\n- policyBase contribution = total premium (with block rule)\n\nSo they're different.\n\nWait, let me re-check. For main items:\n```typescript\nfor (const item of group) {\n  adjustedSum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n}\n```\nThis sums each item's modified premium.\n\n`policyBase += typeBase.premium` adds the total UNMODIFIED base.\n\nSo for a cursed sword: adjustedSum = 150, policyBase = 100.\n\nFor a regular sword: adjustedSum = 100, policyBase = 100.\n\nFor 3 runes (block): adjustedSum = 60, policyBase = 60.\n\nFor 4 runes: adjustedSum = 100, policyBase = 100.\n\nOK so the names \"adjustedSum\" and \"policyBase\" are reasonable, just different concepts.\n\n**Most impactful refactorings**:\n\n1. Extract `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` - reduces nesting in main loop\n2. Rename `adjustedSum` to something clearer like `itemAdjustedSum` (since `policyBase` is also adjusted, just by the policy modifier)\n\nLet me also think about whether there's any DRY issue. Looking at the code... not really. The structure is clean.\n\nHmm, one thing I notice: the constants section has a lot of declarations. They're all clearly named and grouped. That's fine.\n\nLet me think about whether the comment headers (JSDoc) are useful. They explain intent well. Keep them.\n\n**Plan**:\n1. Extract `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` function to reduce nesting in the main loop.\n2. Consider renaming `adjustedSum` to `itemAdjustedSum` for clarity.\n\nLet me also think: is `applyMainItemModifiers` actually doing the right thing? Let me trace through:\n\n```typescript\nfunction applyMainItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) modifier += CURSED_RATE;\n  if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) modifier += HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE;\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nFor a sword with enchantment 7, cursed:\n- modifier = 0.5 + 0.3 = 0.8\n- return 100 * 1.8 = 180\n\nTest (future): \"cursed sword with enchantment 7 (0 years, 1st quote) -> premium 195 G\"\n- adjustedSum = 180\n- policyBase = 100\n- policyModifier = 0.10\n- premium = 180 + 100 * 0.10 + 5 = 195 ✓\n\nFor cursed sword (enchant 0 or undefined):\n- modifier = 0.5\n- return 100 * 1.5 = 150\n\nTest (future): \"cursed sword (0 years, 1st quote, integration example 1) -> premium 165 G\"\n- adjustedSum = 150\n- policyBase = 100\n- premium = 150 + 10 + 5 = 165 ✓\n\nOK looks correct.\n\n**Let me now make the refactoring**:\n\n1. Extract `computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group)` - clearer intent, reduces nesting in main loop