hat.\n\nLet me also think about whether there are any other issues:\n\n1. The constants section has good naming and JSDoc. Don't change.\n2. The `TypeBase` type is reasonable.\n3. The helper functions are well-named.\n4. The main `quote` function is clear.\n\nThe only issue I see is the nested loop + conditional in the main function. Extracting that would be a clear win.\n\nLet me make this refactoring now.\n\nActually, one more thought: should I refactor `computePolicyModifier`?\n\n```typescript\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  let modifier = FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  if (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) modifier -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  if (quoteIndex > 0) modifier -= FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return modifier;\n}\n```\n\nThis is clear enough. The comment explains the additive nature. Fine as-is.\n\nOK, let me apply the refactoring.\n\nWait, before I do that, let me reconsider naming. The current name is `computePolicyModifier`. It returns the additive modifier. The \"modifier\" name is fine.\n\nIn the main function:\n- `policyBase` = sum of base premiums\n- `insuranceSum` = sum of insurance values\n- `adjustedSum` = sum of adjusted premiums (with item modifiers)\n\nThese are good names. I'll keep them.\n\nActually, let me look once more at the whole picture. The flow is:\n1. Group items by type\n2. For each type, compute typeBase (premium + insurance totals)\n3. Sum policyBase, insuranceSum\n4. Compute adjustedSum (with item modifiers for main items)\n5. Compute policyModifier (based on customer + quote index)\n6. Final premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE\n\nStep 3 and 4 both iterate over types but compute different things. Could we combine? No, because they need different processing.\n\nOK let me just do the extraction.\n\nActually wait, let me think about another angle. What if we compute the per-item premium for components as `BLOCK_PREMIUM / count` when