refactoring.\n\nWait, one more thought: the function `applyMainItemModifiers` returns the modified premium. The name is OK but maybe `applyItemModifiersToPremium` is more precise? Hmm, \"item modifiers\" is the right level of abstraction.\n\nLet me proceed with the extraction.\n\nPlan:\n1. Extract `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` (or similar name) from the main loop\n2. The main loop becomes simpler and more declarative\n\nLet me also reconsider what to name the extracted function. Options:\n- `sumAdjustedPremium` - clear about what it does\n- `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` - emphasizes the type-level abstraction\n- `adjustedPremiumForType` - reads as a value-getter\n\nI'll go with `sumAdjustedPremium` since it's a sum.\n\nActually wait. The result isn't quite \"adjusted premium\" for the type. For main items, it's the sum of per-item adjusted premiums (with item modifiers). For components, it's the total premium (with block rule). So the \"adjusted\" here is ambiguous.\n\nLet me name it `sumTypePremium` or similar.\n\nActually, let me look at the existing terminology:\n- `typeBase.premium` - the base premium total for the type\n- The adjusted version applies item-specific modifiers\n\nSo `sumTypeAdjustedPremium` or `adjustedTypePremium`?\n\nLet me just call it `sumAdjustedPremiumForType` to be explicit. Or maybe `typeAdjustedPremium`.\n\nHmm, let me just call it `sumAdjustedPremium` and add a clear comment.\n\nOK let me write the refactored code:\n\n```typescript\n/** Sum the adjusted premium for one type. Main items get per-item modifiers applied; components use the type total (which already accounts for the block rule). */\nfunction sumAdjustedPremium(typeBase: TypeBase, group: Item[]): number {\n  if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n    let sum = 0;\n    for (const item of group) {\n      sum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n    }\n    return sum;\n  }\n  return typeBase.premium;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n\n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    adjustedSum += sumAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n  }\n\n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nThis is cleaner.\n\nNow, before I do this, let me think about the `TypeBase` type once more. It has 4 fields:\n- `premium` - total\n- `insurance` - total\n- `perItemBase` - only used for main items\n- `isMainItem` - boolean flag\n\nA cleaner type might be:\n\n```typescript\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premium: number;\n  insurance: number;\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n  perItemBase?: number;  // only present for main items\n};\n```\n\nOr use a discriminated union:\n\n```typescript\ntype TypeBase = \n  | { premium: number; insurance: number; perItemBase: number; isMainItem: true }\n  | { premium: number; insurance: number; isMainItem: false };\n```\n\nThis would force correct usage at compile time. But it's more verbose.\n\nLet me think about which is cleaner. The current `TypeBase` type is simple but has redundancy. The discriminated union is type-safe but verbose.\n\nActually, looking at `computeTypeBase`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    is