nHmm, but rule 4 says \"Fewest Elements\" - minimize code elements. Adding a function adds an element. Is it worth it?\n\nYes, because:\n- Rule 2 (clarity) trumps Rule 4\n- The extraction makes the main loop easier to read\n- The extracted function has a clear name that explains its purpose\n\nOK applying now.\n\nWait, I also want to think about whether the `quote` function should use `reduce` instead of `for...of` for the outer loop. That would be:\n\n```typescript\nconst { policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum } = Array.from(groupByType(items)).reduce(\n  (acc, [type, group]) => {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    acc.policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    acc.insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    acc.adjustedSum += sumAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n    return acc;\n  },\n  { policyBase: 0, insuranceSum: 0, adjustedSum: 0 },\n);\n```\n\nThis is more functional but more verbose. The for loop is more concise. Keep for loop.\n\nOK, applying now.\n\nLet me also think: should I combine `policyBase` and `adjustedSum` somehow? They're summed in the final formula:\n\n```typescript\nconst premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n```\n\nCould rewrite as:\n```typescript\nconst premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n```\n\nNo, this is already concise. Keep as is.\n\nAlright, applying the refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_677ed312f1ac4cd69f0bb5a7","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":50,"offset":1,"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":525,"output":9615,"cacheRead":10752,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":9524,"totalTokens":20892,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp