nction** - The conditional `if (item.cursed) modifier += CURSED_RATE; if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) modifier += HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE;` could be expressed more concisely. We could extract these as a list of conditions.\n\n2. **`computeTypeBase`** - The function returns `isMainItem` flag to differentiate behavior later. This is a sign that the abstraction is a bit leaky. Maybe the main vs component distinction could be cleaner.\n\n3. **`groupByType`** - Has a `for...of` loop with manual map management. Could use a more concise approach. But for-of with proper structure is probably the cleanest TypeScript.\n\n4. **`computeTypeAdjustedPremium`** - Has an `if` branch for main items vs components. This is a sign of the abstraction leak mentioned above.\n\n5. **`applyMainItemModifiers`** has two separate if statements. This could potentially be cleaner with an array of modifiers.\n\nLet me think about what to refactor:\n\n### Option 1: Use a list of modifiers in applyMainItemModifiers\n\n```typescript\nfunction applyMainItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  const modifiers = [\n    item.cursed ? CURSED_RATE : 0,\n    item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE : 0,\n  ];\n  const modifier = modifiers.reduce((sum, m) => sum + m, 0);\n  return basePremium * (1 + modifier);\n}\n```\n\nBut this adds mass, not reduces it. The original is more efficient.\n\n### Option 2: Use a list of modifier predicates\n\nCould define modifiers as a list of functions that return true/false and add a rate:\n```typescript\nconst MAIN_ITEM_MODIFIERS: Array<{ rate: number; applies: (item: Item) => boolean }> = [\n  { rate: CURSED_RATE, applies: (item) => item.cursed === true },\n  { rate: HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE, applies: (item) => item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD },\n];\n```\n\nThis is conceptually cleaner but add