t >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE : 0);\n  return basePremium * (1 + bonus);\n}\n```\n\nBut this loses the explanatory bindings. Rule 2 trumps mass.\n\nOK let me go with the named bindings version.\n\nSame for `computePolicyModifier`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = quoteIndex > 0 ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  return FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n}\n```\n\nThis is clearer than the mutation-based version.\n\nAlright, let me make these changes.\n\nActually one more thought: should I just leave the code alone? The user has been working through tests one at a time and the code is already fairly clean. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe user task says \"Refactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Run autonomously, return when done.\"\n\nThe implementation has clear naming, well-organized functions, and good comments. But there's still the mutation-based pattern in two functions that could be cleaner. Plus, the mass can be reduced.\n\nOK let me proceed with the refactorings.\n\nLet me also think about whether there are any other improvements I'm missing.\n\nLooking at `quote.ts` again:\n- All function names are clear\n- Constants are well-named\n- Comments explain the purpose of each constant\n- The main flow is clear: group items, compute per-type bases, apply modifiers, sum up, apply policy modifier, add processing fee\n- The helper functions are well-named and focused\n\nThe main improvements are the two mutation-based functions. Let me proceed.\n\nWait, I want to also consider the order of constants. They're grouped:\n- PROCESSING_FEE\n- MAIN_ITEM_BASES\n- COMPONENT_PREMIUM, COMPONENT_INSURANCE\n- BLOCK_SIZE, BLOCK_PREMIUM\n- FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE, LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE, LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS, FOLLOW_UP_DISC