number {\n","firstChangedLine":49},"isError":false,"timestamp":1784946806656},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, I need to think more carefully. The current implementation treats unknown types as components. But the spec says unknown types in quote should throw.\n\nBut what if I have a quote with both a sword and an unknown type? Should the unknown throw immediately, or only if it's the only item?\n\nThe spec says: \"quote includes an item with an unknown type (e.g. {type: \"broomstick\"}) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\".\n\nSo ANY item with an unknown type causes an error. My implementation handles this correctly - if the unknown type is in items, it'll throw.\n\nBut what about checking the component rule? An unknown type that's a single item (no block) should still throw. My current implementation does that.\n\nActually wait, the spec says components like runes and moonstones are valid. So unknown types like broomstick should throw, but runes and moonstones are fine.\n\nThe issue is: my current implementation has the check structure that says \"if count == 3, block; else if count >= 1, individual; else throw\". This means a single unknown item would NOT throw because count >= 1 is true.\n\nThat's wrong! A single \"broomstick\" item should throw.\n\nLet me think about this. The spec distinguishes:\n- Known main items: sword, amulet, staff, potion\n- Known components: rune, moonstone, etc.\n- Unknown types: should throw\n\nHow do I know what's a component vs. an unknown type? I have a list of known main items, but the spec doesn't list all known components. The spec says \"Components — for example runes and moonstones — are insured at 250 G each\".\n\nHmm. Looking at the JSON schema: \"description\": \"sword | amulet | staff | potion | rune | moonstone\"\n\nSo the schema lists sword, amulet, staff, potion, rune, moonstone as the valid types. Anything else is unknown.\n\nSo I need a list of known types, and anything else