"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All 21 tests pass. Now let me apply the refactoring process.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\nLet me look at each function name:\n- `quote` - the main exported function, takes items + customer + quoteIndex and returns a QuoteResult. The name is fine.\n- `computeTypeBase` - computes the TypeBase for a given type and count. Name is descriptive.\n- `applyMainItemModifiers` - applies per-item modifiers to a main item's base premium. Clear.\n- `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` - sum of adjusted premium for all items of one type. Clear.\n- `computePolicyModifier` - sums the additive policy-wide modifier rates. Clear.\n- `groupByType` - groups items by type, preserving first-appearance order. Clear.\n\nThe names look good. Let me evaluate constants:\n- `PROCESSING_FEE = 5` - good\n- `MAIN_ITEM_BASES` - good\n- `COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25` - good\n- `COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250` - good\n- `BLOCK_SIZE = 3` - good\n- `BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60` - good\n- `KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES` - good\n- `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10` - good\n- `LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20` - good\n- `LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2` - good\n- `FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15` - good\n- `CURSED_RATE = 0.5` - good\n- `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3` - good\n- `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5` - good\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\nLet me carefully count the components in `quote.ts`:\n\nConstants:\n- 5 (PROCESSING_FEE) - 1\n- 100, 1000, 60, 600, 80, 800, 40, 400 (MAIN_ITEM_BASES values) - 8 (actually 8 numbers, but as an object literal, each value is a constant)\n- 25 (COMPONENT_PREMIUM) - 1\n- 250 (COMPONENT_INSURANCE) - 1\n- 3 (BLOCK_SIZE) - 1\n- 60 (BLOCK_PREMIUM) - 1\n- \"rune\", \"moonstone\" (KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES values) - 2\n- 0.10 (FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) - 1\n- 0.20 (LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE) - 1\n- 2 (LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS) - 1\n- 0.15 (FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE) - 1\n- 0.5 (CURSED_RATE) - 1\n- 0.3 (HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE) - 1\n- 5 (HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) - 1\n- type/Item/Customer/QuoteResult strings - maybe don't count\n\nTotal constants: ~21\n\nBindings:\n- QuoteResult type - not counted (type annotation)\n- PROCESSING_FEE - 1\n- MAIN_ITEM_BASES - 1\n- COMPONENT_PREMIUM - 1\n- COMPONENT_INSURANCE - 1\n- BLOCK_SIZE - 1\n- BLOCK_PREMIUM - 1\n- KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES - 1\n- FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE - 1\n- LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE - 1\n- LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS - 1\n- FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE - 1\n- CURSED_RATE - 1\n- HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE - 1\n- HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD - 1\n- TypeBase type - not counted\n- computeTypeBase function - 1\n- type, count (params) - 2\n- mainBase, premium, insurance, perItemBase, isMainItem (in return) - 5\n- existing groups, item (in groupByType) - 3\n- applyMainItemModifiers function - 1\n- item, basePremium (params) - 2\n- cursedBonus, highEnchantmentBonus - 2\n- computeTypeAdjustedPremium function - 1\n- typeBase, group (params) - 2\n- sum - 1\n- computePolicyModifier function - 1\n- customer, quoteIndex (params) - 2\n- loyaltyDiscount, followUpDiscount - 2\n- groupByType function - 1\n- items (param) - 1\n- groups - 1\n- quote function - 1\n- items, customer, quoteIndex (params of quote) - 3\n- policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum - 3\n- type, group (destructured) - 2\n- typeBase - 1\n- policyModifier - 1\n- premium - 1\n\nThat's a lot of bindings, let me just focus on the high-level structure.\n\nActually, instead of trying to calculate APP mass to extreme detail, let me focus on identifying potential refactorings and assessing whether they reduce mass while improving clarity.\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 1: Tests Pass\nAll 21 tests pass. ✓\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\nLet me look for opportunities:\n1. `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` has an `if (typeBase.isMainItem)` check, then in the false branch just returns `typeBase.premium`. The function is a bit awkward because the \"adjusted premium\" for components is just the unadjusted total premium.\n\n2. `computeTypeBase` returns a TypeBase which has both `premium` (which represents the base premium total for the type, possibly block-adjusted) and `perItemBase` (which is the per-item base premium). For components, the premium is already block-adjusted. For main items, premium is just count * base.\n\n3. `quote` has three accumulator variables (policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum). The policyBase is used both as part of the type's premium and then scaled by policyModifier. This is a bit hard to follow.\n\n4. The relationship between `premium` in TypeBase and the \"adjustedSum\" calculation could be clearer.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n- Not really seeing obvious duplication.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- The three accumulators in `quote` could perhaps be consolidated.\n- The `TypeBase` shape has 4 fields. Is `isMainItem` needed or can we infer it?\n\nLet me think more carefully. The flow in `quote` is:\n```\nfor each type group:\n  typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, count)\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance\n  adjustedSum += computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group)\n```\n\n`typeBase.premium` is the type's base premium (with block adjustment for components).\n`computeTypeAdjustedPremium` for main items returns the sum of per-item adjusted premiums; for components it returns `typeBase.premium` (the block-adjusted total).\n\nSo we have:\n- policyBase = sum of all base premiums (block-adjusted for components)\n- insuranceSum = sum of all insurance\n- adjustedSum = for main items: sum of per-item adjusted premiums; for components: block-adjusted premium total\n\nThe total premium formula is:\n`adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE`\n\nWhere:\n- adjustedSum contains: per-item-adjusted premiums for main items + block-adjusted total for components\n- policyBase * policyModifier applies the modifier to all base premiums\n- This gives: per-item-adjusted + modifier*all-base-premiums + fee\n\nWait, but for components, the block-adjusted premium is what we want to charge (it's not a \"base\" to be further modified). So including it in `policyBase` and then multiplying by `policyModifier` is double-applying the modifier!\n\nHmm wait, let me reread the test for 3 runes:\n```\nit(\"3 runes (block applies) -> premium 71 G\", () => {\n  const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n  expect(\n    quote(\n      [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }],\n      customer,\n      0,\n    ),\n  ).toEqual({\n    premium: 71,\n    insuranceSum: 750,\n  });\n});\n```\n\n3 runes: block premium = 60, insurance = 750\nWith first insurance rate 0.10, no other modifiers, processing fee 5:\n- adjustedSum (components) = 60 (block-adjusted total)\n- policyBase = 60 (block-adjusted total)\n- premium = 60 + 60*0.10 + 5 = 60 + 6 + 5 = 71 ✓\n\nOK so for components, policyBase is the same as adjustedSum (both are the block-adjusted total), so the modifier effectively applies once to the component total.\n\nFor single rune (no block): \n- block-adjusted total = 25 (no block, so just 1 * 25 = 25)\n- adjustedSum = 25\n- policyBase = 25\n- premium = 25 + 25*0.10 + 5 = 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 → ceil = 33 ✓\n\nFor 7 runes (no block): \n- block-adjusted total = 7 * 25 = 175\n- adjustedSum = 175\n- policyBase = 175\n- premium = 175 + 175*0.10 + 5 = 175 + 17.5 + 5 = 197.5 → ceil = 198 ✓\n\nOK so the model is consistent. For components, the \"base\" IS the block-adjusted total.\n\nNow let me see if there's a clearer way to express this.\n\nActually, thinking about it more, the design has a subtle semantic issue:\n- For main items: \"base premium\" means per-item base (e.g. 100 for sword), then per-item modifiers apply, giving per-item-adjusted premium\n- For components: \"base premium\" means block-adjusted total\n\nThe `TypeBase.premium` is actually different for the two cases:\n- For main items: count * per-item-base (without modifiers)\n- For components: block-adjusted total\n\nFor policyBase calculation, we sum these. Then policyBase * policyModifier scales all of them.\n\nFor adjustedSum, we further modify each main item, but components just use the block-adjusted total as-is.\n\nSo for main items: final contribution = (adjusted premium sum) + (base premium total) * policyModifier\nFor components: final contribution = (block-adjusted total) + (block-adjusted total) * policyModifier = block-adjusted * (1 + policyModifier)\n\nThis means main items and components behave differently with respect to the modifier. For components, the block-adjusted total is counted twice (once via adjustedSum, once via policyBase).\n\nWait, actually no. Let me re-examine. The formula is:\n`adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE`\n\nFor a single rune:\n- adjustedSum = 25 (block-adjusted = 1*25)\n- policyBase = 25 (same)\n- policyModifier = 0.10\n- premium = 25 + 25*0.10 + 5 = 32.5 → ceil = 33 ✓\n\nFor 3 runes:\n- adjustedSum = 60 (block applies)\n- policyBase = 60\n- policyModifier = 0.10\n- premium = 60 + 60*0.10 + 5 = 71 ✓\n\nSo the block-adjusted premium is added as both adjustedSum and policyBase. The block-adjusted premium effectively gets multiplied by (1 + policyModifier). That's intentional - the modifier applies to the component's total just like for main items.\n\nBut wait, for main items:\nFor single sword:\n- adjustedSum = 115 (1 sword, no modifiers, so 100 * 1.15 = 115, where 1.15 = 1 + 0.10 + 0.05 - 0 = 1.15 from first insurance 0.10... wait no)\n\nWait, let me look at the code again. `applyMainItemModifiers` only handles `cursed` and `highEnchantment`. It does NOT handle `policyModifier`.\n\nFor single sword (no curse, no enchantment, 0 years, 1st quote):\n- perItemBase = 100 (from MAIN_ITEM_BASES)\n- per-item adjusted = 100 * (1 + 0 + 0) = 100 (since no cursed/high enchantment)\n- adjustedSum = 100 (1 item)\n- policyBase = 100 (1 sword, premium total)\n- policyModifier = 0.10 - 0 - 0 = 0.10\n- premium = 100 + 100*0.10 + 5 = 100 + 10 + 5 = 115 ✓\n\nOK so for main items, `applyMainItemModifiers` does NOT apply policyModifier, only per-item modifiers (cursed, enchantment). Then policyBase * policyModifier scales all items (main + component) by the policy modifier.\n\nSo the structure is:\n1. Each item has a per-item-adjusted premium (cursed/enchantment applied)\n2. policyBase is the sum of base premiums for all items\n3. policyModifier (first insurance + loyalty discount + follow-up discount) is applied to policyBase\n4. Processing fee added\n5. Total = sum(per-item-adjusted) + policyBase*policyModifier + fee\n\nFor components, there's no per-item modifier, so \"per-item-adjusted\" equals \"block-adjusted\" which equals \"base\" effectively.\n\nHmm, this seems consistent. But the asymmetry between main items (per-item modifiers in adjustedSum, no policy modifier) and components (no per-item modifiers, but block adjustment and policy modifier) is interesting.\n\nActually wait - I think there's an even simpler conceptualization. For main items, each item has a \"base premium\" (100 for sword) and can have per-item modifiers (cursed +50%, high enchantment +30%). For components, each item has a \"base premium\" (25 for rune/moonstone) and blocks of 3 get a discount (60 instead of 75).\n\nSo really:\n- Main items: premium = base * per-item-modifiers * count\n- Components: premium = block-adjusted total\n\nThe current structure uses `TypeBase.premium` to capture the block-adjusted total for components and count*base for main items. Then `adjustedSum` either applies per-item modifiers (main items) or uses the block-adjusted total as-is (components).\n\nThen policyBase*policyModifier adds the policy-wide modifier to everything.\n\nOK so the model is: each item contributes (its base premium) to policyBase. Then policyBase is scaled by the policy modifier. Then per-item modifiers are added on top of main items (and component block adjustments replace their base premium contribution).\n\nHmm, the design has some quirks. Let me see if I can simplify without breaking tests.\n\nOne idea: refactor so `policyBase` is the per-item base * count (no block adjustment), and then for components we use the block-adjusted premium in the `adjustedSum`/`premium` calculation differently.\n\nWait, let me look at this carefully:\n\nFor 3 runes:\n- count*base = 3 * 25 = 75\n- block-adjusted = 60\n- Difference: 60 vs 75\n\nCurrently, the block-adjusted 60 is used in both adjustedSum and policyBase. So we get 60 + 60*0.10 = 66 from runes + 5 fee = 71.\n\nIf we used the unadjusted 75 in policyBase, we'd get 60 + 75*0.10 = 67.5, which is wrong.\n\nSo the current design (using block-adjusted total in policyBase) is intentional and correct.\n\nAlternative formulation: for components, the block-adjusted premium is the final premium (no per-item modifier). For main items, each item contributes its base premium (no block adjustment needed since there's only 1 per \"block\" in main items).\n\nSo really the structure is:\n- For each item of each type, compute a \"type premium\" (base, with block adjustment for components)\n- Sum all type premiums into policyBase\n- Apply per-item modifiers to main items (cursed, enchantment) to get an \"adjustment delta\" added to policyBase\n- Wait, but it's done as adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier, not adjustedDelta + policyBase * (1 + policyModifier)\n\nHmm. Let me re-examine:\n\nFor cursed sword:\n- per-item base = 100\n- per-item adjusted = 100 * 1.5 = 150 (cursed)\n- adjustedSum = 150\n- policyBase = 100\n- policyModifier = 0.10\n- premium = 150 + 100*0.10 + 5 = 150 + 10 + 5 = 165 ✓\n\nOK so for a main item with a per-item modifier, the premium contribution is:\n- per-item-adjusted = base * (1 + per-item-modifier)\n- plus base * policyModifier\n\nThis is equivalent to:\n- base + base*per-item-modifier + base*policyModifier\n- = base * (1 + per-item-modifier + policyModifier)\n\nSo actually, you could think of it as: the base premium is fully counted, plus modifiers.\n\nFor a component (no per-item modifier, possibly block-adjusted):\n- per-item-adjusted = block-adjusted total (this IS the premium contribution)\n- policyBase contribution = same block-adjusted total\n- Total contribution = block-adjusted + block-adjusted * policyModifier\n- = block-adjusted * (1 + policyModifier)\n\nHmm, that's different from main items. For main items, the policy modifier is applied once to the base. For components, the policy modifier is applied to the block-adjusted total.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully, I wonder if the intent is:\n- policyBase represents the \"policy base premium\" which is what gets scaled by policy-wide modifiers\n- per-item modifiers are applied on top of that\n\nFor main items, policyBase is sum of base premiums (no block adjustment since no blocks for main items).\nFor components, policyBase is the block-adjusted total.\n\nThen the formula is: per-item-adjusted-premiums + policyBase * policyModifier + fee\n\nFor components, per-item-adjusted-premium = block-adjusted total (since no per-item modifiers).\nFor main items, per-item-adjusted-premium = sum of base * per-item-modifier per item.\n\nSo the implementation is consistent: each type's \"policyBase contribution\" is the type's premium (block-adjusted for components, count*base for main items). And adjustedSum is the per-item modifier sum (zero for components since no per-item modifiers, but block-adjusted total is just the \"premium\" for components).\n\nOK this is a bit subtle but I think the implementation is correct and reasonable. Let me focus on refactoring opportunities.\n\n## Potential Refactorings\n\n1. The `TypeBase.premium` field has dual semantics depending on `isMainItem`:\n   - For main items: it's count * base (no modifiers)\n   - For components: it's block-adjusted total\n   \n   This dual meaning makes the code a bit hard to follow.\n\n2. The `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` function:\n   - Returns typeBase.premium for components\n   - Returns per-item-adjusted sum for main items\n   \n   It would be cleaner to either:\n   - Always return per-item-adjusted sum (and components happen to have all zero modifiers, so it equals the block-adjusted total)\n   - Or split into two functions\n\n3. The three accumulators (policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum) could potentially be consolidated into a single struct.\n\n4. The `isMainItem` flag in TypeBase could potentially be inferred from whether the type is in MAIN_ITEM_BASES or KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES.\n\nLet me think about #2 more carefully. What if we always returned the per-item adjusted sum?\n- For main items: sum over items of base * per-item-modifier\n- For components: each component has no per-item modifier, so sum is 0. But we want it to be the block-adjusted total!\n\nSo that doesn't work directly. Unless we change the formula.\n\nAlternative: for components, add the block-adjusted total as the \"per-item adjustment\" so that adjustedSum correctly captures the block adjustment.\n\nActually, an even simpler approach: split TypeBase into:\n- `policyBaseContribution` (what gets added to policyBase)\n- `perItemAdjustedPremium` (what gets added to adjustedSum)\n\nFor main items: \n- policyBaseContribution = count * base\n- perItemAdjustedPremium = sum of base * per-item-modifier\n\nFor components:\n- policyBaseContribution = block-adjusted total\n- perItemAdjustedPremium = block-adjusted total (since no per-item modifiers, we need to include the full amount in adjustedSum)\n\nBut that's awkward too because we'd be double-counting the block-adjusted total.\n\nLet me look at the formula differently:\n`adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + fee`\n= (sum of per-item-adjusted) + (sum of base premiums) * modifier + fee\n= sum of (per-item-adjusted + base * modifier) + fee\n\nSo per-item contribution = per-item-adjusted + base * modifier\nFor main items: base * per-item-modifier + base * modifier = base * (per-item-modifier + modifier)\nFor components: block-adjusted-total + block-adjusted-total * modifier (for the entire type, not per item)\n\nHmm. The implementation is fine, just a bit nuanced.\n\nLet me look at code-level simplifications:\n\n1. `applyMainItemModifiers` is clean. No changes needed.\n\n2. `computeTypeBase` could possibly be split: one for main items, one for components. But they share enough structure that keeping them together is fine.\n\n3. `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` - the `if` for main items is a bit awkward. Could be rewritten using a different structure.\n\nLet me try refactoring `computeTypeAdjustedPremium`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase: TypeBase, group: Item[]): number {\n  if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n    let sum = 0;\n    for (const item of group) {\n      sum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n    }\n    return sum;\n  }\n  return typeBase.premium;\n}\n```\n\nThe `typeBase.premium` for components is the block-adjusted total, which is the \"per-item premium\" (since components have no per-item modifiers). So returning typeBase.premium is correct.\n\nBut the asymmetry is awkward. Let me see if I can make this cleaner.\n\nWhat if I separate the responsibilities more clearly:\n- `computeTypeBase` returns just the per-item base premium and per-item insurance\n- The block rule and other logic are applied separately\n\nHmm, that's a bigger refactor. Let me think about what's most valuable.\n\nLooking at the code, I think there's a clearer way to express `quote`. Currently it has:\n\n```typescript\nlet policyBase = 0;\nlet insuranceSum = 0;\nlet adjustedSum = 0;\n\nfor (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n  const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n  adjustedSum += computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n}\n\nconst policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\nconst premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n```\n\nThe three accumulators feel a bit loose. Maybe consolidate into a `Totals` struct?\n\nActually I think this is acceptable. The current code is pretty clean.\n\nLet me look more carefully at `computeTypeBase`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  if (!KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n    throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThe `perItemBase` is used only in `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` for main items. For components, it's not used. That's a slight oddity - having `perItemBase` for components where it's irrelevant.\n\nLet me think about a refactoring that removes this asymmetry.\n\nIdea: What if we restructure so that:\n1. Each type has a `premium` (the base, per-item) and `insurance` (per-item)\n2. For components, we apply the block rule as an adjustment\n3. The total \"type contribution\" = per-item-base * (1 + per-item-modifier) * count (with block adjustment for components)\n\nHmm, but the block adjustment doesn't fit cleanly.\n\nLet me try a different approach. What if `computeTypeBase` returned a different structure:\n\n```typescript\ntype TypeContribution = {\n  premium: number;  // total premium for this type (with all type-level adjustments like block)\n  insurance: number;  // total insurance for this type\n};\n```\n\nAnd `applyItemModifiers` (renamed from applyMainItemModifiers) handles both cases. But for components, what would per-item modifiers be? There's no concept of per-item modifiers for components.\n\nActually, looking at the spec, components don't have material, enchantment, or cursed modifiers. They just have type and the block rule applies.\n\nSo the cleanest split might be:\n1. Main items path: compute per-item-base, apply per-item modifiers per item\n2. Components path: compute block-adjusted total\n\nAnd these are sufficiently different that they should be handled by separate code paths.\n\nI think the current code is actually doing this reasonably well. Let me see if I can improve clarity without breaking anything.\n\nLet me think about renaming `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` - the \"adjusted\" is vague. What does it adjust? It's the per-item-adjusted premium total. Maybe rename to `sumAdjustedPremiums`?\n\nAnd `typeBase.premium` - is this the base premium total? Maybe rename to `baseTotal` to be clearer? But then the field has different meanings for main items vs components...\n\nActually, I think there's a subtle thing here. Let me re-examine:\n\nFor main items: typeBase.premium = count * per-item-base. This is the \"base premium total\" for the type, before per-item modifiers.\n\nFor components: typeBase.premium = block-adjusted total. This is the actual premium for the type (no further modifiers).\n\nSo typeBase.premium is \"the type's contribution to policyBase\" but for components it's already been block-adjusted.\n\nWhat if we made this explicit? Use two different fields:\n\n```typescript\ntype TypeBase = {\n  premiumContribution: number;  // what to add to policyBase\n  insurance: number;\n  perItemBase: number;  // for per-item modifier calculation (main items only)\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n```\n\nHmm, that's the same as before basically. Let me think differently.\n\nWhat if we removed `perItemBase` and `isMainItem` from `TypeBase` and instead made `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` work for all types?\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase: TypeBase, group: Item[]): number {\n  let sum = 0;\n  for (const item of group) {\n    sum += applyItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItem