e, group: Item[]): number {\n  if (typeBase.isMainItem) {\n    let sum = 0;\n    for (const item of group) {\n      sum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, typeBase.perItemBase);\n    }\n    return sum;\n  }\n  return typeBase.premium;\n}\n```\n\nWhat if we extract the main-item loop into a separate function?\n\n```typescript\nfunction sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums(perItemBase: number, items: Item[]): number {\n  let sum = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    sum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, perItemBase);\n  }\n  return sum;\n}\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nfunction computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase: TypeBase, group: Item[]): number {\n  return typeBase.isMainItem\n    ? sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums(typeBase.perItemBase, group)\n    : typeBase.premium;\n}\n```\n\nThis makes `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` a simple ternary, which is much clearer. The loop is extracted.\n\nBut it adds a function. Mass-wise:\n- Before `computeTypeAdjustedPremium`: \n  - 2 params, 1 binding (sum), 1 conditional (`if`), 1 loop (`for`), 1 invocation (`applyMainItemModifiers`), 1 invocation (`*` inside, but operators don't count)\n  - Actually `applyMainItemModifiers` is 1 invocation per iteration of the loop, so 1 invocation per item. But we count the loop once and the invocation inside as separate.\n  - Bindings: 3 (typeBase, group, sum)\n  - Conditionals: 1 (if)\n  - Loops: 1 (for)\n  - Invocations: 1 (applyMainItemModifiers, called inside the loop)\n  - Mass: 3 + 4 + 5 + 2 = 14\n\n- After `computeTypeAdjustedPremium`:\n  - Bindings: 2 (typeBase, group)\n  - Conditionals: 1 (the `?:`)\n  - Invocations: 1 (sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums)\n  - Mass: 2 + 4 + 2 = 8\n\n- New `sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums`:\n  - Bindings: 3 (perItemBase, items, sum)\n  - Loops: 1\n  - Conditionals: 0\n  - Invocations: 1 (applyMainItemModifiers)\n  - Mass: 3 + 5 + 2 = 10\n\nTotal after: 8 + 10 = 18\nTotal before: 14\nDelta: +4 mass\n\nHmm, mass increases. But clarity might improve.\n\nThe new structure:\n- `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` becomes a one-liner ternary: very clear\n- `sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums` is a focused helper: clear\n\nThe original:\n- `computeTypeAdjustedPremium` is a 7-line function with a loop\n\nI think the refactor improves clarity (Rule 2). The mass increase is moderate.\n\nLet me also think about the loop in `quote`:\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n  const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n  policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n  insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n  adjustedSum += computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n}\n```\n\nThis loop iterates over types. Inside, it calls 3 functions. The loop is necessary.\n\nCould we extract this into a function that returns the totals?\n\n```typescript\nfunction computePolicyTotals(items: Item[]): { policyBase: number; insuranceSum: number; adjustedSum: number } {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    adjustedSum += computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n  }\n  return { policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum };\n}\n```\n\nThen `quote` becomes:\n```typescript\nexport function quote(items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): QuoteResult {\n  const { policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum } = computePolicyTotals(items);\n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n```\n\nThis separates the \"compute totals\" step from the \"apply policy modifier\" step. Cleaner.\n\nBut it adds another function. Mass:\n- New `computePolicyTotals`:\n  - Bindings: 4 (items, policyBase, insuranceSum, adjustedSum)\n  - Loops: 1\n  - Invocations: 4 (groupByType, computeTypeBase, c