ment, dragon material)\n- Subtract deductible\n- Cap at remainingCap\n\nLet me think about what tests come next:\n- Test 23: rune damage 200 -> payout 100 G\n- Test 24: sword 500 + amulet 300 -> payout 600 G (per-item deductible)\n- Test 25: sword steel enchant 9 damage 1000 -> payout 400 G\n- Test 26: sword steel enchant 8 -> payout 400 G (boundary)\n- Test 27: sword dragon enchant 5 damage 800 -> payout 700 G\n- Test 28: sword dragon enchant 7 damage 1000 -> payout 900 G\n- Test 29: sword dragon enchant 8 -> payout 400 G\n- Test 30: sword dragon enchant 9 -> payout 400 G\n- Test 31: 2 swords + 2 damages -> payout 1800 G\n- Test 32: cap exhaustion (sword, 2 claims of 1500 G)\n- Test 33: item not in policy -> throws\n- Test 34: negative amount -> throws\n- Test 35: more damages than items -> throws\n- Test 36: rune damage 100 -> payout 0\n\nOK 14 more claim tests. Let me first do a real implementation now since the pattern is clear, then proceed with tests.\n\nActually, let me follow strict TDD: activate one test at a time. Test 23 next.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"22 passing. Refactor to real implementation now:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_36c300b9f16646788ac9ff55","name":"subagent","arguments":{"agent":"refactor","agentScope":"both","task":"Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 22 (21 quote + 1 claim)\nRecent Green phase: Added a stub claim() that hardcodes payout=400 and remainingCap = policy.remainingCap - 400 for any input.\n\nThe implementation should now do real claim processing. The next tests will exercise:\n- rune damage 200 -> payout 100 (full minus deductible, no special clauses)\n- sword 500 + amulet 300 -> payout 600 (deductible per item)\n- sword steel enchant 9 damage 1000 -> payout 400 (50% rule for enchantment >= 8)\n- sword steel enchant 8 -> payout 400 (boundary, enchantment >= 8)\n- sword dragon enchant 5 damage 800 -> payout 700 (dragon material clause)\n- sword dragon enchant 7 damage 1000 -> payout 900 (dragon material clause, no enchantment >= 8)\n- sword dragon enchant 8 -> payout 400 (both clauses apply, 50% wins)\n- sword dragon enchant 9 -> payout 400 (same)\n- 2 swords + 2 damages of 1000 -> payout 1800 (each damage is separate)\n- cap exhaustion (sword insured cap 2000, 2 claims of 1500 -> first 1400/600 remaining, second 600/0)\n- damage to item not in policy -> throws\n- negative damage amount -> throws\n- more damages of a type than items in policy -> throws\n- damage to rune (100) -> payout 0 (cannot go negative)\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Count damage entries by itemType and compare with policy item counts (by type). If any damage type has more entries than policy items of that type, throw.\n2. Validate each damage amount >= 0. If not, throw.\n3. Validate each damage itemType is a known type and present in the policy. If not, throw.\n4. For each damage entry, process:\n   - Find a matching item in the policy (with same type)\n   - Compute reimbursement:\n     - If item.enchantment >= 8: reimbursement = amount * 0.5 (50% rule)\n     - Else if item.material == \"dragon\": reimbursement = amount (full)\n     - Else: reimbursement = amount (full)\n   - Apply 100 G deductible: reimbursement -= 100\n   - If reimbursement < 0, set to 0\n5. Sum all reimbursements.\n6. Apply cap: payout = Math.min(sum, policy.remainingCap). Round DOWN (MHPCO favor).\n7. remainingCap = policy.remainingCap - payout.\n8. Return { payout, remainingCap }.\n\nRounding note: \"a payout calculation that yields 350.5 G -> final payout 350 G (rounded down)\" - so use Math.floor() for the final payout.\n\nNote: spec says \"intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions; only the final premium or payout is rounded\". So we keep fractions internally and only round at the end.\n\nNote: the spec says \"claim references a damage entry whose item is not part of the policy (e.g. an amulet damaged when only a sword is insured, or an item with an unknown type) -> throws\". So unknown item type in damages -> throws (via the count check, since unknown types aren't in policy).\n\nNote: 100% certain that for the rune damage 100 test, payout should be 0 (cannot be negative).\n\nWrite a clean real implementation. Run autonomously, return when done."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":250,"output":1246,"cacheRead":99072,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":352,"totalTokens":100568,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784947687188,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-9d910bb0-d1d5-47f7-95a4-8cd813046422","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_36c300b9f16646788ac9ff55","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Refactoring complete. Here's the report:\n\n---\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n\n**Naming (Rule 2 - Reveals Intent)**:\n- Kept `claim` — name perfectly captures the function's purpose\n- Extracted purpose-named helpers:\n  - `validateDamageCounts` — checks type presence and over-allocation in one pass\n  - `validateDamageAmounts` — checks non-negative amounts\n  - `computeReimbursement` — per-item rule (enchantment/material)\n  - `countByType`, `poolItemsByType`, `takeItemOfType` — each does one thing\n- Extracted constants (required by eslint `no-magic-numbers`):\n  - `DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100`\n  - `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8`\n  - `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR = 0.5`\n\n**Mass Change**: 6 → ~62\nThe stub had very low mass because it did nothing — just `return { payout: 400, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - 400 }`. The full implementation necessarily has higher mass because it actually implements the algorithm. Mass growth is unavoidable and correct here; the implementation stays clean by:\n- Keeping every function under 30 lines (eslint enforced)\n- Cognitive complexity well under the 10 limit per function\n- No duplicated branches or logic — each rule lives in exactly one place\n\n**Simple Design Rules**:\n1. **Tests pass** — 22 still green; verified all 14 todo cases would pass via trace\n2. **Reveals intent** — `computeReimbursement` comment explains \"this also covers the dragon-material clause, which grants full reimbursement\" — the redundant `else if (dragon)` was deliberately omitted since both branches return `amount`, but a comment preserves the intent\n3. **No duplication** — single `countByType` reused; per-item rule lives only in `computeReimbursement`\n4. **Fewest elements** — only helpers that add clarity; no over-engineering\n\n**Key implementation choices**:\n- Validation upfront, before any state mutation (fail fast)\n- `Map<string, Item[]>` + `shift()` for O(n) item matching\n- Deductible applied per-damage entry, clamped to 0 (handles the rune-100 case)\n- Final payout uses `Math.floor` to favor MHPCO on fractional amounts\n- Unknown item types throw via the count check (0 in policy < 1+ in damages) — handles both \"unknown type\" and \"not insured\" with one error path\n\nRefactoring Complete: **Implementation**: real claim processing replaces stub. **Tests**: 22 passing, 14 todo. **Lint**: clean."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-