 full algorithm now, will all 14 tests pass? Let me check the expected outcomes against my understanding of the algorithm:\n\n**Test: regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 -> payout 400 G**\n- Item: sword, material steel, enchantment 3 (not >= 8, not dragon)\n- Damage: 500\n- Reimbursement: 500 (full, since not >= 8 and not dragon)\n- After 100 deductible: 500 - 100 = 400\n- Payout: min(400, 2000) = 400\n- Remaining: 2000 - 400 = 1600 ✓ (matches stub)\n\n**Test: damage to rune (200 G) -> payout 100 G**\n- Item: rune, no material, no enchantment (not >= 8, not dragon)\n- Damage: 200\n- Reimbursement: 200 (full)\n- After 100 deductible: 100\n- Payout: 100 ✓\n\n**Test: sword damaged 500 + amulet damaged 300 -> payout 600 G**\n- Sword: 500 - 100 = 400\n- Amulet: 300 - 100 = 200\n- Sum: 600 ✓\n\n**Test: sword (steel, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (50% rule)**\n- enchantment >= 8, so reimbursement = 1000 * 0.5 = 500\n- After 100 deductible: 400 ✓\n\n**Test: sword (steel, enchantment 8) damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (boundary)**\n- Same as above ✓\n\n**Test: sword (dragon, enchantment 5), damage 800 -> payout 700 G (dragon clause only)**\n- enchantment < 8, material = dragon -> reimbursement = 800 (full)\n- After 100 deductible: 700 ✓\n\n**Test: sword (dragon, enchantment 7), damage 1000 -> payout 900 G (dragon clause only)**\n- enchantment < 8, material = dragon -> reimbursement = 1000 (full)\n- After 100 deductible: 900 ✓\n\n**Test: sword (dragon, enchantment 8), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (both, 50% wins)**\n- enchantment >= 8 -> reimbursement = 500\n- After 100 deductible: 400 ✓\n\n**Test: sword (dragon, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (both, 50% wins)**\n- Same as above ✓\n\n**Test: policy covers two swords, two damage entries (1000 G each) -> payout 1800 G**\n- Each sword: 1000 - 100 = 900\n- Sum: 1800 ✓\n\n**Test: sword insured (cap 2000), two successive claims of 1500 G -> first 1400 / remaining 600, second 600 / remaining 0**\n- Wait, the description in the test says: \"sword insured (cap 2000), two successive claims of 1500 G\"\n- The 2*insuranceSum with sword=1000 -> cap = 2000 ✓\n- First claim: damage 1500, enchantment < 8, not dragon -> 1500 - 100 = 1400\n- Remaining cap after first: 2000 - 1400 = 600\n- Second claim: damage 1500 -> 1500 - 100 = 1400\n- Payout: min(1400, 600) = 600\n- Remaining: 600 - 600 = 0 ✓\n\n**Test: damage references an item not in the policy -> throws**\n- Item type not in policy.items -> throw ✓\n\n**Test: negative damage amount -> throws**\n- amount < 0 -> throw ✓\n\n**Test: more damage entries of a type than items in policy -> throws**\n- If 3 damage entries for \"sword\" but only 2 swords in policy -> throw ✓\n\n**Test: damage to rune (100 G) -> payout 0 G**\n- Damage 100, rune -> 100 - 100 = 0 (capped at 0, not negative)\n- Payout: 0 ✓\n\nOK, so the algorithm is clear. Now, since the task is asking me to act as a refactor subagent, but really I'm being asked to do the implementation. Let me note that the task is unusual — it's a refactor phase that's actually doing the implementation.\n\nWait, the system says I'm a subagent for the refactor phase. Let me read the AGENTS.md again to understand my role.\n\nActually, looking at this carefully:\n- The user is invoking me as a subagent for the refactor phase\n- The instructions in AGENTS.md say:\n  - \"Refactor Phase -- Subagent (isolated context) -- subagent tool with agent: 'refactor', agentScope: 'both'\"\n  - \"The agent will improve code while keeping tests green:\"\n    - \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring (or document why none is possible)\"\n    - \"Evaluate naming FIRST\"\n    - \"Apply Four Rules of Simple Design (priority order)\"\n    - \"Calculate APP (Absolute Priority Premise) mass before/after\"\n\nBut also, the task description says: \"Write a clean real implementation. Run autonomously, return when done.\"\n\nSo the user is using the refactor subagent to do the actual implementation work. This is unusual but the user has explicit instructions.\n\nLet me also note that the system prompt at the top is the refactoring instructions. I should follow them, but ALSO implement the algorithm as described.\n\nLooking at the system prompt structure:\n- I am acting as a refactoring subagent\n- I should attempt at least one refactoring (mandatory)\n- I should evaluate naming FIRST\n- I should calculate APP mass before/after\n- I should keep tests green\n\nBut the actual task is to write the real implementation. So I'll:\n1. First document the current state (the stub has mass X)\n2. Implement the real algorithm\n3. Verify all tests pass\n4. Document the mass change\n\nWait — the tests are todos, not active tests! Only ONE test is currently passing (the regular sword 500 -> 400). So if I implement the full algorithm, I need to be careful that ALL todo tests would pass when activated (since the user said \"all tests should pass\" is the goal).\n\nActually re-reading: \"Passing tests: 22 (21 quote + 1 claim)\". So 1 claim test is passing — that's the regular sword test. The other 14 claim tests are todos.\n\nHmm, but the user wants me to \"Write a clean real implementation\". So I should write the implementation such that it would pass all the tests if they were activated. The user is asking me to implement, not just refactor.\n\nBut this is the refactor phase, and the refactor phase shouldn't activate tests — that's for the red phase.\n\nWait, looking at the user's prompt more carefully:\n- \"Recent Green phase: Added a stub claim() that hardcodes payout=400 and remainingCap = policy.remainingCap - 400 for any input.\"\n- \"The implementation should now do real claim processing.\"\n- \"Write a clean real implementation. Run autonomously, return when done.\"\n\nThe user is asking me to do a refactor that results in a real implementation. The tests are all currently todos (except one), but if they were activated, the implementation should handle them correctly.\n\nI think what I need to do is:\n1. Write the real claim implementation according to the algorithm\n2. Ensure the currently active test (the regular sword 500 -> 400) still passes\n3. The implementation should be designed to handle all the todo tests correctly\n4. Document the mass change\n\nSince the todos aren't active tests, they won't actually run. So technically I only need to keep the one passing test green. But the implementation should be complete.\n\nLet me write a clean implementation following the algorithm.\n\nNow let me think about naming first:\n- The function is `claim()` — that's good, it's the right name\n- `ClaimResult` is the return type — good\n- I might need helper functions\n\nLet me think about the implementation:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  // 1. Validate damage item types: known types present in policy, count <= item count\n  const itemsByType = countByType(policy.items);\n  const damagesByType = countByType(damages.map(d => ({ type: d.itemType })));\n  \n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(damagesByType)) {\n    const policyCount = itemsByType[type] ?? 0;\n    if (policyCount === 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown or uninsured item type: ${type}`);\n    }\n    if (count > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(`Too many damage entries for item type: ${type}`);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // 2. Validate each damage amount >= 0\n  for (const d of damages) {\n    if (d.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Negative damage amount: ${d.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // 3. For each damage entry, find matching item and compute reimbursement\n  const availableItems = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of policy.items) {\n    const list = availableItems.get(item.type) ?? [];\n    list.push(item);\n    availableItems.set(item.type, list);\n  }\n  \n  let sum = 0;\n  for (const d of damages) {\n    const items = availableItems.get(d.itemType)!;\n    const item = items.shift()!; // take one item\n    let reimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, d.amount);\n    reimbursement = Math.max(0, reimbursement - 100); // 100 G deductible, can't go negative\n    sum += reimbursement;\n  }\n  \n  // 6. Apply cap: payout = Math.min(sum, policy.remainingCap). Round DOWN\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(sum, policy.remainingCap));\n  const remainingCap = policy.remainingCap - payout;\n  \n  return { payout, remainingCap };\n}\n\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  // 50% rule: enchantment >= 8 wins over dragon material\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n    return amount * 0.5;\n  }\n  // Otherwise: full reimbursement (dragon material also gets full since enchantment < 8)\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(items: { type: string }[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts[item.type] = (counts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n```\n\nWait, there's an issue with the deductible test: \"damage to rune (100 G) -> payout 0 G (cannot go negative)\". Let me check:\n- Damage 100, rune (no material, no enchantment)\n- Reimbursement: 100 (full)\n- After 100 deductible: 0 (not negative)\n- ✓\n\nBut wait — what if it's \"rune damage 100\" vs \"rune damage 200\"? The expected payout is 100 for damage 200. So for damage 100, reimbursement is 100, then minus 100 = 0. Good.\n\nBut the algorithm says \"If reimbursement < 0, set to 0\". With damage 100, reimbursement is 100, then 100 - 100 = 0, not negative. Good.\n\nBut what about a hypothetical damage 50 to rune? Reimbursement = 50, then 50 - 100 = -50, set to 0. That works.\n\nLet me also think about the dragon material case. The algorithm says:\n- If item.enchantment >= 8: reimbursement = amount * 0.5\n- Else if item.material == \"dragon\": reimbursement = amount (full)\n- Else: reimbursement = amount (full)\n\nSo dragon material gets full amount (not 50%). Only enchantment >= 8 gets 50%. That's correct per the tests.\n\nNow let me check the test about \"dragon, enchantment 5, damage 800 -> payout 700\":\n- enchantment 5 < 8, so 50% rule doesn't apply\n- material = dragon, so full amount: 800\n- After 100 deductible: 700 ✓\n\nAnd \"dragon, enchantment 7, damage 1000 -> payout 900\":\n- enchantment 7 < 8, so 50% rule doesn't apply\n- material = dragon, so full amount: 1000\n- After 100 deductible: 900 ✓\n\nAnd \"dragon, enchantment 8, damage 1000 -> payout 400\":\n- enchantment 8 >= 8, so 50% rule applies: 500\n- After 100 deductible: 400 ✓\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me think about the algorithm more carefully:\n\nThe user says:\n\"2. Validate each damage amount >= 0. If not, throw.\"\n\nBut what about the rune damage 100 -> payout 0? The amount 100 is >= 0, so it passes validation. Good.\n\nWhat about the order of validations? The user says:\n1. Count damage entries by itemType\n2. Validate amounts\n3. Validate item types\n\nBut actually, we can validate item types and counts together — unknown types will have count 0 in policy. Let me re-read:\n\n\"Count damage entries by itemType and compare with policy item counts (by type). If any damage type has more entries than policy items of that type, throw.\"\n\nThis handles both cases:\n- Unknown type: 0 in policy, 1+ in damages -> throw (because 1 > 0)\n- Too many damages: 5 in damages, 2 in policy -> throw\n\nSo a single count check handles both error cases. Good.\n\nBut wait, the spec says \"damage references an item not in the policy\" and \"item with an unknown type\" both throw. Both should produce the same error message or distinct ones? The user just says \"throws\". So either works.\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n- Empty damages array: sum = 0, payout = 0, remainingCap unchanged. Good.\n- Damage to multiple items in one claim: works with the count check.\n\nOK let me finalize the implementation. But first, let me think about naming:\n\n- `claim` - good name\n- `computeReimbursement` - descriptive\n- `countByType` - descriptive\n\nActually, let me think about extracting `computeReimbursement` — is it really worth it? The rule is:\n- If enchantment >= 8: amount * 0.5\n- Else: amount\n\nThis is simple enough to inline. But extracting makes the intent clear. Per Rule 2 (reveals intent), extracting is good.\n\nActually, the logic isn't quite that simple. Let me re-read:\n\nThe algorithm says:\n- If item.enchantment >= 8: reimbursement = amount * 0.5 (50% rule)\n- Else if item.material == \"dragon\": reimbursement = amount (full)\n- Else: reimbursement = amount (full)\n\nSo in both else branches, it's `amount`. But this could just be simplified to:\n- If item.enchantment >= 8: amount * 0.5\n- Else: amount\n\nThe dragon check is redundant (since it doesn't change anything). But to be explicit about the spec (dragon material gets full coverage), we can keep the explicit check.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the tests:\n- \"dragon, enchantment 5, damage 800 -> payout 700 (dragon clause only)\"\n- \"dragon, enchantment 7, damage 1000 -> payout 900 (dragon clause only)\"\n\nBoth have \"dragon clause only\". So the dragon clause clearly does something (full amount instead of... what? maybe a different default?). Let me re-read the user's algorithm:\n\n\"- If item.enchantment >= 8: reimbursement = amount * 0.5 (50% rule)\n- Else if item.material == \"dragon\": reimbursement = amount (full)\n- Else: reimbursement = amount (full)\"\n\nThe dragon clause and the default are the same — both return full amount. So the dragon check is technically unnecessary. But maybe in a more general spec, dragon could mean \"dragon-scale\" or something different. For now, both branches do the same thing.\n\nThe cleanest implementation:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  // Enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n    return amount * 0.5;\n  }\n  // Otherwise: full reimbursement\n  return amount;\n}\n```\n\nOr even simpler (Rule 4: Fewest Elements):\n\n```typescript\nconst reimbursement = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8 ? amount * 0.5 : amount;\n```\n\nBut per Rule 2 (reveals intent), the if statement with comment is better.\n\nLet me also think about whether to put the deductible in the reimbursement function or outside. Per the algorithm:\n- Compute reimbursement (with material/enchantment rules)\n- Apply 100 G deductible\n- Cap at 0\n\nSo deductible is separate. That makes the function `computeReimbursement` not include the deductible. Let me name it `computeBaseReimbursement` or just keep it as `computeReimbursement`.\n\nHmm. Maybe:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n    return amount * 0.5;\n  }\n  return amount;\n}\n```\n\nThis computes the reimbursement before deductible. After this, we apply deductible and clamp to 0.\n\nOK let me write the final implementation:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  // Validate: each damage itemType is known, present in policy, and not over-allocated\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((item) => item.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage count (${damageCount}) for item type \"${type}\" exceeds policy count (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Validate: each damage amount is non-negative\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Process each damage: match to an available item, compute reimbursement\n  const availableItems = groupItems(policy.items);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = availableItems.get(damage.itemType)!.shift()!;\n    const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);\n    total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100); // 100 G deductible, never below 0\n  }\n  \n  // Apply cap, round down (favor MHPCO)\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  // Enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement (overrides dragon material)\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n    return amount * 0.5;\n  }\n  // Otherwise: full reimbursement (covers dragon material clause)\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction groupItems(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const groups = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const list = groups.get(item.type) ?? [];\n    list.push(item);\n    groups.set(item.type, list);\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this looks pretty clean. Let me think about the APP mass.\n\nCurrent code (stub):\n```typescript\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, _damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  return { payout: 400, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - 400 };\n}\n```\n\nAPP mass for stub:\n- Constants: 400, 400 (2 × 1) = 2\n- Bindings: policy, _damages, ClaimResult implicit (return type) = 2\n- Invocations: 1 (return object) = 2\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\nTotal: 6\n\nNew code APP mass:\nLet me count carefully...\n\n```typescript\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((item) => item.type));  // assignment, invocation, lambda\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));        // same\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {        // loop\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;                          // assignment, binding, constant, nullish\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {                                      // conditional\n      throw new Error(...);                                                // invocation, assignment (well, no, throw)\n    }\n  }\n  for (const damage of damages) {                                          // loop\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {                                               // conditional\n      throw new Error(...);\n    }\n  }\n  const availableItems = groupItems(policy.items);                          // assignment, invocation\n  let total = 0;                                                            // assignment, constant\n  for (const damage of damages) {                                           // loop\n    const item = availableItems.get(damage.itemType)!.shift()!;             // assignment, invocation\n    const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);   // assignment, invocation\n    total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100);                         // assignment, invocation, constant\n  }\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));         // assignment, invocation\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nOK this is getting tedious. Let me think more pragmatically. The point of the mass calculation is to compare before and after to see if we improved.\n\nThe stub has very low mass (it's almost nothing). The full implementation has much higher mass because it actually does something. In TDD, mass will go up as we add functionality. The question is: is the implementation as clean as possible?\n\nLet me think about whether there's any duplication or simplification opportunities:\n\n1. The two validation loops could potentially be combined — but they check different things (counts vs amounts). Keep separate for clarity.\n\n2. `countByType` takes string[] but is called with `.map(item => item.type)`. We could pass the items directly. Let me think:\n   - `countByType(damages.map(d => d.itemType))` — passing strings\n   - Alternative: `countByType(damages, d => d.itemType)` — generic\n   - Or just inline the counting:\n     ```typescript\n     const policyCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n     for (const item of policy.items) {\n       policyCounts.set(item.type, (policyCounts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n     }\n     ```\n   - This is more code per call site but avoids the helper. Hmm.\n\n3. `groupItems` returns `Map<string, Item[]>` with shift to remove. This is a bit hacky — mutating while iterating. Alternative:\n   ```typescript\n   const itemIndex = new Map<string, number>();  // next index to use per type\n   for (const item of policy.items) {\n     if (!itemIndex.has(item.type)) itemIndex.set(item.type, 0);\n   }\n   // then use policy.items[itemIndex.get(damage.itemType)++] per damage\n   ```\n   But this requires policy.items to be in order. Actually since we iterate damages and look up items, we need to track position.\n\nActually, let me think again. The damage entries don't reference specific items — they just reference item types. So any sword damage can be matched to any sword in the policy. We just need to make sure we don't double-match.\n\nThe shift approach is fine. Alternative: use a counter:\n\n```typescript\nconst nextItemIndex = new Map<string, number>();\n// Initialize for all policy item types\nfor (const item of policy.items) {\n  if (!nextItemIndex.has(item.type)) {\n    nextItemIndex.set(item.type, 0);\n  }\n}\n\n// When processing each damage:\nconst itemsOfType = policy.items.filter(i => i.type === damage.itemType);\nconst idx = nextItemIndex.get(damage.itemType)!;\nconst item = itemsOfType[idx];\nnextItemIndex.set(damage.itemType, idx + 1);\n```\n\nHmm, but filter is O(n) per damage, making the whole thing O(n²). Bad.\n\nBetter: pre-build the array per type, use index counter:\n\n```typescript\nconst itemsByType = new Map<string, Item[]>();\nconst indices = new Map<string, number>();\nfor (const item of policy.items) {\n  if (!itemsByType.has(item.type)) {\n    itemsByType.set(item.type, []);\n    indices.set(item.type, 0);\n  }\n  itemsByType.get(item.type)!.push(item);\n}\n\n// When processing:\nconst idx = indices.get(damage.itemType)!;\nconst item = itemsByType.get(damage.itemType)![idx];\nindices.set(damage.itemType, idx + 1);\n```\n\nThis is similar to my original `groupItems` but uses an index counter. The shift approach is cleaner but mutates the array.\n\nOr even simpler: just use the index in the original array. Items of the same type are grouped by virtue of how the policy is structured. Actually no, they might not be.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The simplest implementation might be:\n\n```typescript\n// For each damage entry, find the first unused item of the same type\nconst used = new Set<number>(); // indices of used policy items\nfor (const damage of damages) {\n  const idx = policy.items.findIndex((item, i) => \n    item.type === damage.itemType && !used.has(i)\n  );\n  if (idx === -1) throw new Error(...);\n  used.add(idx);\n  const item = policy.items[idx];\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nBut this is O(n²) for many damages.\n\nOK, the shift approach is fine. Let me stick with it but maybe rename for clarity:\n\nActually wait. Let me think about whether we need to match specific items to specific damages at all. The user said \"Find a matching item in the policy (with same type)\". So we just need to find a matching item — any item of the same type. The damage doesn't care which specific item, just that it can be matched.\n\nBut for enchantment/material rules, we DO need to know the item's properties. So we need to look up a specific item.\n\nCould two damages to the same item type but different items (e.g., one enchanted, one not) produce different results? Yes! E.g., policy has sword(enchant=3) and sword(enchant=9), damages are [{type:sword, amount:500}, {type:sword, amount:1000}].\n\nWith my current algorithm:\n- First damage: matches some sword (let's say enchant=3): reimbursement = 500, deductible 100, total 400\n- Second damage: matches the other sword (enchant=9): reimbursement = 500, deductible 100, total 400\n- Sum: 800\n\nBut the actual order of matching depends on the policy order. If policy is [sword(enchant=3), sword(enchant=9)] and damages are processed in order:\n- Damage 500 matches sword(enchant=3) -> 500\n- Damage 1000 matches sword(enchant=9) -> 500 (after 50% rule)\n\nThe user's test \"policy covers two swords, two damage entries (1000 G each) -> payout 1800 G\" doesn't specify the enchantments, so I'll assume they're identical (probably regular steel swords with low enchantment). Let me check:\n\nActually the test makes the policy with `makePolicy` and creates two swords. The makePolicy doesn't set material or enchantment, so they'd be undefined. Both swords are identical. So the order doesn't matter.\n\nFor now, my \"first matching item\" approach works. Let me think about whether to use shift or index counter. Shift mutates the array which is a bit ugly. Index counter is cleaner:\n\n```typescript\nfunction buildItemPools(items: Item[]): { pools: Map<string, Item[]>; indices: Map<string, number> } {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  const indices = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!pools.has(item.type)) {\n      pools.set(item.type, []);\n      indices.set(item.type, 0);\n    }\n    pools.get(item.type)!.push(item);\n  }\n  return { pools, indices };\n}\n```\n\nBut this combines two things in one function. Maybe split:\n\n```typescript\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> { ... }\n\n// Then in claim():\nconst pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\nconst indices = new Map<string, number>(Array.from(pools.keys(), k => [k, 0]));\n\nfor (const damage of damages) {\n  const pool = pools.get(damage.itemType)!;\n  const idx = indices.get(damage.itemType)!;\n  const item = pool[idx];\n  indices.set(damage.itemType, idx + 1);\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is verbose. The shift approach is more concise. Let me think about the simplest clean approach.\n\nActually, the cleanest is probably:\n\n```typescript\nfunction claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamages(policy, damages);\n  \n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeItem(pools, damage.itemType);\n    const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);\n    total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100);\n  }\n  \n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction takeItem(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  const pool = pools.get(type);\n  if (!pool || pool.length === 0) {\n    throw new Error(`No available item of type \"${type}\"`);\n  }\n  return pool.shift()!;\n}\n```\n\nThis is OK. The `shift()` mutates the array, which is a side effect, but it's contained within the helper.\n\nAlternatively, we can avoid mutation:\n\n```typescript\nfunction takeItem(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  const pool = pools.get(type);\n  if (!pool || pool.length === 0) {\n    throw new Error(`No available item of type \"${type}\"`);\n  }\n  const [item, ...rest] = pool;\n  pools.set(type, rest);\n  return item;\n}\n```\n\nBut this creates a new array each time. Performance isn't really a concern for tests, but immutability is nicer.\n\nActually, for simplicity, let me just use the index counter approach inline:\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The validation step already ensures that we have enough items. So we don't need to handle \"no items left\" in `takeItem` — that case won't happen if validation passes. But if we want to be defensive...\n\nHmm, let me just keep things simple. The validation is the contract; the processing assumes it.\n\nLet me look at the code once more and finalize.\n\nActually, I realize there might be an even simpler approach. Instead of pre-pooling items, we can just process damages and for each damage, find an item of matching type that hasn't been used yet. But this is O(n²).\n\nOr we can use a simpler data structure: keep the items array and a \"used\" set.\n\n```typescript\nfunction claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamages(policy, damages);\n  \n  const remainingItems = new Map<Item, number>(); // item -> remaining count\n  for (const item of policy.items) {\n    remainingItems.set(item, (remainingItems.get(item) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  \n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = findAndConsumeItem(remainingItems, damage.itemType);\n    const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);\n    total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100);\n  }\n  \n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction findAndConsumeItem(remaining: Map<Item, number>, type: string): Item {\n  for (const [item, count] of remaining) {\n    if (item.type === type && count > 0) {\n      if (count === 1) remaining.delete(item);\n      else remaining.set(item, count - 1);\n      return item;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error(`No available item of type \"${type}\"`);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is O(n²) too. Not great.\n\nLet me just go with the pool approach. It's clean enough.\n\nOK let me write the final code:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n  \n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = pools.get(damage.itemType)!.shift()!;\n    const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);\n    total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100); // 100 G deductible, never below 0\n  }\n  \n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (count > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage entries for \"${type}\" (${count}) exceed insured items (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  // Enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement (overrides dragon material)\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n    return amount * 0.5;\n  }\n  // Default (incl. dragon material): full reimbursement\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!pools.has(item.type)) {\n      pools.set(item.type, []);\n    }\n    pools.get(item.type)!.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but the `!.shift()!` is a bit ugly with two non-null assertions. Let me think... Actually since validation passed, we know there's an item. But TypeScript doesn't know that.\n\nMaybe extract:\n```typescript\nfunction claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  // ...\n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeAvailable(pools, damage.itemType);\n    // ...\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n\nfunction takeAvailable(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  const pool = pools.get(type);\n  if (!pool || pool.length === 0) {\n    throw new Error(`No available item of type \"${type}\"`);\n  }\n  return pool.shift()!;\n}\n```\n\nThis adds a helper but makes the main code cleaner. Trade-off.\n\nActually, the validation should make this case impossible. But TypeScript can't prove that. So we either need:\n1. Non-null assertion `!`\n2. Throw inside the helper\n3. Use `?? throw`\n\nOption 3 is nice:\n```typescript\nconst item = pools.get(damage.itemType)?.shift() ?? throwNewError(`...`);\n```\n\nBut `throwNewError` isn't a function. We'd need:\n```typescript\nfunction fail(msg: string): never { throw new Error(msg); }\nconst item = pools.get(damage.itemType)?.shift() ?? fail(`...`);\n```\n\nHmm, that's also OK. But the helper function is clearer for someone reading the code.\n\nLet me think about this more holistically. The current code uses `pool.shift()!`. The `!` says \"trust me, it's defined\". The validation step earlier ensures this is true. So the assertion is technically safe but not