nough items, the inner pool should never be empty. The outer `.get()` could return undefined if the type isn't in pools — but validation ensures it is.\n\nLet me just commit to one approach. I'll use the helper function for clarity.\n\nFinal implementation:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n  \n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);\n    const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);\n    total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100); // 100 G deductible, never negative\n  }\n  \n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage entries for \"${type}\" (${damageCount}) exceed insured items (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const { amount } of damages) {\n    if (amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  // Enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement (overrides dragon material clause)\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8) {\n    return amount * 0.5;\n  }\n  // Default (incl. dragon material): full reimbursement\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    let pool = pools.get(item.type);\n    if (!pool) {\n      pool = [];\n      pools.set(item.type, pool);\n    }\n    pool.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n\nfunction takeItemOfType(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  const pool = pools.get(type)!;\n  return pool.shift()!;\n}\n```\n\nOK this looks reasonable. Let me run tests to make sure the passing test still passes.\n\nActually wait, looking at `takeItemOfType`, the `!` after `pools.get(type)` is fine because validation has already verified this exists. The `!` after `pool.shift()` is fine because validation has already verified there's at least one item.\n\nLet me verify the test:\n\nTest: regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 -> payout 400 G\n- policy: makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }], 2000)\n- damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }]\n- expected: { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 }\n\nLet me trace through my implementation:\n1. validateDamageCounts:\n   - policyCounts: { sword: 1 }\n   - damageCounts: { sword: 1 }\n   - For type \"sword\": damageCount=1, policyCount=1, 1 > 1 is false, no throw\n2. validateDamageAmounts:\n   - amount 500 >= 0, no throw\n3. pools = { sword: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }] }\n4. Loop: damage = { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }\n   - item = takeItemOfType(pools, \"sword\") -> { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }\n   - baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, 500) -> enchantment 3 < 8, so return 500\n   - total += max(0, 500 - 100) = 400, total = 400\n5. payout = Math.floor(Math.min(400, 2000)) = 400\n6. return { payout: 400, remainingCap: 2000 - 400 } = { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 } ✓\n\nThe test passes.\n\nNow, will the todo tests pass if activated? Let me trace a few:\n\nTest: \"sword (steel, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (50% rule)\"\n- policy: makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9 }], 2000)\n- damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }]\n- Trace:\n  1. validation passes\n  2. pools = { sword: [{ ..., enchantment: 9 }] }\n  3. computeReimbursement: enchantment 9 >= 8, return 1000 * 0.5 = 500\n  4. total = max(0, 500 - 100) = 400\n  5. payout = floor(min(400, 2000)) = 400\n  6. return { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 } ✓\n\nTest: \"policy covers two swords, two damage entries (1000 G each) -> payout 1800 G\"\n- policy: makePolicy([{type:\"sword\"}, {type:\"sword\"}], 4000) // insuranceSum = 2000, cap = 4000\n- damages: [{itemType:\"sword\", amount:1000}, {itemType:\"sword\", amount:1000}]\n- Trace:\n  1. validation: policyCounts = { sword: 2 }, damageCounts = { sword: 2 }, no throw\n  2. pools = { sword: [{type:\"sword\"}, {type:\"sword\"}] }\n  3. Damage 1: take first sword, reimbursement = 1000 (no enchant), max(0, 1000-100) = 900, total = 900\n  4. Damage 2: take second sword, reimbursement = 1000, max(0, 1000-100) = 900, total = 1800\n  5. payout = floor(min(1800, 4000)) = 1800\n  6. return { payout: 1800, remainingCap: 2200 } ✓\n\nTest: \"sword insured (cap 2000), two successive claims of 1500 G -> first 1400 / remaining 600, second 600 / remaining 0\"\n- First claim:\n  - policy: makePolicy([{type:\"sword\"}], 2000)\n  - damages: [{itemType:\"sword\", amount:1500}]\n  - Trace:\n    1. validation passes\n    2. pools = { sword: [{type:\"sword\"}] }\n    3. reimbursement = 1500, max(0, 1500-100) = 1400\n    4. payout = floor(min(1400, 2000)) = 1400\n    5. return { payout: 1400, remainingCap: 600 } ✓\n\n- Second claim (with new policy where remainingCap = 600):\n  - policy: makePolicy([{type:\"sword\"}], 600)\n  - damages: [{itemType:\"sword\", amount:1500}]\n  - Trace:\n    1. validation passes\n    2. reimbursement = 1500, max(0, 1500-100) = 1400\n    3. payout = floor(min(1400, 600)) = 600\n    4. return { payout: 600, remainingCap: 0 } ✓\n\nTest: \"damage to rune (100 G) -> payout 0 G\"\n- policy: makePolicy([{type:\"rune\"}], 500) // rune insurance = 250, cap = 500\n- damages: [{itemType:\"rune\", amount:100}]\n- Trace:\n  1. validation: rune count matches\n  2. pools = { rune: [{type:\"rune\"}] }\n  3. reimbursement = 100 (no enchantment/material), max(0, 100-100) = 0\n  4. payout = floor(min(0, 500)) = 0\n  5. return { payout: 0, remainingCap: 500 } ✓\n\nAll tests should pass.\n\nNow let me think about the APP mass calculation. Let me count more carefully for the new implementation.\n\n```typescript\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  // Line: validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  // - 1 invocation\n  \n  // Line: validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n  // - 1 invocation\n  \n  // Line: const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  // - 1 assignment\n  // - 1 invocation\n  // - 1 invocation (.items property access — not counted?)\n  \n  // Line: let total = 0;\n  // - 1 assignment\n  // - 1 constant (0)\n  \n  // Line: for (const damage of damages) {\n  // - 1 loop\n  \n  // Inside loop:\n  //   Line: const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);\n  //   - 1 assignment\n  //   - 1 invocation\n  \n  //   Line: const baseReimbursement = computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount);\n  //   - 1 assignment\n  //   - 1 invocation\n  \n  //   Line: total += Math.max(0, baseReimbursement - 100);\n  //   - 1 assignment\n  //   - 1 invocation (Math.max)\n  //   - 1 constant (0)\n  //   - 1 constant (100)\n  \n  // Line: }\n  \n  // Line: const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  // - 1 assignment\n  // - 2 invocations (Math.floor, Math.min)\n  \n  // Line: return {\n  //   payout,\n  //   remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  // };\n  // - 1 assignment (payout)\n  // - 1 assignment (remainingCap)\n  // - 1 invocation (return)\n}\n```\n\nOK this is getting really tedious. Let me just focus on the qualitative analysis.\n\nThe stub mass is very low (~6). The new implementation has significantly higher mass but it's all necessary for the actual functionality. The implementation is clean: clear function names, separation of concerns, validation upfront, etc.\n\nThe user is asking for a \"clean real implementation\" — and I've provided that. Let me just verify the tests pass and document the changes.\n\nNow let me also think: do I need to make any refactoring choices explicit? Looking at the refactor instructions:\n- MUST attempt at least one refactoring (mandatory)\n- Evaluate naming FIRST\n- Apply Four Rules of Simple Design (priority order)\n- Calculate APP mass before/after\n\nI've evaluated naming (kept `claim`, added descriptive helpers).\nI've applied Simple Design Rules:\n- Rule 1: Tests pass (will verify)\n- Rule 2: Names are descriptive (claim, validateDamageCounts, validateDamageAmounts, computeReimbursement, countByType, poolItemsByType, takeItemOfType)\n- Rule 3: No duplication (validation is separated, processing is in main function)\n- Rule 4: Fewest elements (extracted only what's necessary)\n\nLet me check: is there any duplication I can eliminate?\n\nLooking at the code:\n- `validateDamageCounts` and `validateDamageAmounts` both iterate damages. Could combine? But they check different things, separating improves clarity.\n- `countByType` is a helper. Only used twice (one in policyCounts, one in damageCounts). Could inline? But extracting improves clarity.\n\nI think the implementation is clean. Let me run tests to verify.\n\nActually, wait. I just realized the tests are todos, so they won't actually run. Only the one active test will run. So I need to make sure that test still passes, which it should.\n\nLet me also check the eslint config to make sure I'm not breaking any linting rules.\n\nLet me write the implementation and run the tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll start by examining the current state, then implement the real claim processing logic following the algorithm."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b94e82cc4e924cb287194dda","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/eslint.config.mjs"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_462e60dfe89c41e9afeb2dfe","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/vitest.config.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":527,"output":12172,"cacheRead":8704,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":12011,"totalTokens":21403,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784947734295,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-f09f419f-5b56-401e-a8ff-cef1ea28374d","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b94e82cc4e924cb287194dda","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import sonarjs from \"eslint-plugin-sonarjs\";\nimport tseslint from \"typescript-eslint\";\n\nexport default [\n  {\n    files: [\"src/**/*.ts\"],\n    ignores: [\"src/**/*.spec.ts\"],\n    languageOptions: {\n      parser: tseslint.parser,\n      parserOptions: {\n        projectService: true,\n      },\n    },\n    plugins: {\n      sonarjs,\n    },\n    rules: {\n      // Complexity smells\n      \"sonarjs/cognitive-complexity\": [\"error\", 10],\n      \"max-depth\": [\"error\", 3],\n      \"max-lines-per-function\": [\"error\", { max: 30, skipBlankLines: true, skipComments: true }],\n      \"max-params\": [\"error\", 4],\n\n      // Duplication smells\n      \"sonarjs/no-duplicate-string\": [\"error\", { threshold: 3 }],\n      \"sonarjs/no-duplicated-branches\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/no-identical-functions\": \"error\",\n\n      // Dead code smells\n      \"no-unused-vars\": \"off\",\n      \"sonarjs/no-unused-collection\": \"error\",\n      \"no-unreachable\": \"error\",\n\n      // Magic numbers\n      \"no-magic-numbers\": [\"error\", { ignore: [0, 1, -1], ignoreArrayIndexes: true }],\n\n      // Boolean/logic smells\n      \"sonarjs/no-redundant-boolean\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/no-gratuitous-expressions\": \"error\",\n\n      // Code quality smells\n      \"sonarjs/no-collapsible-if\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/no-redundant-jump\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/no-useless-catch\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/prefer-immediate-return\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/prefer-single-boolean-return\": \"error\",\n\n      // Nested complexity\n      \"sonarjs/no-nested-switch\": \"error\",\n      \"sonarjs/no-nested-template-literals\": \"error\",\n    },\n  },\n];\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948044429},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_462e60dfe89c41e9afeb2dfe","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  test: {\n    include: ['src/**/*.spec.ts'],\n    globals: true,\n    coverage: {\n      provider: 'v8',\n      reporter: ['json-summary', 'text'],\n      include: ['src/**/*.ts'],\n      exclude: ['src/**/*.spec.ts'],\n    },\n  },\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948044429},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Important constraints from eslint:\n- `max-lines-per-function`: 30 lines per function\n- `max-depth`: 3\n- `sonarjs/cognitive-complexity`: 10\n- `no-magic-numbers`: cannot use numbers other than 0, 1, -1\n\nThis is important! The `100` deductible and `0.5` factor are magic numbers. I need to make them named constants.\n\nLet me also note:\n- `sonarjs/no-duplicate-string`: cannot have same string 3+ times\n\nLet me adjust:\n- Define `DEDUCTIBLE = 100` and `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR = 0.5` as constants.\n- The threshold for high enchantment is 8 — should also be a constant.\n- The threshold for cap application is implicit (Math.min).\n\nLet me redesign:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n  \n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);\n    total += Math.max(0, computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM);\n  }\n  \n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(total, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage entries for \"${type}\" (${damageCount}) exceed insured items (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const { amount } of damages) {\n    if (amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR;\n  }\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    let pool = pools.get(item.type);\n    if (!pool) {\n      pool = [];\n      pools.set(item.type, pool);\n    }\n    pool.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n\nfunction takeItemOfType(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  return pools.get(type)!.shift()!;\n}\n```\n\nWait, `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR` is fine because eslint only complains about magic numbers, not constants. But I have a 0.5 — is that a magic number? Looking at the rule: `\"no-magic-numbers\": [\"error\", { ignore: [0, 1, -1], ignoreArrayIndexes: true }]`. So 0.5 is a magic number. But if it's in a named constant, the rule shouldn't trigger.\n\nLet me check the cognitive complexity. The main `claim` function is pretty simple — just sequence of statements. Should be fine.\n\nLet me check `validateDamageCounts`:\n- A for-of loop\n- An if statement\n- A throw\nThat's simple, cognitive complexity ~2.\n\n`computeReimbursement`:\n- One if-else, return in each branch. Complexity ~2.\n\n`poolItemsByType`:\n- A for-of loop with an if inside. Complexity ~2.\n\nOK these are all simple.\n\nLet me also check line counts:\n- `claim`: ~10 lines, OK\n- `validateDamageCounts`: ~10 lines, OK\n- `validateDamageAmounts`: ~5 lines, OK\n- `computeReimbursement`: ~5 lines, OK\n- `countByType`: ~5 lines, OK\n- `poolItemsByType`: ~7 lines, OK\n- `takeItemOfType`: ~3 lines, OK\n\nAll under 30. \n\nLet me also check `max-depth`: 3. None of my functions have nested control flow beyond simple ifs. OK.\n\nNow let me also check `sonarjs/no-duplicate-string`. Let me look for repeated strings:\n- \"Damage entries for\" - used once\n- \"Damage amount cannot be negative:\" - used once\n- Error messages generally different\n\nShould be OK.\n\nNow let me also think about whether the non-null assertions `!` are good. TypeScript with strict mode might complain about non-null assertions in some contexts, but they're allowed.\n\nActually wait, `pools.get(type)!.shift()!` — this mutates and returns. The `!` says \"trust me, it's not null\". After validation, this is true. But ESLint might complain about non-null assertion. Let me check... actually, the eslint config doesn't disable non-null assertions explicitly, so it should be fine.\n\nActually let me re-check my approach. The user wants a clean implementation. Let me write it out and test.\n\nOne more thought: should I make the destructuring `{ amount }` in `validateDamageAmounts`? It's slightly more readable but adds a tiny bit of indirection. Let me keep it.\n\nActually, on reflection, I'll use `damage.amount` directly for consistency with the rest of the code:\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nEither way works. Let me use the destructuring for slight clarity.\n\nOK let me finalize and write the code.\n\nActually one more consideration: I have `item.enchantment ?? 0`. This is to handle items without an enchantment field. But the eslint rule `sonarjs/no-unused-collection` and `no-magic-numbers`... wait, `?? 0` uses 0 which is in the ignore list. Good.\n\nLet me also verify the count check handles unknown types correctly. If damages has `{ itemType: \"unknownType\" }` and policy doesn't have any \"unknownType\":\n- damageCounts: { unknownType: 1 }\n- For \"unknownType\": policyCount = 0, damageCount = 1, 1 > 0 -> throw ✓\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me also think about validation order. The user said:\n1. Count damage entries (handles unknown types AND too many of one type)\n2. Validate amounts\n3. Validate item types\n\nBut step 1 and 3 are essentially the same check (unknown type has count 0 in policy). So I just need steps 1 and 2. ✓\n\nOK let me write the code.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand the constraints. The eslint config has strict rules — particularly `no-magic-numbers` (only 0, 1, -1 allowed) and `max-lines-per-function` (30 lines). Let me implement the real claim processing.\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- Current `claim` function name fits perfectly — it processes claims.\n- The function's purpose is now clear: real claim processing (not just a stub).\n- I'll need helper functions; will name them by their purpose.\n\n**Current Mass (stub)**:\n- ~6 (1 invocation for return, 2 constants 400, 1 binding _damages, 1 binding policy, etc.)\n\nLet me write the clean implementation:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4f7072001189401f8e1dab08","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"import type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\n// Per-claim flat deductible (MHPCO business rule).\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100;\n\n// Enchantment at or above this threshold triggers the 50% reimbursement rule.\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n\n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n\n  // Cap never exceeded; round down to favor MHPCO.\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage entries for \"${type}\" (${damageCount}) exceed insured items (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Per-item reimbursement BEFORE the per-claim deductible.\n// Enchantment >= 8 wins; otherwise the item is reimbursed in full\n// (this also covers the dragon-material clause, which grants full reimbursement).\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR;\n  }\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    let pool = pools.get(item.type);\n    if (!pool) {\n      pool = [];\n      pools.set(item.type, pool);\n    }\n    pool.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n\n// Validation has already guaranteed a matching, unused item exists.\nfunction takeItemOfType(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  return pools.get(type)!.shift()!;\n}\n","path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":544,"output":2624,"cacheRead":21376,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1673,"totalTokens":24544,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948044430,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-84038f06-5f84-47c5-9e94-faf612bdb7d0","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4f7072001189401f8e1dab08","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 2861 bytes to /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948112454},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me run the tests and lint check to verify everything is working.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_69780d13d57f4f41b5161a7f","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -30"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0b8d14aaea67443ebefe127b","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"npx tsc --noEmit 2>&1 | tail -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":167,"output":85,"cacheRead":24448,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":16,"totalTokens":24700,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948112455,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c5b82938-a244-43ca-947d-fafb160d8590","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_69780d13d57f4f41b5161a7f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.