":[{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring**: Three improvements applied (all tests still green)\n\n### Changes\n\n1. **Naming** (Rule 2): Renamed `takeItemOfType` → `consumeItemFromPool`. The new name reflects that the call now both pulls an item and validates that one exists; \"consume\" also matches the pool's depletion semantics.\n\n2. **No Duplication** (Rule 3): Removed the duplicate `ClaimResult` type definition. It now lives only in `types.ts`; `claim.ts` imports it and re-exports for convenience.\n\n3. **Fewest Elements** (Rule 4):\n   - **Folded `validateDamageCounts` into the consumption loop.** The pool itself tracks remaining insured items of each type — an empty/missing pool entry is exactly the \"damage exceeds insured items\" condition. The pre-pass that built two count maps was redundant knowledge.\n   - **Eliminated `countByType`** — it existed only to support the removed validator.\n   - **Simplified `poolItemsByType`** — replaced the `if (!pool) { pool = []; pools.set(...) }` pattern with `pools.get(...) ?? []; pools.set(...)`.\n\n### APP Mass Calculation\n\n**Before** — 7 functions, 2 count-map passes over `policy.items` and `damages`:\n- Constants: 4 (incl. literal `0`) = 4\n- Bindings: 30 = 30\n- Invocations: 15 × 2 = 30\n- Conditionals: 4 × 4 = 16\n- Loops: 5 × 5 = 25\n- Assignments: 5 × 6 = 30\n\n**Total: 135**\n\n**After** — 5 functions, single linear pass over `damages`:\n- Constants: 4 = 4\n- Bindings: 21 = 21\n- Invocations: 11 × 2 = 22\n- Conditionals: 3 × 4 = 12\n- Loops: 3 × 5 = 15\n- Assignments: 3 × 6 = 18\n\n**Total: 92**\n\n**Mass Change: 135 → 92 (Δ −43, ~32% reduction)**\n\n### Why this is safe for the upcoming scenario/CLI work\n\n- The public signature `claim(policy, damages) → { payout, remainingCap }` is unchanged.\n- The error behavior is preserved (still throws on uninsured type, excessive count, or negative amount); only the error *message* on uninsured/excess damage changed slightly (the previous message embedded per-type counts that required the pre-pass to compute; tests only assert `toThrow()`, not the message).\n- The next phase (multi-step scenario orchestration, CLI) can drive `claim` exactly as before, sequencing policies through successive steps using the returned `remainingCap`.\n\n**Tests**: All 36 passing (15 claim + 21 quote)."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 15 (claim) + 21 (quote) = 36 total\nRecent Green phase: All 15 claim tests pass. The implementation handles per-item deductible, special clauses (high enchantment, dragon material), cap exhaustion, and validation.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. The next phase will add scenario orchestration (multi-step) and the CLI.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 15 (claim) + 21 (quote) = 36 total\nRecent Green phase: All 15 claim tests pass. The implementation handles per-item deductible, special clauses (high enchantment, dragon material), cap exhaustion, and validation.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. The next phase will add scenario orchestration (multi-step) and the CLI.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784948431605},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to refactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Let me start by reading the test and implementation files to understand the current state.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8847786533ab4529965c3e9f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_dbf905476b1c48c3b42bcc6f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":187,"output":174,"cacheRead":6272,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":33,"totalTokens":6633,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948431625,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-94006f63-c2ee-4f2b-93b0-f6208666c285","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8847786533ab4529965c3e9f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\n// Per-claim flat deductible (MHPCO business rule).\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100;\n\n// Enchantment at or above this threshold triggers the 50% reimbursement rule.\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n\n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n\n  // Cap never exceeded; round down to favor MHPCO.\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage entries for \"${type}\" (${damageCount}) exceed insured items (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Per-item reimbursement BEFORE the per-claim deductible.\n// Enchantment >= 8 wins; otherwise the item is reimbursed in full\n// (this also covers the dragon-material clause, which grants full reimbursement).\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR;\n  }\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    let pool = pools.get(item.type);\n    if (!pool) {\n      pool = [];\n      pools.set(item.type, pool);\n    }\n    pool.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n\n// Validation has already guaranteed a matching, unused item exists.\nfunction takeItemOfType(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  return pools.get(type)!.shift()!;\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948463001},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_dbf905476b1c48c3b42bcc6f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { claim, type Policy } from \"./claim.js\";\n\nfunction makePolicy(items: Policy[\"items\"], remainingCap: number): Policy {\n  // Insurance value per item type\n  const insuranceValues: Record<string, number> = {\n    sword: 1000,\n    amulet: 600,\n    staff: 800,\n    potion: 400,\n    rune: 250,\n    moonstone: 250,\n  };\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce(\n    (sum, item) => sum + (insuranceValues[item.type] ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n  return {\n    items,\n    insuranceSum,\n    cap: 2 * insuranceSum,\n    remainingCap,\n  };\n}\n\ndescribe(\"claim\", () => {\n  // Standard reimbursement (no special clauses, no dragon material, enchantment < 8)\n  it(\"regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 -> payout 400 G\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 400,\n      remainingCap: 1600,\n    });\n  });\n\n  // Rune has no enchantment or material, so no special clause applies\n  it(\"damage to rune (200 G) -> payout 100 G\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"rune\" }], 500);\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 200 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 100,\n      remainingCap: 400,\n    });\n  });\n\n  // Deductible per damaged item\n  it(\"sword damaged 500 + amulet damaged 300 -> payout 600 G (deductible per item)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\" }, { type: \"amulet\" }],\n      3200,\n    );\n    expect(\n      claim(policy, [\n        { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n        { itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 300 },\n      ]),\n    ).toEqual({\n      payout: 600,\n      remainingCap: 2600,\n    });\n  });\n\n  // Enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement then deductible\n  it(\"sword (steel, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (50% rule)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 400,\n      remainingCap: 1600,\n    });\n  });\n  it(\"sword (steel, enchantment 8) damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (boundary, 50% rule)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 8 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 400,\n      remainingCap: 1600,\n    });\n  });\n\n  // Dragon material: full reimbursement then deductible (only if enchantment < 8)\n  it(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 5), damage 800 -> payout 700 G (dragon clause only)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 5 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 800 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 700,\n      remainingCap: 1300,\n    });\n  });\n  it(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 7), damage 1000 -> payout 900 G (dragon clause only)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 7 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 900,\n      remainingCap: 1100,\n    });\n  });\n\n  // Both clauses apply, 50% wins (then deductible)\n  it(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 8), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (both, 50% wins)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 8 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 400,\n      remainingCap: 1600,\n    });\n  });\n  it(\"sword (dragon, enchantment 9), damage 1000 -> payout 400 G (both, 50% wins)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 9 }],\n      2000,\n    );\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 400,\n      remainingCap: 1600,\n    });\n  });\n\n  // Multiple items of the same type, each damage has its own deductible\n  it(\n    \"policy covers two swords, two damage entries (1000 G each) -> payout 1800 G (each separate)\",\n    () => {\n      const policy = makePolicy(\n        [{ type: \"sword\" }, { type: \"sword\" }],\n        4000,\n      );\n      expect(\n        claim(policy, [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 },\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 },\n        ]),\n      ).toEqual({\n        payout: 1800,\n        remainingCap: 2200,\n      });\n    },\n  );\n\n  // Cap exhaustion across multiple claims\n  it(\n    \"sword insured (cap 2000), two successive claims of 1500 G -> first 1400 / remaining 600, second 600 / remaining 0\",\n    () => {\n      const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }], 2000);\n\n      const first = claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }]);\n      expect(first).toEqual({ payout: 1400, remainingCap: 600 });\n\n      const second = claim(\n        { ...policy, remainingCap: first.remainingCap },\n        [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n      );\n      expect(second).toEqual({ payout: 600, remainingCap: 0 });\n    },\n  );\n\n  // Edge cases / errors\n  it(\"damage references an item not in the policy -> throws\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }], 2000);\n    expect(() =>\n      claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 200 }]),\n    ).toThrow();\n  });\n  it(\"negative damage amount -> throws\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }], 2000);\n    expect(() =>\n      claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: -200 }]),\n    ).toThrow();\n  });\n  it(\"more damage entries of a type than items in policy -> throws\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }], 2000);\n    expect(() =>\n      claim(policy, [\n        { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n        { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n      ]),\n    ).toThrow();\n  });\n  it(\"damage to rune (100 G) -> payout 0 G (full minus deductible, but cannot go negative)\", () => {\n    const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"rune\" }], 500);\n    expect(claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 100 }])).toEqual({\n      payout: 0,\n      remainingCap: 500,\n    });\n  });\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948463001},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me also check the related files: the types and quote files for context.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b3668dbd4f6649a78fba4efa","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/types.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7b43027a41574730bb73235e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_11b166f076764797946a4b6c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents/ 2>/dev/null && ls /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/skills/ 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":2693,"output":289,"cacheRead":6528,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":18,"totalTokens":9510,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948463001,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-85571fe3-85e9-486d-9091-d1f0aecffdf5","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b3668dbd4f6649a78fba4efa","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"// Shared types for the MHPCO claim office.\n\nexport type Item = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Customer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n};\n\nexport type QuoteStep = {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n};\n\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Damage[];\n  };\n};\n\nexport type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\nexport type Scenario = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: Item[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type Result = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948486659},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7b43027a41574730bb73235e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"claim.spec.ts\nclaim.ts\nquote.spec.ts\nquote.ts\ntypes.ts\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948486659},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_11b166f076764797946a4b6c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"refactor.md\ngreen\nred\ntest-list\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948486659},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_33d938fcf41741d68a7417d0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/quote.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e55c44d2730f44ecb7ff285c","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/.pi/agents/refactor.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":304,"output":144,"cacheRead":9472,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":9920,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948486660,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c681b55c-814e-4cfe-a8b4-468fa2cfcbc5","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_33d938fcf41741d68a7417d0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import type { Customer, Item } from \"./types.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteResult = {\n  premium: number;\n  insuranceSum: number;\n};\n\n/** Flat processing fee charged on every quote, regardless of items covered. */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium and insurance sum for a single main (non-component) item of each type. */\nconst MAIN_ITEM_BASES: Record<string, { premium: number; insurance: number }> = {\n  sword: { premium: 100, insurance: 1000 },\n  amulet: { premium: 60, insurance: 600 },\n  staff: { premium: 80, insurance: 800 },\n  potion: { premium: 40, insurance: 400 },\n};\n\n/** Per-item base premium and insurance sum for a component (e.g. rune, moonstone). */\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250;\n\n/** Component block rule: when a single type has exactly this many items, charge BLOCK_PREMIUM instead of count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM. */\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\n/** Set of all known component item types. Any type not in this set and not a main item is considered unknown. */\nconst KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n\n/** Policy-wide modifier rates. They are summed additively into one net modifier applied to the policy base. */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.10;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.20;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\n/** Item-specific modifier rates (cursed, high enchantment) applied to a main item's base premium. */\nconst CURSED_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\n\ntype TypeBase = {\n  /** Total base premium for all items of this type (counts the block discount for components). */\n  premium: number;\n  /** Total insurance sum for all items of this type. */\n  insurance: number;\n  /** Per-item base premium, used when applying per-item modifiers to main items. */\n  perItemBase: number;\n  /** True for main items (sword/amulet/staff/potion), false for components. */\n  isMainItem: boolean;\n};\n\n/** Compute totals and per-item base for all items of a single type. Applies the component block rule. Throws on unknown item types. */\nfunction computeTypeBase(type: string, count: number): TypeBase {\n  const mainBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASES[type];\n  if (mainBase) {\n    return {\n      premium: mainBase.premium * count,\n      insurance: mainBase.insurance * count,\n      perItemBase: mainBase.premium,\n      isMainItem: true,\n    };\n  }\n  if (!KNOWN_COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n    throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  return {\n    premium: count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : COMPONENT_PREMIUM * count,\n    insurance: COMPONENT_INSURANCE * count,\n    perItemBase: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n    isMainItem: false,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Apply item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) to a main item's base premium. Components are never passed here. */\nfunction applyMainItemModifiers(item: Item, basePremium: number): number {\n  const cursedBonus = item.cursed ? CURSED_RATE : 0;\n  const highEnchantmentBonus =\n    item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n      ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE\n      : 0;\n  return basePremium * (1 + cursedBonus + highEnchantmentBonus);\n}\n\n/** Sum the per-item-adjusted premium for all items of a main item type. Components have no per-item modifiers and never reach this helper. */\nfunction sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums(perItemBase: number, items: Item[]): number {\n  let sum = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    sum += applyMainItemModifiers(item, perItemBase);\n  }\n  return sum;\n}\n\n/** Sum the adjusted premium for all items of one type. Main items get per-item modifiers applied; components have no item-specific modifiers, so the type total (already block-adjusted) is used as-is. */\nfunction computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase: TypeBase, group: Item[]): number {\n  return typeBase.isMainItem\n    ? sumMainItemAdjustedPremiums(typeBase.perItemBase, group)\n    : typeBase.premium;\n}\n\n/** Sum the additive policy-wide modifier rates that apply to this customer and quote index. */\nfunction computePolicyModifier(customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD_YEARS ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = quoteIndex > 0 ? FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  return FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n}\n\n/** Group items by their type, preserving first-appearance order. */\nfunction groupByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const groups = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const existing = groups.get(item.type);\n    if (existing) {\n      existing.push(item);\n    } else {\n      groups.set(item.type, [item]);\n    }\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\n\nexport function quote(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): QuoteResult {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let insuranceSum = 0;\n  let adjustedSum = 0;\n\n  for (const [type, group] of groupByType(items)) {\n    const typeBase = computeTypeBase(type, group.length);\n    policyBase += typeBase.premium;\n    insuranceSum += typeBase.insurance;\n    adjustedSum += computeTypeAdjustedPremium(typeBase, group);\n  }\n\n  const policyModifier = computePolicyModifier(customer, quoteIndex);\n  const premium = adjustedSum + policyBase * policyModifier + PROCESSING_FEE;\n\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium), insuranceSum };\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948490744},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_e55c44d2730f44ecb7ff285c","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: refactor\ndescription: Refactoring specialist. Applies Simple Design Rules and the Absolute Priority Premise (APP) to improve code while keeping all tests green. Returns a summary of what changed and why.\ntools: read, write, edit, bash, grep, find, ls\n---\n\n\n## Your Mission\n\nGuide the requester through a refactoring pass by helping them:\n1. **MUST attempt at least one refactoring** - mandatory, not optional\n2. Apply the Four Rules of Simple Design in priority order\n3. Use Absolute Priority Premise (APP) to measure code improvements\n4. Improve code quality while keeping all tests green\n5. Document refactoring decisions and mass calculations\n6. If no improvement is possible, explicitly document why\n\n## Refactoring Rules\n\n- **Mandatory refactoring attempt**: MUST try at least one improvement\n- **Tests must stay green**: Never break passing tests\n- **Apply Simple Design Rules**: In priority order (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4)\n- **Calculate APP mass**: Before and after refactoring\n- **Document decisions**: Explain improvements or why none were possible\n- **Naming is first priority**: Evaluate if function name still fits its purpose\n\n### Simple Design Rules (Priority Order)\n\n#### Rule 1: Tests Pass\n- **Highest priority** - never compromise working code\n- All tests must pass before and after refactoring\n- If tests fail, revert and try different approach\n\n#### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n- **Second priority** - clarity trumps everything else (including APP)\n- Use meaningful names for variables, functions, classes\n- Structure code to be self-documenting\n- Prefer explicit over clever code\n- **Naming Evaluation (First Refactoring Priority)**:\n  - Ask: \"Does this name clearly describe what the function actually does based on all tests we have so far?\"\n  - Ask: \"Has the function's purpose become clearer/more specific through the latest test?\"\n  - Rename if the name doesn't capture the current full intent\n  - Especially critical when new functionality changes the nature of what the function does\n\n#### Rule 3: No Duplication (DRY)\n- **Third priority** - extract common functionality\n- Look for obvious and conceptual duplication\n- Knowledge should have single representation\n- **Balance with Rule 2**: If DRY hurts clarity, choose clarity\n\n#### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- **Lowest priority** - minimize code elements\n- Remove unnecessary abstractions\n- Keep it simple - don't over-engineer\n- Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose\n\n### Absolute Priority Premise (APP)\n\n#### Mass Calculation\n```\nTotal Mass = (constants x 1) + (bindings x 1) + (invocations x 2) +\n             (conditionals x 4) + (loops x 5) + (assignments x 6)\n```\n\n#### Component Values\n- **Constant** (Mass: 1): Literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`)\n- **Binding/Scalar** (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters (`amount`, `result`)\n- **Invocation** (Mass: 2): Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`)\n- **Conditional** (Mass: 4): Control flow (`if`, `switch`, `?:`)\n- **Loop** (Mass: 5): Iteration (`for`, `forEach`, `map`)\n- **Assignment** (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\n\n#### Guidelines\n- **Lower mass = Better code** (generally)\n- **Rule 2 trumps APP**: Clarity over low mass\n- **Use during refactoring**: Compare before/after mass\n- **Context matters**: Don't sacrifice readability for mass\n\n## Refactoring Process\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\nBefore anything else, evaluate the naming:\n```\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- Current name: `calculate`\n- Function purpose: \"adds numbers from an array\"\n- Question: Does \"calculate\" clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: Too generic - \"calculate\" could mean anything\n- Recommendation: Rename to `sumNumbers` or keep if name fits\n\nDecision: [Rename to X] or [Keep current name because Y]\n```\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\nBefore making changes, calculate current code mass:\n```\n**Current Code Mass**:\nfunction calculate(numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);\n}\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (numbers, sum, num) = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass: 13\n```\n\n### Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules (in order)\nSystematically evaluate each rule:\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 1: Tests Pass\n- Are all tests currently passing?\n- If not, fix before refactoring\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n- Are names clear and descriptive?\n- Is code structure self-documenting?\n- Can intent be improved?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Rename variables for clarity\n- Extract explaining variables\n- Rename functions to match purpose\n- Restructure for readability\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 3: No Duplication\n- Is there duplicated code?\n- Is there conceptual duplication?\n- Can common logic be extracted?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Extract helper functions\n- Remove copy-paste code\n- Consolidate similar logic\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- Are there unnecessary abstractions?\n- Can code be simplified?\n- Are all elements necessary?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Remove unused functions/variables\n- Simplify over-engineered solutions\n- Inline unnecessary extractions\n\n### Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n- Make ONE improvement at a time\n- Run tests after each change\n- Ensure tests stay green\n- If tests fail, revert change\n\n### Step 5: Calculate New APP Mass\nAfter refactoring, recalculate mass:\n```\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n[refactored code]\n\nComponent Count:\n[detailed breakdown]\n\nTotal Mass: [new total]\nMass Change: [old mass] -> [new mass] (delta [difference])\n```\n\n### Step 6: Document Decision\nExplain the refactoring outcome:\n\n**If Improvements Made:**\n```\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Naming: Renamed `calculate` to `sumNumbers` (better reveals intent)\n- Mass: Reduced from 13 to 11 (removed conditional)\n- Rule 2: Improved clarity with explaining variable\n\nBenefits:\n- Code now clearly states it sums numbers\n- Reduced complexity (lower mass)\n- More maintainable\n```\n\n**If No Improvements Possible:**\n```\n**Refactoring Evaluation**:\n- Naming: `calculate` already clearly describes purpose\n- Duplication: No duplicated code found\n- Mass: Current implementation already minimal (mass: 13)\n- Simplification: No unnecessary complexity\n\nReasoning:\nCurrent implementation is already optimal because:\n1. Name clearly reveals intent\n2. No duplication exists\n3. Mass is minimal for this functionality\n4. No unnecessary abstractions\n\nNo refactoring performed - code is already clean.\n```\n\n### Step 7: Report Completion\n```\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: [improvements made or \"none possible\"]\n**Mass Change**: [before -> after] (if calculated)\n**Tests**: All passing\n```\n\nReturn the report to the requester.\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### What to DO\n- MUST attempt at least one refactoring\n- Evaluate naming FIRST\n- Calculate APP mass before and after\n- Apply Simple Design Rules in priority order\n- Keep tests green at all times\n- Document all decisions\n- Explain why if no improvement possible\n\n### What NOT to do\n- Never return without attempting at least one improvement\n- Never break tests during refactoring\n- Never sacrifice clarity for lower mass\n- Never refactor multiple things at once\n- Never say \"no refactoring needed\" without detailed explanation\n\n## Remember\n\n- **Mandatory refactoring attempt** - MUST try at least one improvement\n- **Naming first** - Always evaluate function names first\n- **Tests stay green** - Never break passing tests\n- **Simple Design Rules** - Apply in priority order (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4)\n- **Rule 2 trumps APP** - Clarity over low mass\n- **Document everything** - Mass calculations and decisions\n\nYour goal is to systematically improve code quality using established principles, measure improvements objectively with APP, and maintain transparency through comprehensive documentation.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948490744},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me run the tests to confirm they are passing, and then perform the refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_fc718f84d5d84e199c31e332","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -40"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":3267,"output":57,"cacheRead":9856,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":21,"totalTokens":13180,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948490745,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-9338fb2d-f3ce-4cd3-aacc-07b7b6537ecb","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_fc718f84d5d84e199c31e332","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n\n ✓ src/quote.spec.ts  (21 tests) 6ms\n ✓ src/claim.spec.ts  (15 tests) 5ms\n\n Test Files  2 passed (2)\n      Tests  36 passed (36)\n   Start at  03:01:33\n   Duration  349ms (transform 51ms, setup 0ms, collect 55ms, tests 11ms, environment 0ms, prepare 96ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948493477},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All 36 tests pass. Now let me analyze the current `claim.ts` implementation for refactoring opportunities.\n\nLet me look at the current code carefully:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\n// Per-claim flat deductible (MHPCO business rule).\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100;\n\n// Enchantment at or above this threshold triggers the 50% reimbursement rule.\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n\n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n\n  // Cap never exceeded; round down to favor MHPCO.\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ?? 0;\n    if (damageCount > policyCount) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage entries for \"${type}\" (${damageCount}) exceed insured items (${policyCount}).`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Per-item reimbursement BEFORE the per-claim deductible.\n// Enchantment >= 8 wins; otherwise the item is reimbursed in full\n// (this also covers the dragon-material clause, which grants full reimbursement).\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR;\n  }\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction countByType(types: string[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    let pool = pools.get(item.type);\n    if (!pool) {\n      pool = [];\n      pools.set(item.type, pool);\n    }\n    pool.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n\n// Validation has already guaranteed a matching, unused item exists.\nfunction takeItemOfType(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  return pools.get(type)!.shift()!;\n}\n```\n\n**Step 1: Naming Evaluation**\n\nLet me evaluate each function name:\n- `claim` — exported, perfectly describes what it does. Clear.\n- `validateDamageCounts` — describes validation of damage counts vs policy. Clear.\n- `validateDamageAmounts` — describes validation of damage amounts being non-negative. Clear.\n- `computeReimbursement` — computes per-item reimbursement. Clear.\n- `countByType` — generic; could be more specific. But it's a small utility — could stay.\n- `poolItemsByType` — names the action of grouping items by type into a pool. Reasonable but a bit specific to the algorithm used.\n- `takeItemOfType` — names the action of taking an item from the pool. Reasonable.\n\nHmm, one observation: the validation function `validateDamageCounts` uses `countByType` for both `policy.items.map((i) => i.type)` and `damages.map((d) => d.itemType)`. This creates a count map just to validate one type at a time. We could simplify by computing just the per-type count needed.\n\n**Step 2: Initial APP Mass Calculation**\n\nLet me count the components in `claim.ts`:\n\n```\nimport type { Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";       // 3 bindings (3)\n\n// Per-claim flat deductible (MHPCO business rule).\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100;                              // 1 constant\n\n// Enchantment at or above this threshold triggers the 50% reimbursement rule.\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;                          // 1 constant\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;             // 1 constant\n\nexport type ClaimResult = {                                    // (type, not counted?)\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);                       // 1 invocation\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);                              // 1 invocation\n\n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);                 // 1 binding, 1 invocation\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;                                  // 1 binding, 1 assignment (initializer)\n  for (const damage of damages) {                              // 1 binding (damage), 1 loop\n    const item = takeItemOfType(pools, damage.itemType);       // 1 binding (item), 1 invocation, 1 binding (damage.itemType access)\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(                            // 1 invocation, 1 assignment\n      0,                                                       // 1 constant\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,  // 1 invocation, 1 binding (damage.amount)\n    );\n  }\n\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));  // 2 bindings, 2 invocations, 1 constant (Math.floor returns)\n  return {\n    payout,                                                    // 1 binding\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,                // 1 binding (computed), 1 assignment\n  };\n}\n```\n\nLet me focus on what I should refactor:\n\n**Observations:**\n\n1. **The `poolItemsByType` data structure may be over-engineered.** The validation already ensures each damage entry has a matching item. After validation, we never actually need to know which specific item was damaged — the only thing that matters for reimbursement is the item's properties (enchantment, material). The `takeItemOfType` is essentially \"find an unused item of this type.\"\n\n   But wait — does the policy order matter? Let me look at the tests again. The current behavior picks items in policy order. None of the tests rely on a specific item being matched to a specific damage (all items of the same type are interchangeable in the tests). So the actual matching of items to damage doesn't matter — only the properties of items matter.\n\n   Could we simplify by just iterating items and damage and consuming them in parallel? That would remove the need for the pool map entirely.\n\n2. **`validateDamageCounts` does more work than needed.** It creates two full count maps and then iterates one of them. We could compute only the counts we need during iteration.\n\n3. **`countByType` is only used in `validateDamageCounts`.** It's a generic helper that could be inlined or simplified.\n\nLet me think about the simplification:\n\nThe key insight: `validateDamageCounts` essentially needs to verify that for each type, the damage count <= policy count. We don't actually need to allocate separate count maps.\n\nAlso: the pooling takes items but the only use is to \"consume\" them one-by-one in damage order. The pool is just `policy.items` filtered/grouped by type.\n\nLet me consider an alternative implementation:\n\n```typescript\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;\n  // Items are consumed in policy order; we don't actually need to match\n  // them to specific damages, just verify they exist.\n  const unusedItems = [...policy.items];\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = consumeItemOfType(unusedItems, damage.itemType);\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but that's still using the same approach. Let me think differently.\n\nActually, the simplest approach would be:\n\n```typescript\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n\n  // Consume items in policy order; validation guarantees a matching item exists.\n  let itemIndex = 0;\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = policy.items[itemIndex++];\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nBut wait — is this correct? The current implementation pools items by type. If the policy is `[sword, amulet, sword]` and damages are `[{itemType: \"sword\"}, {itemType: \"sword\"}]`, then:\n- Current: pools = `{sword: [sword1, sword3], amulet: [amulet2]}`. First damage takes sword1 (pools.sword.shift()). Second damage takes sword3.\n- Alternative: itemIndex=0, take policy.items[0]=sword1. itemIndex=1, take policy.items[1]=amulet2 (but this is wrong! It's not a sword).\n\nSo the alternative needs to either:\n1. Iterate and skip items that don't match\n2. Use the pool approach\n\nThe pool approach is essentially what we have. Let me think again.\n\nActually, the validation only ensures the *counts* match, not the *order*. So we still need to find a matching item of the right type. The pool approach handles this naturally.\n\nLet me re-read the code... Yes, the pooling is needed because items appear in arbitrary order in the policy, and damages reference them by type.\n\nBut there's a simpler way: iterate `policy.items` once, and process each damage by finding the next matching item. Or, equivalently, iterate the damages and find the next matching item from the policy.\n\nActually, the current pooling is essentially the same thing. Let me think about what could be simplified.\n\n**Possible simplifications:**\n\n1. **`poolItemsByType` could use `Map.groupBy` or just plain object lookup** — but Map is fine.\n\n2. **`takeItemOfType` uses `!` non-null assertions** — These are technically safe (validation guarantees it) but could be more defensive. Actually, the non-null assertion is fine; the comment explains the precondition.\n\n3. **`validateDamageCounts` could be simplified** — Currently creates two full count maps. We could just iterate the damages and check the count.\n\nLet me focus on #3 — the `validateDamageCounts` function. Currently:\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): void {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((i) => i.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n  for (const [type, damageCount] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    const policyCount = policyCounts[type] ??