    const pool = pools.get(damage.itemType);\n    if (!pool || pool.length === 0) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `Damage references \"${damage.itemType}\" not covered by policy.`,\n      );\n    }\n    const item = pool.shift()!;\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThis consolidates validation into the main loop, eliminating `validateDamageCounts` and the double counting. It's also more efficient.\n\nBut the error message would be different. Let me check the tests:\n\n```typescript\nit(\"damage references an item not in the policy -> throws\", () => {\n  const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }], 2000);\n  expect(() =>\n    claim(policy, [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 200 }]),\n  ).toThrow();\n});\nit(\"more damage entries of a type than items in policy -> throws\", () => {\n  const policy = makePolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }], 2000);\n  expect(() =>\n    claim(policy, [\n      { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n      { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n    ]),\n  ).toThrow();\n});\n```\n\nThe tests only check that `claim` throws, not the specific error message. So we have flexibility in the error message.\n\nThis consolidation removes:\n- `validateDamageCounts` function (declaration + 12 lines)\n- `countByType` function (declaration + 7 lines) — since it's only used here\n- The double count-map construction\n\nThe new code:\n- Adds: a conditional check inside the loop\n- Removes: two separate validation passes\n\nLet me check the mass:\n\n**Before (claim + validateDamageCounts + countByType)**:\n- claim: bindings(policy, damages, pools, totalReimbursement, damage, item, payout) = 7; invocations(validateDamageCounts, validateDamageAmounts, poolItemsByType, takeItemOfType, Math.max, co