5 × 6 = 30\n\n**Total: 135**\n\n**After** — 5 functions, single linear pass over `damages`:\n- Constants: 4 = 4\n- Bindings: 21 = 21\n- Invocations: 11 × 2 = 22\n- Conditionals: 3 × 4 = 12\n- Loops: 3 × 5 = 15\n- Assignments: 3 × 6 = 18\n\n**Total: 92**\n\n**Mass Change: 135 → 92 (Δ −43, ~32% reduction)**\n\n### Why this is safe for the upcoming scenario/CLI work\n\n- The public signature `claim(policy, damages) → { payout, remainingCap }` is unchanged.\n- The error behavior is preserved (still throws on uninsured type, excessive count, or negative amount); only the error *message* on uninsured/excess damage changed slightly (the previous message embedded per-type counts that required the pre-pass to compute; tests only assert `toThrow()`, not the message).\n- The next phase (multi-step scenario orchestration, CLI) can drive `claim` exactly as before, sequencing policies through successive steps using the returned `remainingCap`.\n\n**Tests**: All 36 passing (15 claim + 21 quote)."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":274,"output":621,"cacheRead":27648,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":28543,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784948838917,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-a7371901-9a33-4120-894e-abdfb505f0cc","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"}],"stderr":"","usage":{"input":10192,"output":13209,"cacheRead":334208,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0,"contextTokens":28543,"turns":17},"model":"requesty/tensorx/minimax-m3","stopReason":"stop"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948857855},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_870efbf140844320abd0a4ea","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-55_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/claim.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":688,"output":74,"cacheRead":110720,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":111482,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948857860,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-2ac930dd-516a-4a47-bb25-8e127547f421","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_870efbf140844320abd0a4ea","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import type { ClaimResult, Damage, Item, Policy } from \"./types.js\";\n\n// Per-claim flat deductible (MHPCO business rule).\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM = 100;\n\n// Enchantment at or above this threshold triggers the 50% reimbursement rule.\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\nexport type { ClaimResult };\n\nexport function claim(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): ClaimResult {\n  validateDamageAmounts(damages);\n\n  const pools = poolItemsByType(policy.items);\n  let totalReimbursement = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = consumeItemFromPool(pools, damage.itemType);\n    totalReimbursement += Math.max(\n      0,\n      computeReimbursement(item, damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE_PER_CLAIM,\n    );\n  }\n\n  // Cap never exceeded; round down to favor MHPCO.\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalReimbursement, policy.remainingCap));\n  return {\n    payout,\n    remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageAmounts(damages: Damage[]): void {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Per-item reimbursement BEFORE the per-claim deductible.\n// Enchantment >= 8 wins; otherwise the item is reimbursed in full\n// (this also covers the dragon-material clause, which grants full reimbursement).\nfunction computeReimbursement(item: Item, amount: number): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_FACTOR;\n  }\n  return amount;\n}\n\nfunction poolItemsByType(items: Item[]): Map<string, Item[]> {\n  const pools = new Map<string, Item[]>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const pool = pools.get(item.type) ?? [];\n    pools.set(item.type, pool);\n    pool.push(item);\n  }\n  return pools;\n}\n\n// Pool size tracks remaining insured items of each type, so an empty/missing\n// pool entry means a damage references an uninsured item or one already consumed.\nfunction consumeItemFromPool(pools: Map<string, Item[]>, type: string): Item {\n  const pool = pools.get(type);\n  if (!pool || pool.length === 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references \"${type}\" beyond insured items.`);\n  }\n  return pool.shift()!;\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948862403},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now let me think about the scenario orchestration and CLI. I need to:\n1. Create a scenario.ts that processes multi-step scenarios\n2. Create a cli.ts that reads JSON from stdin and writes JSON to stdout\n\nLet me create tests for the scenario orchestrator first.\n\nActually, let me also create tests for the CLI to verify it reads stdin/writes stdout correctly.\n\nThe scenarios work like this:\n- Quote step creates a policy (stored by step index)\n- Claim step references a policy by step index\n- The scenario tracks all policies\n\nLet me write the scenario tests.\n\nWait, looking at the spec, the scenarios include:\n- A single scenario with multiple steps\n- The customer's quoteIndex (for follow-up contract) is determined by the number of quotes in the scenario\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Walk through steps\n2. Track which step index corresponds to each quote (since claims reference quote steps by index)\n3. For each quote step, calculate the quoteIndex (0-based count of quotes for this customer)\n4. For each claim step, look up the policy by step index and process the claim\n\nLet me design the API:\n\n```typescript\nfunction processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: Result[] };\n```\n\nThis returns the results array.\n\nNow let me also think about the CLI. The CLI:\n1. Reads JSON from stdin\n2. Calls processScenario\n3. Writes JSON results to stdout\n4. On error, writes to stderr and exits with non-zero code\n\nLet me create the scenario and CLI.\n\nActually, looking at the test setup, the tests I have so far cover the core logic. The scenario orchestrator is glue code. The CLI is also glue.\n\nLet me write the scenario orchestrator and tests, then the CLI and tests.\n\nFor the scenario orchestrator tests, I want to test:\n1. Quote followed by claim - works correctly\n2. Multiple quotes with follow-up contract discount on 2nd\n3. Multi-step scenario from the spec example (customer 5 years, amulet, then claim)\n\nLet me create a new spec file for the scenario.\n\nHmm wait, I should think about whether to add new tests at all. The prompt says I should keep going until all tests in the test list are implemented. I've already done 36 tests covering all the rules and examples.\n\nBut the kata also requires a CLI. Let me check what counts as \"all tests\" - the test list I created was 36 tests, all of which are passing.\n\nThe spec requires:\n- Source files implementing the logic and the `claim-office` CLI - need CLI\n- Test files - have these\n- CLI entry point at `src/cli.ts` - need this\n\nSo I still need to create:\n1. src/cli.ts\n2. Maybe src/scenario.ts as a helper\n\nAnd maybe tests for these.\n\nActually, the prompt says \"Continue autonomously through ALL tests in the test list until you have written experiment-done.txt\". My test list had 36 tests. All 36 pass.\n\nBut the spec says the implementation must include a CLI. Let me create the CLI and scenario orchestrator (which doesn't have separate tests but is needed).\n\nActually let me add tests for the scenario orchestrator and CLI as well. The TDD workflow says tests should drive the design. The spec mentions scenarios that span multiple steps.\n\nLet me write:\n1. src/scenario.ts - processScenario function\n2. src/scenario.spec.ts - tests for processScenario\n3. src/cli.ts - CLI entry point\n4. src/cli.spec.ts - tests for CLI\n\nThen write experiment-done.txt.\n\nActually, since the spec says the CLI is the main interface, let me make sure to test it properly.\n\nLet me proceed with scenario.ts and tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me create the scenario orchestrator and CLI.\n\n## Red -- Test 37: scenario with quote then claim"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6b83a5c4e8ae444e8ce16d10","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { processScenario } from \"./scenario.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"processScenario\", () => {\n  it(\"quote then claim produces correct results (spec example)\", () => {\n    const scenario = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 5 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\" as const,\n          items: [\n            {\n              type: \"amulet\",\n              material: \"silver\",\n              enchantment: 2,\n              cursed: false,\n            },\n          ],\n        },\n        {\n          op: \"claim\" as const,\n          policy: 0,\n          incident: {\n            cause: \"fire\",\n            damages: [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 200 }],\n          },\n        },\n      ],\n    };\n    const { results } = processScenario(scenario);\n    expect(results).toHaveLength(2);\n    expect(results[0]).toEqual({ premium: expect.any(Number) });\n    expect(results[1]).toMatchObject({\n      payout: expect.any(Number),\n      remainingCap: expect.any(Number),\n    });\n  });\n\n  it(\"multi-step scenario with two quotes (2nd is follow-up contract) and a claim\", () => {\n    const scenario = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 3 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\" as const,\n          items: [{ type: \