mbursement: ✓\n- Both clauses (50% wins): ✓\n- Multiple damages: ✓\n- Cap exhaustion: ✓\n- Insurance sum calculation: ✓\n- Empty item list: ✓\n- Unknown item type: ✓\n- Damage not in policy: ✓\n- Negative damage amount: ✓\n- More damages than items: ✓\n- CLI: ✓\n- Multi-step scenarios: ✓\n\nLet me also verify the integration examples:\n\nIntegration example 1 (newcomer with cursed sword):\n- 0 years, no previous contract, item cursed sword (steel, enchant 3)\n- Premium: 165 G\n- 100 base + 50 curse + 10 first insurance + 5 fee = 165 ✓\n\nIntegration example 2 (long-standing customer's 2nd contract):\n- 3 years, 2nd quote, item cursed sword (steel, enchant 7)\n- Premium: 160 G\n- 100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment - 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance - 15 follow-up + 5 fee = 160 ✓\n\nBoth verified!\n\nLet me now also check the cap exhaustion example:\n- Sword insured, cap 2000, two claims of 1500 each\n- First: payout 1400, remaining 600\n- Second: payout 600, remaining 0\n- This is covered in test 32.\n\nLet me also add a few more tests for edge cases that I might have missed:\n\nWait, let me also re-check the insurance sum for components. The spec says: \"Components — for example runes and moonstones — are insured at 250 G each\". So each rune = 250 G insurance.\n\nMy implementation has COMPONENT_INSURANCE = 250. Good.\n\nFor a 3-rune block: insurance sum = 3 * 250 = 750 G. Cap = 1500 G. ✓\n\nLet me also verify the dragon-material example: \"a cursed sword (insurance value 1000 G, premium with modifiers 165 G) → cap 2000 G (based on the unmodified insurance value; premium modifiers do not raise the cap)\"\n\nThis means cap is based on insurance value, not on premium. My implementation: cap = 2 * insuranceSum. ✓\n\nOK I think the implementation is comprehensive. Let me also add a couple of additional tests for the multi-item cap scenario to be thorough.\n\nActually, let me also add a test for the \"sword + amulet\" insurance sum example from the spec:\n\"a policy covers a sword and an amulet → insurance sum 1600 G (= 1000 + 600, the sum of the items' insurance values), cap 3200 G\"\n\nThis is more of a documentation/test for the insurance sum computation. My scenario test #37 doesn't verify this directly. Let me add it.\n\nActually, I think 42 tests is comprehensive enough. Let me make sure all tests pass and write the experiment-done.txt.\n\nBut wait, the TDD workflow requires refactor after green. Let me do one more refactor pass for the scenario and CLI.\n\nActually let me skip the refactor for now and just run all tests to make sure everything passes. Then write experiment-done.txt.\n\nActually, I should add some more tests to make sure I cover edge cases. Let me think about what else needs testing:\n\n1. Two swords cap: insurance sum 2000, cap 4000 (spec example)\n2. Sword + 3 runes (block) insurance sum: 1750 (spec example)\n3. Multi-step scenario with cap exhaustion across claims\n\nLet me add a couple of these.\n\nActually, looking at the spec test cases I have, I think I have comprehensive coverage. Let me move to refactoring.\n\nLet me think about whether to add more tests. The spec explicitly mentions:\n- \"a policy covers two swords → insurance sum 2000 G (= 2×1000), cap 4000 G\" - test needed\n- \"a policy covers a sword and an amulet → insurance sum 1600 G (= 1000 + 600, the sum of the items' insurance values), cap 3200 G\" - test needed\n- \"a policy covers a sword and 3 runes (a block) → insurance sum 1750 G (= 1000 + 3×250); the block discount affects the premium only, not the insurance sum\" - test needed\n\nThese verify insuranceSum calculation. Let me add them.\n\nActually I can verify these through my quote() function. Let me add a few quote tests for these.\n\nHmm, but my test list is already comprehensive. Let me focus on the highest-impact additions and not bloat.\n\nLet me add:\n1. Two swords insurance sum 2000, cap 4000\n2. Sword + amulet insurance sum 1600, cap 3200\n3. Sword + 3 runes insurance 