     processClaimStep(step, policies, results);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but this adds more elements. The extracted functions need to know about the mutable policies and results arrays, which complicates their signatures. Rule 4 says fewest elements, so this might not be a clear win.\n\nActually, I think the inline version is clear enough. Let me focus on the smaller wins.\n\n**Final Refactoring Plan**:\n\n1. **scenario.ts**:\n   - Remove unnecessary `as Item[]` cast\n   - Rename `quoteCount` → `quoteIndex` (matches `quote()` parameter)\n   - Extract `cap = 2 * insuranceSum` to remove duplication\n\nLet me now do the refactoring. I should:\n1. Calculate initial mass\n2. Make changes\n3. Verify tests pass\n4. Calculate final mass\n5. Document\n\nLet me be more careful with the mass calculation. Looking at scenario.ts:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Scenario, Result, Policy, Item } from \"./types.js\";  // imports\nimport { quote } from \"./quote.js\";\nimport { claim } from \"./claim.js\";\n\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: Result[] } {\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];                      // 1 binding, 1 invocation (Array), 1 assignment\n  const results: Result[] = [];                       // 1 binding, 1 invocation (Array), 1 assignment\n  let quoteCount = 0;                                 // 1 binding, 1 constant, 1 assignment\n\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {                // 1 loop, 2 bindings (step, scenario.steps)\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {                        // 1 conditional, 1 binding (step.op), 1 constant (\"quote\")\n      const { premium, insuranceSum } = quote(        // 2 bindings, 1 invocation\n        step.items,                                   // 1 binding\n        scenario.customer,                            // 1 binding\n        quoteCount                                    // 1 binding\n      );\n      const policy: Policy = {                        // 1 binding